(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1c.1c63feaed8.js"]={"14":[{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q01_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Bohr and Haldane effects in oxygen and carbon dioxide transport","batch":1,"stem":"Most descriptions of the Bohr effect focus on O₂ release in the tissues. Guyton points out that the same mechanism also acts in the pulmonary capillaries as CO₂ diffuses from the blood into the alveoli. What is the effect there?","choices":{"A":"The curve shifts right, unloading O₂ into the alveoli","B":"The curve is unchanged, as alveolar PO₂ is already high","C":"The curve shifts left, so more O₂ binds at alveolar PO₂","D":"The curve flattens, lowering arterial O₂ saturation","E":"The curve shifts right, freeing sites for CO₂ to bind"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton explains that in the tissues rising PCO₂ and H⁺ shift the O₂-hemoglobin curve to the right and downward, forcing O₂ off hemoglobin, and that exactly the opposite occurs in the lungs: CO₂ diffusing from the blood into the alveoli reduces blood PCO₂ and H⁺, shifting the curve to the left and upward, so the quantity of O₂ that binds at any given alveolar PO₂ is considerably increased, allowing greater O₂ transport to the tissues. A rightward shift in the lungs would oppose loading, and hemoglobin does not unload O₂ into alveoli. The curve does change, because the fall in PCO₂ and H⁺ happens as blood crosses the capillary. Flattening with lower saturation is not the effect described. Freeing sites for CO₂ binding describes the reciprocal Haldane effect in tissues, not the Bohr effect in the lungs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The Bohr effect works in both directions — in tissues rising PCO2 and H+ shift the curve right and force O2 off hemoglobin; in the lungs CO2 leaves the blood, PCO2 and H+ fall, and the curve shifts left so more O2 binds at any alveolar PO2. COMLEX: FA frames the curve as matching local need — right shift where O2 must be unloaded, left shift where it must be loaded.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — the O2 and CO2 gradients between alveoli, capillary blood and tissues","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The curve does change, because"}},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q02_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Bohr and Haldane effects in oxygen and carbon dioxide transport","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology instructor contrasts the Bohr effect, in which CO₂ and H⁺ displace O₂ from hemoglobin, with the Haldane effect, in which O₂ binding displaces CO₂ from the blood. According to Guyton, how do the two compare in quantitative importance?","choices":{"A":"Haldane matters far more for CO₂ transport than Bohr does for O₂","B":"Bohr matters far more for O₂ transport than Haldane does for CO₂","C":"Both are equal, being one reaction seen from two sides","D":"Both are negligible beside the steep dissociation curve","E":"Haldane acts solely in tissues; Bohr solely in lungs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton notes that binding of O₂ with hemoglobin tends to displace CO₂ from the blood, and that this Haldane effect is quantitatively far more important in promoting CO₂ transport than the Bohr effect is in promoting O₂ transport; the Haldane effect roughly doubles the CO₂ released in the lungs and picked up in the tissues. Ranking the Bohr effect higher reverses Guyton's statement. Although the two effects are reciprocal, they are not equal in size. The Haldane effect is far from negligible, since it accounts for about half of normal CO₂ exchange. Both effects act at both sites: each operates in one direction in the tissues and in the opposite direction in the lungs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bohr effect = CO2/H+ lowering hemoglobin's O2 affinity; Haldane effect = O2 binding displacing CO2. Guyton: the Haldane effect is quantitatively far more important for CO2 transport than the Bohr effect is for O2 transport — it roughly doubles CO2 release in the lungs and uptake in the tissues. COMLEX: FA states the Haldane effect simply: Hb oxygenation in the lungs promotes H+ dissociation and CO2 release from red cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 2 — CO2 transport as bicarbonate, carbaminohemoglobin and dissolved CO2","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Ranking the Bohr effect higher","C":"Although the two effects are r","D":"The Haldane effect is far from","E":"Both effects act at both sites"}},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q03_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Bohr and Haldane effects in oxygen and carbon dioxide transport","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman's arterial blood is analyzed under different conditions. At a pH of 7.4, her hemoglobin is 97% saturated at a PO₂ of 95 mm Hg. When the pH is experimentally decreased to 7.2 (more acidic), what happens to the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve?","choices":{"A":"The curve shifts leftward, requiring lower PO₂ to achieve 97% saturation","B":"The curve remains unchanged because pH doesn't affect hemoglobin binding","C":"The curve becomes steeper without shifting left or right","D":"The curve shifts upward, increasing maximum saturation to 100%","E":"The curve shifts rightward, so a higher PO₂ is needed for 97% saturation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When blood pH decreases from the normal 7.4 to 7.2 (increased H⁺ concentration, more acidic), the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve shifts, on average, about 15% to the right. This rightward shift means that at any given PO₂, hemoglobin saturation will be lower, or conversely, a higher PO₂ is required to achieve the same saturation. This rightward shift with acidosis is part of the Bohr effect and is physiologically beneficial in tissues where metabolism produces acids—the lower pH helps release oxygen where it's needed. Conversely, an increase in pH from 7.4 to 7.6 (more alkaline) shifts the curve about 15% to the left, increasing hemoglobin's oxygen affinity. A leftward shift shows the opposite shift. An unchanged curve is incorrect because pH profoundly affects hemoglobin. A steeper curve and an upward shift to 100% describe changes that don't occur.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A fall in pH from 7.4 to 7.2 shifts the oxyhemoglobin curve, on average, about 15% to the right; a rise to 7.6 shifts it a similar amount to the left. FA: right shift = ↓ affinity, ↑ P50, more unloading. COMLEX: In acidemia a given PaO2 corresponds to a lower saturation, so pulse oximetry and ABG values are read together.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 1 — PO2, PCO2 and pH as the basic measures of pulmonary performance","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q04_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Factors shifting the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old man ascends to high altitude (10,000 feet) and remains there for several weeks. His body adapts by increasing 2,3-biphosphoglycerate (BPG) levels in red blood cells. How does increased BPG affect oxygen transport?","choices":{"A":"Increased BPG shifts the curve rightward, enhancing tissue oxygen delivery","B":"Increased BPG shifts the curve leftward, impairing oxygen delivery to tissues","C":"Increased BPG increases hemoglobin concentration, improving oxygen-carrying capacity","D":"Increased BPG has no effect on oxygen transport or the dissociation curve","E":"Increased BPG decreases hemoglobin's maximum oxygen-binding capacity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Normal BPG (2,3-biphosphoglycerate) in blood always keeps the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve shifted slightly to the right. In hypoxic conditions that last longer than a few hours (such as prolonged high-altitude exposure), the quantity of BPG in blood increases considerably, shifting the curve even farther to the right. This rightward shift causes oxygen to be released to tissues at as much as 10 mm Hg higher tissue oxygen pressure than would occur without the increased BPG. This adaptation is particularly important at high altitude where arterial PO₂ is reduced—the rightward shift helps maintain adequate tissue oxygen delivery despite lower arterial oxygen content. The BPG mechanism represents an important adaptation to chronic hypoxia caused by poor tissue blood flow or reduced atmospheric oxygen. A leftward shift shows the wrong direction. Increased hemoglobin concentration confuses BPG with erythropoietin effects. No effect and decreased maximum binding capacity are incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normal BPG keeps the curve slightly right-shifted; in hypoxia lasting longer than a few hours BPG rises considerably and shifts it farther right, releasing O2 at as much as 10 mm Hg higher tissue PO2. FA: at altitude, ↑ 2,3-BPG (from increased glycolysis) is listed with ↑ erythropoietin and ↑ ventilation. COMLEX: FA lists chronic hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction at altitude as a route to pulmonary hypertension and RVH.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 9 — the phenomenon of acclimatization to altitude","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q05_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Factors shifting the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve","batch":1,"stem":"A 3-month-old infant is given a well-water formula high in nitrates and becomes cyanotic. Blood drawn from her looks chocolate-brown, and co-oximetry shows that 25% of her hemoglobin iron is in the ferric (Fe³⁺) state. How does this disorder affect her oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve, according to First Aid?","choices":{"A":"Right shift, raising P50 and easing tissue unloading","B":"No shift, as just the O₂ content is reduced","C":"Right shift from the rise in red cell 2,3-BPG","D":"Left shift, lowering P50 and impeding unloading","E":"Downward shift with an unchanged P50"},"correct":"D","explanation":"First Aid 2025 lists methemoglobinemia (increased MetHb) with carbon monoxide among the pathologic causes of a left shift of the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve: hemoglobin's O₂ affinity rises, P50 falls, and O₂ unloading in the tissues decreases. First Aid's O₂ table and methemoglobin entry (p688) add that the ferric iron itself binds O₂ poorly, so SaO₂ and total O₂ content fall while PaO₂ and hemoglobin concentration stay normal; nitrites from dietary intake or polluted water oxidize Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺, and the blood is chocolate-colored. A right shift with a higher P50 occurs with exercise, acidosis, fever, and hypoxia-induced 2,3-BPG, not with methemoglobin. The reduced O₂ content is real, but the curve is not left unchanged. A downward shift with unchanged P50 is not how First Aid describes this disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FA lists ↑ methemoglobin with CO as pathologic causes of a left shift — affinity rises, P50 falls, unloading falls; MetHb's ferric iron (Fe3+) binds O2 poorly, lowering SaO2 and O2 content while PaO2 stays normal. COMLEX: Nitrites (including from well water), dapsone and benzocaine cause it; cyanosis does not improve with O2, blood is chocolate-colored, and methylene blue with vitamin C treats it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 11 — causes of hypoxia, including anemia and abnormal hemoglobin","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"A right shift with a higher P5","B":"The reduced O₂ content is real"}},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q06_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Factors shifting the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology researcher studies factors affecting the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve. Which of the following combinations would shift the curve to the LEFT, thereby increasing hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen and potentially impairing oxygen release to tissues?","choices":{"A":"Increased temperature, increased CO₂, and increased H⁺ concentration","B":"Decreased temperature, increased CO₂, and increased 2,3-BPG levels","C":"Normal temperature, increased CO₂, and decreased 2,3-BPG levels","D":"Increased temperature, decreased CO₂, and increased 2,3-BPG levels","E":"Decreased temperature, decreased CO₂, and decreased H⁺ (increased pH)"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve shifts to the LEFT (increasing hemoglobin's oxygen affinity) when: (1) temperature decreases, (2) CO₂ concentration decreases, (3) H⁺ concentration decreases (pH increases/becomes more alkaline), and (4) 2,3-BPG levels decrease. A leftward shift means hemoglobin binds oxygen more tightly, which is beneficial in the lungs for oxygen loading but can impair oxygen release to tissues. This combination might occur in hypothermic patients or during hyperventilation (which lowers CO₂ and raises pH). In contrast, a rightward shift occurs with increased temperature, increased CO₂, increased H⁺ (decreased pH), and increased 2,3-BPG—all of which decrease hemoglobin's oxygen affinity and enhance tissue oxygen delivery. All-increased factors, normal temperature with increased CO₂ and low BPG, and increased temperature with low CO₂ and high BPG contain factors that would shift the curve rightward. Decreased temperature with increased CO₂ and BPG is mixed, but the increased CO₂ and BPG would dominate, causing a rightward shift.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Right-shift factors are increased CO2, H+, temperature and BPG; the reverse of each — cooling, low CO2, alkalosis, low BPG — shifts the curve left, raising affinity and impairing unloading. FA adds CO, methemoglobin and fetal hemoglobin as left-shift causes ('left is lower' P50). COMLEX: Hypothermia and iatrogenic alkalosis can leave tissues hypoxic despite a reassuring saturation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — oxygen transport principles and factors that influence hemoglobin saturation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"All-increased factors, normal "}},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q07_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Exercise adaptations enhancing oxygen transport","batch":1,"stem":"A 26-year-old competitive cyclist undergoes exercise testing. At rest, his pulmonary diffusing capacity for oxygen is 21 mL/min per mm Hg. During maximal exercise, his diffusing capacity increases substantially. What is the primary mechanism responsible for this increase in diffusing capacity during exercise?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar surface area decreases, concentrating gas exchange in fewer alveoli","B":"Respiratory membrane thickness increases to strengthen the barrier","C":"Minute ventilation decreases to allow more time for diffusion","D":"Hemoglobin concentration in blood doubles during exercise","E":"More pulmonary capillaries are recruited, increasing capillary surface area"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The diffusing capacity for oxygen increases almost 3-fold during exercise, from about 21 mL/min per mm Hg at rest to about 65 mL/min per mm Hg during maximal exercise. This increase results mainly from two mechanisms: (1) increased surface area of capillaries participating in diffusion—as cardiac output increases, previously closed or poorly perfused alveolar capillaries become recruited and fully perfused, dramatically increasing the total surface area available for gas exchange; (2) more ideal ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) ratios in the upper parts of the lungs as both ventilation and perfusion increase proportionally. This enhanced diffusing capacity, combined with the safety factor that blood normally becomes fully oxygenated within the first third of capillary transit time, ensures arterial blood remains well-oxygenated even when capillary transit time is reduced to less than half normal during intense exercise. Decreased surface area, thicker membrane, and decreased minute ventilation describe changes opposite to what occurs. Doubled hemoglobin is incorrect because hemoglobin concentration doesn't acutely double during exercise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: O2 diffusing capacity rises almost 3-fold in exercise, mainly from more participating capillaries (larger surface area) and a more ideal V/Q in the upper lungs. FA: with exercise apical capillaries dilate and V/Q approaches 1. COMLEX: Patients who have lost capillary bed (emphysema, ILD, pulmonary vascular disease) cannot recruit, so their limitation shows during exertion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 10 — O2 and CO2 diffusing capacities at rest and during exercise","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Decreased surface area, thicke","B":"Decreased surface area, thicke"}},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q08_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Exercise adaptations enhancing oxygen transport","batch":1,"stem":"A 30-year-old marathon runner's blood is analyzed during peak exercise. Her tissue PO₂ in active leg muscles has dropped from the resting value of 40 mm Hg to 15 mm Hg. What is her utilization coefficient (the percentage of oxygen extracted from blood) during this intense exercise?","choices":{"A":"The utilization coefficient increases slightly to 35% during intense exercise","B":"The utilization coefficient remains at 25% regardless of exercise intensity","C":"The utilization coefficient decreases to 10% because of reduced capillary transit time","D":"The utilization coefficient increases to approximately 75-85% during intense exercise","E":"The utilization coefficient reaches 50% at maximum during intense exercise"},"correct":"D","explanation":"During intense exercise when tissue PO₂ falls to 15 mm Hg, the utilization coefficient can increase to 75-85%, meaning that 75-85% of the oxygen bound to hemoglobin in arterial blood is extracted and delivered to tissues. This is a dramatic increase from the normal resting utilization coefficient of 25%. At a tissue PO₂ of 15 mm Hg, hemoglobin saturation drops to approximately 22%, leaving only about 4.4 mL of oxygen bound per 100 mL blood (compared to 19.4 mL in arterial blood). This means 19.4 - 4.4 = 15 mL of oxygen is delivered per 100 mL blood flow, representing a 3-fold increase over the normal 5 mL delivered at rest. In highly metabolically active tissues with very slow blood flow, utilization coefficients approaching 100% have been recorded. This demonstrates the remarkable extraction capacity of tissues during periods of high metabolic demand. The other options all underestimate the extraction capability during intense exercise. 35% is far below the 75 to 85% utilization seen when tissue PO2 falls to 15 mm Hg. Utilization increases during exercise and does not decrease to 10%. 50% understates utilization, which can reach 75 to 85% at this tissue PO2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Utilization coefficient = fraction of arterial O2 given up in the tissues — about 25% at rest, 75–85% for the whole body in strenuous exercise, and near 100% in tissues with very slow flow or very high metabolism. COMLEX: FA — exercise widens the arteriovenous O2 difference through a right-shifted curve, so a low mixed venous saturation is expected at peak effort.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 4 — how exercise raises O2 demand and extraction","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"35% is far below the 75 to 85% utilization seen when tissue ","C":"Utilization increases during exercise and does not decrease ","E":"50% understates utilization, which can reach 75 to 85% at th"}},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q09_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Exercise adaptations enhancing oxygen transport","batch":1,"stem":"Arterial blood normally has a pH of about 7.41, and as it picks up CO₂ in resting tissues its pH falls only to about 7.37. During heavy exercise, or when blood flow through a tissue is sluggish, how large can the fall in pH of the tissue blood become, according to Guyton?","choices":{"A":"About 0.04 unit, the same as at rest","B":"About 0.10 unit, 2.5 times normal","C":"About 0.02 unit, half the resting value","D":"About 0.50 unit, about 12 times normal","E":"About 1.50 units, about 40 times normal"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guyton notes that buffers keep the normal arterial-to-venous pH change to about 0.04 unit (7.41 to 7.37), but during heavy exercise, other conditions of high metabolic activity, or sluggish tissue blood flow, the decrease in pH in the tissue blood and tissues can be as much as 0.50, about 12 times normal, causing significant tissue acidosis; the extra H⁺ also contributes to the rightward shift of the dissociation curve that unloads O₂ in active muscle. 0.04 unit is the resting change, and 0.02 unit is smaller still. 0.10 unit understates the exercise value. A fall of 1.50 units would drive blood pH to about 5.9, far below what Guyton describes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Buffers keep the arterial-to-venous pH change to about 0.04 unit (7.41 to 7.37); in heavy exercise or sluggish tissue flow the fall can reach 0.50, about 12 times normal, causing significant tissue acidosis. COMLEX: Local tissue acidosis is also a right-shift stimulus, so poorly perfused tissue unloads O2 more readily from what blood does reach it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — how blood flow changes alter tissue O2 and CO2 levels","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q10_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — CO₂ transport mechanisms including carbonic anhydrase, bicarbonate, and carbaminohemoglobin","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old man's red blood cells are studied in vitro. When CO₂ is added to the blood sample, the enzyme carbonic anhydrase rapidly catalyzes a reaction. Approximately how much does carbonic anhydrase accelerate the reaction between CO₂ and water compared to the uncatalyzed reaction?","choices":{"A":"10-fold acceleration of the CO₂-water reaction","B":"5000-fold acceleration of the CO₂-water reaction","C":"100-fold acceleration of the CO₂-water reaction","D":"500-fold acceleration of the CO₂-water reaction","E":"50-fold acceleration of the CO₂-water reaction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Carbonic anhydrase, an enzyme found inside red blood cells, catalyzes the reaction between CO₂ and water and accelerates the reaction rate by approximately 5000-fold. This dramatic acceleration is essential for efficient CO₂ transport. Without carbonic anhydrase, the reaction CO₂ + H₂O → H₂CO₃ would require many seconds or minutes to reach equilibrium. With carbonic anhydrase, the reaction occurs so rapidly that it reaches almost complete equilibrium within a small fraction of a second, even before blood leaves the tissue capillaries. This rapid reaction allows tremendous amounts of CO₂ to combine with red blood cell water and be transported as bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻), which accounts for about 70% of CO₂ transport. When carbonic anhydrase is experimentally blocked with inhibitors like acetazolamide, CO₂ transport becomes so impaired that tissue PCO₂ can rise from the normal 45 mm Hg to 80 mm Hg or higher. The other options all underestimate the catalytic power of this essential enzyme. 10-fold understates carbonic anhydrase, which accelerates the reaction about 5000-fold. 100-fold understates the acceleration of about 5000-fold. 500-fold understates the acceleration of about 5000-fold. 50-fold understates the acceleration of about 5000-fold.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Red-cell carbonic anhydrase accelerates CO2 + H2O → H2CO3 about 5000-fold, so a reaction that takes seconds to minutes in plasma occurs within a fraction of a second; H2CO3 then dissociates to H+ and HCO3−. COMLEX: FA — acetazolamide inhibits this enzyme; it augments renal HCO3− excretion at altitude and is used in idiopathic intracranial hypertension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 2 — carbonic anhydrase inhibitors and their role in acute mountain sickness","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"10-fold understates carbonic anhydrase, which accelerates th","C":"100-fold understates the acceleration of about 5000-fold.","D":"500-fold understates the acceleration of about 5000-fold.","E":"50-fold understates the acceleration of about 5000-fold."}},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q11_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — CO₂ transport mechanisms including carbonic anhydrase, bicarbonate, and carbaminohemoglobin","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology student learns about the chloride shift that occurs in red blood cells during CO₂ transport. After carbonic anhydrase catalyzes the formation of carbonic acid, which then dissociates into H⁺ and HCO₃⁻, what happens to these ions?","choices":{"A":"Both H⁺ and HCO₃⁻ remain in red blood cells throughout circulation","B":"Both H⁺ and HCO₃⁻ diffuse into plasma, leaving red blood cells","C":"H⁺ binds to hemoglobin while HCO₃⁻ diffuses to plasma; Cl⁻ enters RBCs in exchange","D":"HCO₃⁻ binds to hemoglobin while H⁺ diffuses to plasma; Na⁺ enters RBCs in exchange","E":"H⁺ and HCO₃⁻ immediately recombine to form CO₂ again"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After carbonic acid (H₂CO₃) dissociates into H⁺ and HCO₃⁻ ions in red blood cells, these ions follow different paths: (1) Most H⁺ ions combine with hemoglobin because hemoglobin protein is a powerful acid-base buffer, preventing large pH changes; (2) Many HCO₃⁻ ions diffuse from red blood cells into plasma, while chloride ions (Cl⁻) diffuse into red blood cells to take their place and maintain electrical neutrality. This exchange is facilitated by a special bicarbonate-chloride carrier protein in the red blood cell membrane that shuttles these two ions in opposite directions at rapid velocities. This phenomenon is called the \"chloride shift\" or \"Hamburger shift.\" As a result, the chloride content of venous red blood cells is greater than that of arterial red blood cells. In the lungs, this process reverses—HCO₃⁻ moves back into RBCs, combines with H⁺ to form H₂CO₃, which dissociates into CO₂ and water, and the CO₂ diffuses into alveoli. The other options describe incorrect mechanisms. Bicarbonate diffuses out of the red cell into plasma rather than remaining inside. H+ does not diffuse out; it binds to hemoglobin, while only bicarbonate leaves. It is H+ that binds to hemoglobin and bicarbonate that leaves, with chloride entering, not sodium. The ions do not recombine; H+ is buffered by hemoglobin and bicarbonate moves to plasma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: After carbonic anhydrase acts, most H+ binds hemoglobin (a powerful buffer), HCO3− leaves the red cell for plasma, and Cl− enters on a bicarbonate-chloride carrier — so venous red cells hold more chloride (the chloride shift). FA: about 90% of CO2 travels as HCO3− in plasma (Guyton: about 70%). COMLEX: The red cell's buffering keeps the venous pH change small despite the CO2 load.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 2 — CO2 transport mechanisms and chemical combinations in blood","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Bicarbonate diffuses out of the red cell into plasma rather ","B":"H+ does not diffuse out; it binds to hemoglobin, while only ","D":"It is H+ that binds to hemoglobin and bicarbonate that leave","E":"The ions do not recombine; H+ is buffered by hemoglobin and "}},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q12_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — CO₂ transport mechanisms including carbonic anhydrase, bicarbonate, and carbaminohemoglobin","batch":1,"stem":"Carbon dioxide forms carbamino compounds by reacting directly with amine radicals of proteins. In tissue capillaries it can react this way with both hemoglobin and the plasma proteins. Why does Guyton consider the plasma-protein reaction much less significant for CO₂ transport?","choices":{"A":"Plasma proteins lack the amine radicals CO₂ binds","B":"The plasma reaction needs carbonic anhydrase","C":"Plasma proteins bind CO₂ too tightly to release it","D":"Plasma carbamino forms in the lungs, not the tissues","E":"Plasma proteins total about a quarter of hemoglobin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Guyton states that a small amount of CO₂ reacts with plasma proteins in the tissue capillaries in the same way it reacts with hemoglobin, but this is much less significant because the quantity of these proteins in the blood is only one-fourth as great as the quantity of hemoglobin; carbamino transport by both together is about 30% of the total in theory, and probably no more than 20% in practice because the reaction is slower than the carbonic anhydrase reaction. Plasma proteins do have amine radicals, which is why the reaction occurs at all. Carbamino formation is a direct reaction that does not require carbonic anhydrase; that enzyme catalyzes CO₂ hydration inside red cells and is absent from plasma. The carbamino bond is loose and easily reversed, not too tight. Carbamino compounds form in the tissues, where PCO₂ is high, and release CO₂ in the lungs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CO2 forms carbamino compounds with amine radicals on both hemoglobin and plasma proteins, but plasma proteins total only about one-fourth as much as hemoglobin, so their share is small. FA: carbamino compounds form at the N-terminus of globin in deoxygenated hemoglobin (about 5% of CO2). COMLEX: Deoxygenated hemoglobin binds CO2 more readily — the Haldane effect at the venous end.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 2 — carbaminohemoglobin among the CO2 transport routes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Plasma proteins do have amine ","B":"Carbamino formation is a direc"}},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q13_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Effects of carbon monoxide on hemoglobin function and oxygen-carrying capacity","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old man is exposed to carbon monoxide (CO) in his garage. CO competes with oxygen for binding sites on hemoglobin. Approximately how much more tightly does CO bind to hemoglobin compared to oxygen?","choices":{"A":"CO binds approximately 250 times more tightly than oxygen to hemoglobin","B":"CO binds approximately 50 times more tightly than oxygen to hemoglobin","C":"CO binds approximately 10 times more tightly than oxygen to hemoglobin","D":"CO binds with the same affinity as oxygen to hemoglobin","E":"CO binds approximately 1000 times more tightly than oxygen to hemoglobin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Carbon monoxide binds to hemoglobin at the same sites as oxygen but with approximately 250 times as much tenacity (Guyton Ch 41; First Aid 2025 gives > 200 times the affinity of O₂). This is demonstrated by the CO-hemoglobin dissociation curve, which is almost identical to the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve except that the CO partial pressures are at a level 1/250 of those for oxygen. Therefore, a CO partial pressure of only 0.4 mm Hg in alveoli (1/250 of the normal alveolar oxygen pressure of 100 mm Hg) allows CO to compete equally with oxygen for hemoglobin binding, causing 50% of hemoglobin to become bound with CO instead of oxygen. A CO pressure of only 0.6 mm Hg (less than one part per 1000 in air) can be lethal. This extraordinarily high affinity makes CO poisoning particularly dangerous—even small amounts of CO can displace large amounts of oxygen from hemoglobin, severely impairing oxygen delivery to tissues. The other options underestimate or overestimate the binding differential. 50 times understates the affinity; CO binds about 250 times as tightly as oxygen. 10 times understates it; CO binds about 250 times more tightly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CO binds hemoglobin at the O2 sites with about 250 times O2's tenacity (Guyton; FA: more than 200 times), so an alveolar PCO of only 0.4 mm Hg competes equally with normal alveolar O2 and about 0.6 mm Hg can be lethal. COMLEX: FA — CO comes from furnaces, space heaters, motor exhaust and fires; multiple victims in one household suggests it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 11 — the CO method for diffusing capacity, which relies on this avid binding","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"50 times understates the affinity; CO binds about 250 times ","C":"10 times understates it; CO binds about 250 times more tight"}},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q14_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Effects of carbon monoxide on hemoglobin function and oxygen-carrying capacity","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with suspected carbon monoxide poisoning from a faulty heater. Despite having 50% of her hemoglobin bound to CO (carboxyhemoglobin), her arterial PO₂ measurement is normal at 95 mm Hg. Why is the arterial PO₂ normal despite severe CO poisoning?","choices":{"A":"CO increases oxygen diffusion from alveoli, raising arterial PO₂ to compensate","B":"The body compensates by producing more hemoglobin immediately","C":"PO₂ reflects dissolved O₂ only; CO lowers O₂ content but not PO₂","D":"CO binding to hemoglobin doesn't affect oxygen content, only PO₂","E":"CO is rapidly converted to CO₂ in the lungs, restoring normal oxygenation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Even though oxygen content of blood is greatly reduced in CO poisoning (because hemoglobin binding sites are occupied by CO), the arterial PO₂ can remain normal. This occurs because PO₂ measures the partial pressure of dissolved oxygen in plasma, not the oxygen bound to hemoglobin. The alveolar-to-blood oxygen gradient remains normal, so dissolved oxygen equilibrates normally, maintaining normal arterial PO₂. This situation makes CO exposure especially dangerous because: (1) the blood appears bright red (not cyanotic) since carboxyhemoglobin is bright red like oxyhemoglobin; (2) there are no obvious visual signs of hypoxemia like bluish discoloration of lips or fingertips; (3) the PO₂ is not reduced, so the feedback mechanism that normally stimulates increased respiration in response to low oxygen (detected by peripheral chemoreceptors responding to PO₂) is absent. The person may become disoriented and unconscious before becoming aware of danger because the brain is one of the first organs affected by lack of oxygen delivery. The other options describe mechanisms that don't occur. CO does not speed O2 diffusion; Guyton says it displaces O2 from hemoglobin, lowering O2 content while blood PO2 may stay normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PaO2 measures dissolved O2, so in CO poisoning it stays normal while O2 content and %O2Hb fall; the blood is bright red and there is no cyanosis. FA: normal PaO2, ↑ carboxyhemoglobin on co-oximetry — standard pulse oximetry cannot distinguish it. COMLEX: Order co-oximetry for headache, confusion or vomiting after heater or exhaust exposure even when the SpO2 looks normal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — hemoglobin's role in O2 transport and O2-carrying capacity","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"CO does not speed O2 diffusion; Guyton says it displaces O2 "}},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q15_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Effects of carbon monoxide on hemoglobin function and oxygen-carrying capacity","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man severely poisoned with carbon monoxide is brought to the hospital. What is the most effective treatment to rapidly remove CO from his blood and restore oxygen-carrying capacity?","choices":{"A":"Intravenous administration of methylene blue to convert carboxyhemoglobin","B":"Pure oxygen plus 5% CO₂ to stimulate ventilation and displace CO","C":"Blood transfusion to replace CO-bound red blood cells with fresh cells","D":"Hyperventilation with room air to increase CO₂ removal","E":"Administration of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors to enhance gas exchange"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A patient severely poisoned with CO can be effectively treated by administering pure oxygen (100% O₂) because oxygen at high alveolar pressure can displace CO from its combination with hemoglobin much more rapidly than breathing room air. Additionally, simultaneous administration of 5% CO₂ provides further benefit because CO₂ strongly stimulates the respiratory center, increasing alveolar ventilation and reducing alveolar CO concentration. With intensive oxygen and CO₂ therapy, CO can be removed from blood as much as 10 times more rapidly than without therapy. The high oxygen partial pressure (potentially 600-700 mm Hg when breathing 100% O₂) overwhelms CO's much higher binding affinity (about 250-fold in Guyton, > 200-fold in First Aid 2025) through mass action—the sheer abundance of oxygen molecules competing for hemoglobin binding sites. In clinical practice the mainstay is 100% O₂, with hyperbaric O₂ (pressures >1 atmosphere) for severe cases (First Aid); the added CO₂ is Guyton's physiological refinement. Methylene blue treats methemoglobinemia, not carboxyhemoglobin; transfusion and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors do not remove CO; and hyperventilating room air lacks the high alveolar PO₂ that displaces CO.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's treatment for severe CO poisoning — pure O2, because high alveolar PO2 displaces CO from hemoglobin rapidly, plus 5% CO2, which strongly stimulates the respiratory center, raising alveolar ventilation and lowering alveolar CO. COMLEX: FA — give 100% O2 at once and hyperbaric O2 if severe; consider concurrent cyanide in fire victims.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — hyperbaric oxygen therapy, its rationale and mechanism","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Methylene blue treats methemog"}},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q17_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Bohr and Haldane effects in oxygen and carbon dioxide transport","batch":1,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman hyperventilates during a panic attack, causing her arterial PCO₂ to drop from 40 mm Hg to 25 mm Hg and her pH to rise from 7.4 to 7.6. How does this respiratory alkalosis affect oxygen delivery to her tissues through the Bohr effect?","choices":{"A":"Decreased PCO₂ and increased pH shift the curve leftward, impairing tissue oxygen release","B":"Decreased PCO₂ and increased pH shift the curve rightward, enhancing oxygen delivery","C":"Decreased PCO₂ and increased pH have no effect on hemoglobin-oxygen binding","D":"Decreased PCO₂ increases hemoglobin concentration, improving oxygen capacity","E":"Decreased PCO₂ denatures hemoglobin, preventing all oxygen transport"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When PCO₂ decreases and pH increases (becomes more alkaline) during hyperventilation, the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve shifts to the LEFT. This leftward shift increases hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen, which impairs oxygen release to tissues. The Bohr effect normally works in favor of tissue oxygenation—when blood passes through metabolically active tissues where PCO₂ is high and pH is low (acidic), the curve shifts right to enhance oxygen release. In this hyperventilation scenario, the opposite occurs: the leftward shift means hemoglobin holds oxygen more tightly, making it harder to release at normal tissue PO₂ levels. The paresthesias and carpopedal spasm of hyperventilation come mainly from alkalosis lowering ionized calcium (more Ca²⁺ bound to plasma protein), not from the left shift itself. The phenomenon demonstrates why respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation can paradoxically worsen tissue oxygenation. The other options are incorrect. A rise in pH from 7.4 to 7.6 shifts the curve about 15% to the left, so hemoglobin-oxygen binding is affected.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyperventilation lowers PCO2 and raises pH, shifting the curve left (Guyton: pH 7.4 → 7.6 shifts it about as much as 7.4 → 7.2 shifts it right), so hemoglobin holds O2 more tightly and tissue unloading falls. COMLEX: FA — at altitude, hypoxic hyperventilation produces the same respiratory alkalosis, and renal HCO3− excretion (augmentable with acetazolamide) compensates.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — respiratory and renal compensation during altitude acclimatization","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"A rise in pH from 7.4 to 7.6 shifts the curve about 15% to t"}},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q18_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Bohr and Haldane effects in oxygen and carbon dioxide transport","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology researcher studies the CO₂ dissociation curve under different oxygen saturation conditions. At a PCO₂ of 45 mm Hg (normal venous), blood with PO₂ of 40 mm Hg (deoxygenated) carries 52 volume percent CO₂. When the same blood is oxygenated to PO₂ of 100 mm Hg while PCO₂ remains at 45 mm Hg, what happens to the CO₂ content?","choices":{"A":"CO₂ content decreases due to the Haldane effect as oxygen binding displaces CO₂","B":"CO₂ content increases because oxygen binding enhances CO₂ transport","C":"CO₂ content remains unchanged at 52 volume percent","D":"CO₂ content increases to 55 volume percent from enhanced carbonic anhydrase activity","E":"CO₂ content decreases to 40 volume percent from competitive inhibition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Haldane effect describes how oxygen binding to hemoglobin decreases the blood's CO₂-carrying capacity at any given PCO₂. When oxygen binds to hemoglobin, it makes hemoglobin a stronger acid, which has two consequences: (1) the more acidic hemoglobin has less tendency to form carbaminohemoglobin, releasing CO₂ that was bound to hemoglobin; (2) the increased acidity causes hemoglobin to release H⁺ ions, which combine with HCO₃⁻ to form carbonic acid (H₂CO₃), which dissociates into CO₂ and water. Therefore, when blood at PCO₂ 45 mm Hg is oxygenated, the CO₂ dissociation curve shifts downward, and the CO₂ content decreases even though PCO₂ hasn't changed. This is why the CO₂ dissociation curve shows different curves for oxygenated versus deoxygenated blood. In the lungs, this effect facilitates CO₂ release; in tissues, deoxygenation facilitates CO₂ uptake. An increase from enhanced transport and an increase to 55 volume percent incorrectly show increases. No change at 52 volume percent ignores the Haldane effect. A decrease to 40 volume percent overestimates the magnitude.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Haldane effect — at the same PCO2, oxygenated blood holds less CO2 than deoxygenated blood because acidified oxyhemoglobin forms less carbaminohemoglobin and releases H+ that converts HCO3− to CO2. COMLEX: FA's one-liner for the lungs: Hb oxygenation promotes H+ dissociation and CO2 release from red cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 2 — CO2 transport mechanisms and the CO2 dissociation curve","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"No change at 52 volume percent","E":"A decrease to 40 volume percen"}},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q19_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Bohr and Haldane effects in oxygen and carbon dioxide transport","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with chronic lung disease has blood pH of 7.25 (acidemia) due to CO₂ retention. His oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve is analyzed. How would the PO₂ at which his hemoglobin is 50% saturated (P₅₀) change?","choices":{"A":"P₅₀ would decrease, reflecting a leftward shift","B":"P₅₀ would stay at its normal value despite the pH change","C":"P₅₀ would decrease from increased oxygen affinity","D":"P₅₀ would increase from CO₂ competing at the O₂-binding site","E":"P₅₀ would increase, reflecting a rightward (Bohr) shift"},"correct":"E","explanation":"P₅₀ is the PO₂ at which hemoglobin is 50% saturated; an increase in P₅₀ reflects decreased O₂ affinity and a decrease reflects increased affinity (Costanzo Ch 5). A fall in pH from 7.4 toward 7.2 shifts the curve, on average, about 15% to the right (Guyton Ch 41), so a higher PO₂ is needed to reach 50% saturation and P₅₀ rises. This Bohr shift helps release O₂ to the tissues despite his lung disease. CO₂ acts through H⁺ and carbamino formation, not by competing for the O₂-binding site. A lower P₅₀ would mean a leftward shift, which alkalosis rather than acidemia produces, and P₅₀ does not stay fixed when pH changes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: P50 is the PO2 at 50% saturation; acidemia from CO2 retention shifts the curve right (about 15% for pH 7.4 → 7.2), so P50 rises. FA: right shift ↔ ↑ P50; 'left is lower'. COMLEX: In chronic CO2 retention the right shift helps tissues unload O2 even as arterial saturation falls.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — the pathophysiology of hypoxia and hypercapnia in chronic emphysema","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q20_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Factors shifting the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve","batch":1,"stem":"A 22-year-old medical student donates blood, and a unit of packed red blood cells is stored at 4°C for 28 days before transfusion. During storage, 2,3-BPG levels in the red blood cells decrease significantly. How does this affect the stored blood's ability to deliver oxygen to tissues when transfused?","choices":{"A":"Decreased 2,3-BPG has no effect on oxygen delivery from transfused blood","B":"Decreased 2,3-BPG shifts the curve leftward, impairing oxygen release to tissues","C":"Decreased 2,3-BPG shifts the curve rightward, enhancing oxygen delivery","D":"Decreased 2,3-BPG increases hemoglobin concentration, improving oxygen capacity","E":"Decreased 2,3-BPG causes immediate hemolysis, preventing oxygen transport"},"correct":"B","explanation":"During blood storage, 2,3-BPG (2,3-biphosphoglycerate) levels decrease progressively because metabolism continues slowly even at 4°C. Decreased 2,3-BPG shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the LEFT, increasing hemoglobin's oxygen affinity. This leftward shift means the transfused hemoglobin holds oxygen more tightly and releases it less readily to tissues. Stored blood with depleted 2,3-BPG may have impaired oxygen-delivering capacity despite normal oxygen content, which can be clinically significant in patients receiving large transfusions. After transfusion the red cells gradually regenerate 2,3-BPG and the curve returns toward its normal position. Normal 2,3-BPG keeps the curve shifted slightly right; its absence removes this rightward shift. No effect ignores this important effect. A rightward shift shows the wrong direction. Increased hemoglobin concentration and immediate hemolysis describe incorrect mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 2,3-BPG binds deoxyhemoglobin and keeps the curve slightly right-shifted; less BPG shifts it left, raising affinity and holding O2 back from tissues. FA lists low 2,3-BPG with CO, methemoglobin and fetal hemoglobin as left-shift causes. COMLEX: A left-shifted curve means a normal saturation can overstate how much O2 actually reaches the tissues.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — hemoglobin's role in carrying O2 from alveoli to tissues","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"A rightward shift shows the wr","D":"Increased hemoglobin concentra"}},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q21_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Factors shifting the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve","batch":1,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman in her third trimester asks how her fetus obtains enough oxygen from her blood across the placenta. Fetal hemoglobin (HbF) binds 2,3-BPG less avidly than adult hemoglobin (HbA). How does this property affect oxygen transfer at the placenta?","choices":{"A":"HbF and HbA have identical curves, so transfer depends only on PO₂ gradients","B":"HbF's curve lies left of maternal HbA's, so O₂ moves from maternal to fetal Hb","C":"Weaker 2,3-BPG binding lowers HbF's O₂ affinity, slowing fetal O₂ uptake","D":"HbF's curve lies right of maternal HbA's, so fetal blood unloads O₂ to the mother","E":"Maternal blood begins producing HbF, raising its own oxygen affinity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"2,3-BPG binds deoxyhemoglobin and lowers its O₂ affinity. Because HbF (α2γ2) binds 2,3-BPG less avidly, it has a higher O₂ affinity than HbA, so its dissociation curve lies to the left of the maternal curve; at the placental PO₂, fetal hemoglobin can capture O₂ released by maternal hemoglobin (First Aid, hemoglobin development). Weaker 2,3-BPG binding raises, not lowers, affinity, and the curves are not identical or reversed. The mother does not begin making HbF in pregnancy; her own adaptations include a mild respiratory alkalosis from increased ventilation (First Aid, physiologic changes in pregnancy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HbF (α2γ2) binds 2,3-BPG with lower affinity than HbA (α2β2), so it has higher O2 affinity and a left-shifted curve, letting fetal blood capture O2 from maternal hemoglobin across the placenta. COMLEX: FA's switch mnemonic — 'Alpha always; gamma goes, becomes beta' — tracks the postnatal shift from fetal to adult hemoglobin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — factors that influence hemoglobin saturation and O2 transport","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q22_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Factors shifting the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology student plots O₂ saturation against PO₂ for adult hemoglobin and for myoglobin from the same subject. The myoglobin curve lies to the left of the hemoglobin curve and is hyperbolic rather than sigmoidal. Which feature of myoglobin best explains both differences?","choices":{"A":"It binds 2,3-BPG more avidly than hemoglobin","B":"It is a tetramer that resists the Bohr effect","C":"It is a monomer with no positive cooperativity","D":"It contains ferric iron that cannot release O₂","E":"It binds CO₂ in place of O₂ at low PO₂"},"correct":"C","explanation":"First Aid 2025 explains that the sigmoidal shape of hemoglobin's curve comes from positive cooperativity, since the tetramer has higher affinity for each successive O₂ bound, whereas myoglobin is a single polypeptide chain with one heme, shows no cooperativity, and has a hyperbolic curve; it also has a higher affinity for O₂ than hemoglobin, placing its curve to the left, and it is essential for full oxygenation of aerobically active muscle. Avid 2,3-BPG binding would lower affinity and shift a curve to the right. Myoglobin is monomeric, not a tetramer. Ferric (Fe³⁺) iron binds O₂ poorly and describes methemoglobin, not myoglobin. Myoglobin does not substitute CO₂ for O₂.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myoglobin is a single polypeptide chain with one heme — no positive cooperativity, so its curve is hyperbolic and left of hemoglobin's; hemoglobin's tetramer, with higher affinity for each successive O2, gives the sigmoidal curve. COMLEX: FA — myoglobin's higher O2 affinity is essential for full oxygenation of aerobically active muscle, which is why it is concentrated in working skeletal and cardiac muscle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 6 — intracellular PO2 and the pressure gradients that drive O2 into cells","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Myoglobin is monomeric, not a ","E":"Myoglobin does not substitute "}},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q23_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Exercise adaptations enhancing oxygen transport","batch":1,"stem":"A 24-year-old sprinter's blood is analyzed immediately after a 400-meter race. Her venous blood from leg muscles shows PO₂ of 18 mm Hg, pH of 7.15, PCO₂ of 55 mm Hg, and temperature of 39.5°C. How do these local conditions in active muscle affect oxygen extraction compared to resting conditions?","choices":{"A":"These conditions shift the curve leftward, reducing oxygen extraction to 15%","B":"These conditions have no net effect because factors cancel out","C":"These conditions moderately increase oxygen extraction to 40%","D":"These conditions cause hemoglobin denaturation, preventing oxygen release","E":"These conditions shift the curve rightward, raising extraction to 75-85%"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The extreme local conditions in intensely exercising muscle create a powerful rightward shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve through multiple mechanisms: (1) very low pH (7.15, acidosis from lactic acid and CO₂), (2) very high PCO₂ (55 mm Hg from enhanced metabolism), (3) elevated temperature (39.5°C, 2.5°C above normal), and (4) increased 2,3-BPG from sustained exercise. All these factors shift the curve rightward (Bohr effect and other mechanisms), greatly facilitating oxygen release from hemoglobin even at relatively high tissue PO₂ levels. Combined with the very low tissue PO₂ (18 mm Hg), this allows oxygen extraction (utilization coefficient) to reach the 75–85% Guyton gives for strenuous exercise (Ch 41), or higher in local tissue. At 18 mm Hg, hemoglobin saturation might be only 20-25%, meaning 75-80% of oxygen has been extracted. This demonstrates the remarkable physiological adaptations that allow muscles to extract maximal oxygen during intense exercise. A leftward shift to 15%, no net effect, and A moderate rise to 40% underestimate this effect. Hemoglobin denaturation describes a mechanism that doesn't occur at physiological temperatures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Active muscle adds CO2, acids and a 2–3°C temperature rise, each shifting the curve right; together with low tissue PO2 this raises the whole-body utilization coefficient from about 25% to 75–85%. COMLEX: FA — the right shift in exercise widens the arteriovenous O2 difference while arterial PO2 stays normal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 4 — exercise effects on O2 demand and diffusion efficiency","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q24_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Exercise adaptations enhancing oxygen transport","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old marathon runner trains at sea level (PO₂ = 150 mm Hg) and then competes at high altitude (10,000 feet, PO₂ = 110 mm Hg). After 3 weeks of altitude acclimatization, which adaptation helps maintain tissue oxygen delivery despite reduced atmospheric oxygen?","choices":{"A":"Increased 2,3-BPG causing rightward curve shift to enhance tissue oxygen release","B":"Decreased hemoglobin concentration to improve blood flow properties","C":"Rightward curve shift from respiratory alkalosis to enhance oxygen unloading","D":"Decreased cardiac output to conserve oxygen for critical tissues","E":"Conversion to fetal hemoglobin with higher oxygen affinity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At high altitude, the body makes multiple adaptations to maintain tissue oxygen delivery despite reduced atmospheric PO₂. One of the most important adaptations during acclimatization (taking days to weeks) is increased red blood cell 2,3-BPG levels, which shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the RIGHT. This rightward shift decreases hemoglobin's oxygen affinity, allowing oxygen to be released to tissues at higher PO₂ levels (by as much as 10 mm Hg higher than normal). While this seems counterintuitive (since arterial PO₂ is already reduced), the benefit occurs at the tissue level—oxygen is more readily released from hemoglobin to meet metabolic demands. Other important adaptations include increased hemoglobin concentration (polycythemia, not decreased), increased capillary density, increased mitochondrial density, and hyperventilation. Alkalosis-driven rightward shift is wrong because the respiratory alkalosis of hyperventilation shifts the curve leftward, not rightward. Decreased hemoglobin, decreased cardiac output, and conversion to fetal hemoglobin describe changes that don't occur or would be detrimental.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypoxia lasting more than a few hours raises red-cell BPG, shifting the curve right so O2 is released at up to 10 mm Hg higher tissue PO2. FA: altitude response = ↑ ventilation, ↑ erythropoietin → ↑ Hct, ↑ 2,3-BPG, ↑ mitochondria, renal HCO3− excretion. COMLEX: FA notes acetazolamide can augment the renal compensation for the respiratory alkalosis of ascent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — respiratory and renal compensation in high-altitude acclimatization","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Decreased hemoglobin, decrease"}},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q25_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Exercise adaptations enhancing oxygen transport","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old cyclist completes an incremental exercise test. At peak exercise, her pulmonary blood flow is 25 L/min and her pulmonary capillary transit time is reduced to 0.3 seconds. Despite this brief transit time, her arterial oxygen saturation remains at 96%. Which factor is MOST responsible for maintaining adequate oxygenation?","choices":{"A":"Decreased metabolic rate during exercise reduces oxygen demand","B":"Hemoglobin oxygen-binding affinity increases during exercise","C":"Alveolar PO₂ increases to 200 mm Hg during heavy breathing","D":"Diffusing capacity triples; blood saturates in the capillary's first third","E":"Blood velocity decreases during exercise, increasing capillary transit time"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Two critical factors maintain adequate oxygenation despite reduced capillary transit time during exercise: (1) at rest, blood becomes fully saturated with oxygen within the first THIRD of its transit through the pulmonary capillary, providing a 3-fold safety margin; (2) during exercise, the pulmonary diffusing capacity for oxygen increases almost 3-fold (from ~21 to ~65 mL/min/mm Hg) due to recruitment of additional capillary surface area and more ideal ventilation-perfusion ratios. Even though capillary transit time may be reduced from about 0.8 second to about 0.3 second (Guyton Ch 39) (less than half of resting), the combination of the resting safety margin and increased diffusing capacity ensures complete oxygenation. Decreased metabolic rate is incorrect—metabolic rate increases dramatically. Increased hemoglobin affinity is incorrect—affinity actually decreases (Bohr effect). Alveolar PO₂ of 200 mm Hg is incorrect—alveolar PO₂ cannot approach 200 mm Hg on room air, since humidified inspired air has a PO₂ of only about 149 mm Hg (Guyton Ch 40). Slower blood velocity is opposite to what occurs—blood velocity increases with increased cardiac output.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two safety factors keep arterial blood saturated in exercise — blood normally saturates within the first third of the capillary (about three times the time needed), and O2 diffusing capacity rises almost 3-fold. COMLEX: Exertional desaturation therefore signals lost reserve — thickened membrane or lost capillary bed — and is a common early finding in ILD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 12 — normal capillary transit time and gas exchange efficiency","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q26_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — CO₂ transport mechanisms including carbonic anhydrase, bicarbonate, and carbaminohemoglobin","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with glaucoma is prescribed acetazolamide, a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, to reduce intraocular pressure. As a side effect, the drug also inhibits carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells throughout her body. What compensatory mechanism partially maintains CO₂ transport despite carbonic anhydrase inhibition?","choices":{"A":"Increased ventilation rate to compensate for impaired CO₂ transport","B":"Conversion of all CO₂ transport to the carbaminohemoglobin pathway","C":"Enhanced CO₂ solubility in plasma from drug-induced pH changes","D":"Increased hemoglobin concentration to enhance CO₂-carrying capacity","E":"Direct CO₂ excretion through the kidneys bypassing pulmonary elimination"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Red-cell carbonic anhydrase normally makes the bicarbonate route carry about 70% of the CO₂ leaving the tissues; blocking it makes CO₂ transport so poor that tissue PCO₂ can rise toward 80 mm Hg instead of the normal 45. The rising PCO₂ stimulates the central and peripheral chemoreceptors, and the extra ventilation lowers alveolar and arterial PCO₂ — widening the gradient that carries CO₂ out of the tissues and the blood. An all-carbaminohemoglobin pathway is wrong because carbaminohemoglobin cannot take over all CO₂ transport (the slow uncatalyzed reaction and dissolved CO₂ still carry some); enhanced plasma solubility, increased hemoglobin, and renal CO₂ excretion describe effects that do not occur.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Without red-cell carbonic anhydrase, CO2 transport is so poor that tissue PCO2 may rise toward 80 mm Hg; the rising PCO2 and H+ stimulate the chemoreceptors and increase ventilation, partly offsetting the defect. COMLEX: FA — acetazolamide is a sulfa drug used for altitude, idiopathic intracranial hypertension and urinary alkalinization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 7 — how central and peripheral chemoreceptors drive respiration","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q27_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — CO₂ transport mechanisms including carbonic anhydrase, bicarbonate, and carbaminohemoglobin","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology student learns that in venous blood, chloride concentration in red blood cells is higher than in arterial blood, while bicarbonate concentration in RBCs is lower in venous blood. What is the primary mechanism responsible for this \"chloride shift\"?","choices":{"A":"Active transport pumps that move Cl⁻ into RBCs in exchange for ATP","B":"Carbonic anhydrase directly transports Cl⁻ across the RBC membrane","C":"A bicarbonate-chloride exchanger that swaps the ions to keep electroneutrality","D":"Hemoglobin binding to chloride ions, pulling them into red blood cells","E":"Passive diffusion of Cl⁻ and HCO₃⁻ following their concentration gradients alone"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The chloride shift (also called the Hamburger shift) occurs via a specific bicarbonate-chloride exchanger protein (also known as anion exchanger 1 or Band 3 protein) in the red blood cell membrane. This carrier protein facilitates the reciprocal exchange of HCO₃⁻ and Cl⁻ ions at rapid velocities to maintain electrical neutrality across the RBC membrane. In tissues, as CO₂ enters RBCs and is converted to HCO₃⁻ by carbonic anhydrase, the HCO₃⁻ concentration inside RBCs rises. Much of this HCO₃⁻ diffuses out into plasma to be transported to the lungs. To maintain electrical neutrality as negatively charged HCO₃⁻ leaves the cell, negatively charged Cl⁻ ions enter from the plasma. In the lungs, the process reverses—HCO₃⁻ moves back into RBCs, and Cl⁻ moves out. This explains why venous RBCs have higher Cl⁻ and lower HCO₃⁻ than arterial RBCs. An ATP-driven pump incorrectly invokes ATP. Carbonic anhydrase transport misattributes the transport function. Hemoglobin binding chloride describes incorrect hemoglobin binding. Passive diffusion ignores the specific carrier protein required.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The chloride shift — a bicarbonate-chloride carrier protein in the red-cell membrane exchanges HCO3− out for Cl− in at rapid velocity, so venous red cells contain more chloride than arterial red cells. FA's CO2 transport diagram shows the same HCO3−/Cl− exchange. COMLEX: The exchange lets the red cell export most of its bicarbonate to plasma, where FA places about 90% of CO2 (Guyton: about 70%).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 2 — CO2 transport mechanisms and the bicarbonate route","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q28_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — CO₂ transport mechanisms including carbonic anhydrase, bicarbonate, and carbaminohemoglobin","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man's blood sample is analyzed in the laboratory. When the blood PCO₂ is 40 mm Hg and the sample is fully oxygenated (PO₂ = 100 mm Hg), the blood contains approximately 48 volume percent CO₂. What approximate CO₂ content would you expect if the same blood were deoxygenated (PO₂ = 40 mm Hg) while maintaining the same PCO₂ of 40 mm Hg?","choices":{"A":"45 volume percent (decreased from oxygenated state)","B":"48 volume percent (unchanged from oxygenated state)","C":"46 volume percent (slightly decreased from oxygenated state)","D":"52 volume percent (significantly increased from oxygenated state)","E":"50 volume percent (slightly increased from oxygenated state)"},"correct":"E","explanation":"At the same PCO₂, deoxygenated blood carries more CO₂ than oxygenated blood due to the Haldane effect. When hemoglobin releases oxygen (becomes deoxygenated), it becomes a weaker acid, which has two consequences: (1) reduced hemoglobin can form carbaminohemoglobin more readily, binding more CO₂; (2) reduced hemoglobin takes up H⁺ ions more readily, driving the equilibrium HCO₃⁻ + H⁺ → H₂CO₃ → CO₂ + H₂O to the left, allowing more CO₂ to be carried as bicarbonate. The CO₂ dissociation curves for oxygenated and deoxygenated blood are shifted vertically by about 2 volume percent: at a PCO₂ of 40 mm Hg, oxygenated blood holds about 48 volume percent CO₂ and deoxygenated blood about 50. This difference is crucial for CO₂ transport—in the tissues, deoxygenation facilitates CO₂ uptake; in the lungs, oxygenation facilitates CO₂ release. 45 volume percent and 46 volume percent incorrectly show decreases. 48 volume percent incorrectly suggests no change. 52 volume percent is the venous value at a PCO₂ of 45 mm Hg — it adds the effect of the higher PCO₂ to the Haldane shift.","boardPrep":"Step 1: At the same PCO2, deoxygenated blood holds more CO2 than oxygenated blood — reduced hemoglobin is a weaker acid, forms carbaminohemoglobin more readily and binds more H+, favoring bicarbonate formation (Haldane effect). COMLEX: This is why venous blood takes up CO2 in tissues where O2 is unloaded — the two transports reinforce each other.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 2 — CO2 transport and the CO2 dissociation curve","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"48 volume percent incorrectly "}},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q29_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Effects of carbon monoxide on hemoglobin function and oxygen-carrying capacity","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old man is found unconscious in his car with the engine running in a closed garage. His arterial blood shows 40% carboxyhemoglobin (CO-Hgb). Besides directly reducing oxygen-carrying capacity, how does carbon monoxide poisoning additionally impair oxygen delivery to tissues?","choices":{"A":"CO shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the LEFT, impairing oxygen release","B":"CO shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the RIGHT, causing excessive release","C":"CO has no effect on the dissociation curve for hemoglobin still bound to oxygen","D":"CO increases 2,3-BPG levels, which partially compensates for reduced oxygen capacity","E":"CO increases oxygen solubility in plasma, which fully compensates for hemoglobin loss"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Carbon monoxide poisoning impairs oxygen delivery through two mechanisms: (1) directly reducing oxygen-carrying capacity by occupying hemoglobin binding sites, and (2) shifting the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the LEFT for the remaining oxygen-bound hemoglobin. This leftward shift occurs because when some hemoglobin subunits are bound to CO, the remaining subunits that are bound to oxygen have INCREASED affinity for oxygen due to cooperative binding effects. This means the oxygen that is still bound to hemoglobin is held more tightly and released less readily to tissues. This combination—reduced oxygen content AND impaired oxygen release—makes CO poisoning particularly dangerous. For example, with 50% CO-Hgb, the blood carries only half the normal oxygen, and what oxygen remains is released less readily. The leftward shift can lower tissue PO₂ even more than would be predicted from the reduced oxygen content alone, potentially causing tissue hypoxia even at oxygen saturations that might be tolerable in simple anemia. The other options are incorrect. First Aid lists raised CO as a cause of a left shift, not a right one; a right shift would lower hemoglobin affinity and ease O2 unloading.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CO harms O2 delivery twice — it occupies hemoglobin's O2 sites (lower content and %O2Hb) and shifts the curve of the remaining oxyhemoglobin left, so tissues get less of what O2 there is. FA: left shift → ↑ affinity → ↓ unloading; CO is the leading worldwide cause of death by poisoning. COMLEX: FA — CO is classically associated with bilateral globus pallidus lesions on MRI.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 11 — classification of hypoxia, including abnormal hemoglobin transport of O2","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"First Aid lists raised CO as a cause of a left shift, not a "}},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q30_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Effects of carbon monoxide on hemoglobin function and oxygen-carrying capacity","batch":1,"stem":"A 25-year-old woman is exposed to carbon monoxide while camping with a faulty propane heater. Her blood is analyzed and shows 30% carboxyhemoglobin. Despite this significant CO poisoning, her peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid and aortic bodies) do NOT trigger increased ventilation. Why do peripheral chemoreceptors fail to respond appropriately to CO poisoning?","choices":{"A":"CO directly inhibits peripheral chemoreceptor function, preventing their activation","B":"CO poisoning causes immediate unconsciousness, eliminating all respiratory drive","C":"CO increases PCO₂ dramatically, which overrides the hypoxia signal completely","D":"Peripheral chemoreceptors respond to arterial PO₂, which remains normal in CO poisoning","E":"CO converts to CO₂ in peripheral chemoreceptors, fooling them into sensing normal oxygen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid and aortic bodies) primarily respond to arterial PO₂ (the partial pressure of dissolved oxygen in arterial blood), NOT to oxygen content or hemoglobin saturation. In CO poisoning, even though oxygen content is dramatically reduced (because hemoglobin sites are occupied by CO), the arterial PO₂ remains normal or even elevated because dissolved oxygen equilibrates normally with alveolar air. The alveolar-to-blood oxygen gradient is unaffected, so dissolved oxygen (measured as PO₂) is normal. Since peripheral chemoreceptors sense PO₂ rather than oxygen content, they fail to detect the tissue hypoxia occurring in CO poisoning. This lack of appropriate ventilatory response makes CO poisoning particularly insidious—victims may become disoriented, weak, and unconscious without experiencing the breathlessness (dyspnea) that would normally accompany severe hypoxia. Direct chemoreceptor inhibition is incorrect—CO doesn't directly inhibit chemoreceptors. Immediate unconsciousness is incorrect—consciousness may be maintained initially. A dramatic PCO₂ rise is incorrect—PCO₂ doesn't dramatically increase. Conversion of CO to CO₂ is incorrect—CO doesn't convert to CO₂.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In CO poisoning arterial PO2 is not reduced, so the feedback that normally raises respiration in hypoxemia is absent, and the blood stays bright red without cyanosis. COMLEX: FA — CO poisoning shows normal PaO2 with ↑ carboxyhemoglobin on co-oximetry, so neither SpO2 nor breathing pattern rules it out.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 6 — central versus peripheral chemoreceptors and what each senses","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q31_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Effects of carbon monoxide on hemoglobin function and oxygen-carrying capacity","batch":1,"stem":"A 50-year-old firefighter is rescued from a burning building with suspected carbon monoxide poisoning. Blood analysis reveals 45% carboxyhemoglobin. He is treated with 100% oxygen at 3 atmospheres pressure (hyperbaric oxygen therapy). What is the rationale for using hyperbaric oxygen rather than just 100% oxygen at 1 atmosphere?","choices":{"A":"High pressure oxygen damages carboxyhemoglobin molecules, preventing CO rebinding","B":"Hyperbaric conditions cause hemoglobin to release CO more rapidly through pressure effects","C":"Raised O₂ pressure speeds CO displacement and adds dissolved oxygen","D":"High pressure oxygen stimulates new red blood cell production to replace CO-bound cells","E":"Hyperbaric therapy is no more effective than normobaric 100% oxygen for CO poisoning"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Raising the inspired O₂ pressure speeds CO removal and adds dissolved O₂. CO binds hemoglobin about 250 times as tightly as O₂ (Guyton Ch 41; > 200 times in First Aid 2025), so a very high alveolar PO₂ is needed to displace it by mass action. The carboxyhemoglobin half-life is about 300–320 minutes on room air, about 80 minutes on 100% O₂ at 1 atm, and about 20 minutes with hyperbaric O₂ at 2–3 atm (First Aid 2025; Katzung Ch 56). Dissolved O₂ rises in proportion to PO₂ (about 0.29 mL/100 mL at a PO₂ of 95 mm Hg, Guyton Ch 41), so at several atmospheres plasma alone carries a useful amount of O₂ while hemoglobin is blocked. Katzung calls hyperbaric O₂ somewhat controversial but recommends considering it when carboxyhemoglobin exceeds 50% and particularly in pregnancy. High pressure does not damage carboxyhemoglobin, force CO off by a direct pressure effect, or act by stimulating red cell production; the faster CO clearance is why it is chosen over normobaric O₂ in severe cases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: High alveolar PO2 displaces CO from hemoglobin by mass action and adds dissolved O2. FA: CO-Hb half-life is about 300 min on room air, about 80 min on 100% O2 and about 20 min in a hyperbaric chamber. COMLEX: FA — give 100% O2 at once, hyperbaric O2 if severe, and suspect concurrent cyanide poisoning in fire victims.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — hyperbaric oxygen therapy and its mechanism of action","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"High pressure does not damage ","B":"High pressure does not damage "}}],"15":[{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q01_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — PO₂, PCO₂, and pH as fundamental measures of pulmonary performance","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with suspected respiratory failure has arterial blood gases drawn in the emergency department. The laboratory measures his arterial PO₂, PCO₂, and pH using specialized electrodes. Which combination of methods is used to measure these three fundamental parameters?","choices":{"A":"All three measurements use the same polarography electrode technique","B":"All three measurements use variations of the pH meter with glass electrodes","C":"pH: glass electrode; PCO₂: bicarbonate-pH electrode; PO₂: polarography","D":"pH uses colorimetry; PCO₂ uses infrared spectroscopy; PO₂ uses polarography","E":"All three measurements use infrared spectroscopy techniques"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Blood gas measurements use three distinct electrode systems: (1) pH is measured using a pH meter with a glass electrode and reference electrode that measures voltage differences; (2) PCO₂ is measured using a glass pH electrode surrounded by a sodium bicarbonate solution separated from blood by a plastic membrane—CO₂ diffuses into the bicarbonate solution and the resulting pH change is measured and converted to PCO₂ using the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation; (3) PO₂ is measured using polarography with a negative platinum electrode separated from blood by a plastic membrane—oxygen diffuses to the electrode and the resulting current flow is directly proportional to PO₂. These three measurements can often be made within a minute using a single droplet of blood, allowing moment-by-moment monitoring at the bedside. The other options incorrectly describe the measurement techniques used for these fundamental parameters. Only PO2 uses polarography; pH and PCO2 use glass and bicarbonate electrodes. Colorimetry and infrared spectroscopy are not among the methods Guyton describes; pH uses a glass electrode, PCO2 a modified pH electrode and PO2 polarography. Infrared spectroscopy is not among the methods Guyton describes; pH, PCO2 and PO2 are measured with electrode systems.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Blood gases are measured in minutes from a few drops of blood — pH with a glass electrode, PCO2 with a glass pH electrode bathed in bicarbonate behind a CO2-permeable membrane, and PO2 by polarography at a negative platinum electrode. COMLEX: FA's ABG ladder starts with pH (< 7.35 acidemia, > 7.45 alkalemia), then PCO2 and HCO3− to find the primary disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 5 — how O2, CO2 and H+ act as the controlled variables of respiration","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Only PO2 uses polarography; pH and PCO2 use glass and bicarb","D":"Colorimetry and infrared spectroscopy are not among the meth","E":"Infrared spectroscopy is not among the methods Guyton descri"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q02_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — PO₂, PCO₂, and pH as fundamental measures of pulmonary performance","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist needs to rapidly assess pulmonary performance in a patient with acute respiratory distress. Arterial blood gas analysis shows PO₂ of 55 mm Hg, PCO₂ of 60 mm Hg, and pH of 7.28. Why are these three measurements considered the most fundamental tests of pulmonary performance?","choices":{"A":"They directly assess lung volumes, dead space, and respiratory muscle strength simultaneously","B":"They report oxygenation, ventilation, and acid-base status quickly enough to guide therapy","C":"They measure pulmonary diffusing capacity more accurately than other available methods","D":"They are the only tests that can differentiate obstructive from restrictive lung disease","E":"They directly measure airway resistance and lung compliance in real-time"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PO₂, PCO₂, and pH are considered the most fundamental tests of pulmonary performance because they provide immediate, crucial information that guides therapy: (1) PO₂ assesses oxygenation adequacy—low PO₂ (hypoxemia) indicates impaired gas exchange; (2) PCO₂ assesses ventilation adequacy—elevated PCO₂ (hypercapnia) indicates alveolar hypoventilation or severe V/Q mismatch; (3) pH assesses acid-base status—changes in pH reflect respiratory compensation (or failure) and metabolic disturbances. These measurements can be made rapidly (within minutes) using only drops of blood, allowing clinicians to determine appropriate therapy for acute respiratory distress or acid-base abnormalities. This patient shows hypoxemia, hypercapnia, and respiratory acidosis, indicating severe respiratory failure requiring immediate intervention. The other options describe capabilities these measurements don't possess—they assess gas exchange status, not mechanical properties or structural characteristics of lungs. Guyton values blood PO2, PCO2 and pH because they can be measured within minutes to guide therapy; diffusing capacity is measured separately, by the carbon monoxide method.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton calls arterial PO2, PCO2 and pH among the most fundamental tests of pulmonary performance — often measured within minutes, they report oxygenation, ventilation and acid-base status at once. COMLEX: FA — PCO2 > 44 mm Hg with pH < 7.35 is respiratory acidosis, from hypoventilation, airway obstruction, lung disease, opioids or sedatives, or muscle weakness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 14 — when hypoxia and hypercapnia coexist and when they occur independently","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The other options describe cap","C":"Guyton values blood PO2, PCO2 and pH because they can be mea","E":"The other options describe cap"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q03_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — PO₂, PCO₂, and pH as fundamental measures of pulmonary performance","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman with suspected pulmonary disease undergoes arterial blood gas analysis. Her results show PO₂ of 58 mm Hg (normal 80-100), PCO₂ of 48 mm Hg (normal 35-45), and pH of 7.34 (normal 7.35-7.45). What do these measurements reveal about her pulmonary performance?","choices":{"A":"Normal pulmonary function with no gas exchange abnormalities","B":"Isolated metabolic acidosis without respiratory involvement","C":"Normal oxygenation with isolated ventilatory failure","D":"Hyperventilation causing respiratory alkalosis","E":"Impaired oxygenation and ventilation with respiratory acidosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient has impaired pulmonary performance on all three measurements: (1) hypoxemia (PO₂ 58 mm Hg, below the normal 80–100), indicating impaired oxygenation; (2) hypercapnia (PCO₂ 48 mm Hg, above 35–45), indicating inadequate alveolar ventilation or severe V/Q mismatch; and (3) pH 7.34, just below 7.35, an acidemia caused by the retained CO₂ — a respiratory acidosis. No bicarbonate is given, so whether the kidneys have begun to compensate cannot be judged from these values. The combination of low PO₂ and high PCO₂ points to significant lung disease such as COPD or severe pneumonia. The values are not normal, the high PCO₂ rules out a purely metabolic process or respiratory alkalosis, and the oxygenation is clearly abnormal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low PO2 means impaired oxygenation, PCO2 above normal means inadequate alveolar ventilation, and pH below 7.35 with a high PCO2 is a respiratory acidosis. FA: PCO2 > 44 mm Hg with acidemia → respiratory acidosis; renal HCO3− reabsorption compensates later. COMLEX: Acute versus chronic is judged by how far HCO3− has risen — compensation is delayed in respiratory disorders.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 10 — the composite effects of PCO2, pH and PO2 on alveolar ventilation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"The values are not normal, the"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q04_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Principle of maximum expiratory flow","batch":1,"stem":"A 50-year-old man performs a maximum expiratory flow maneuver. Despite exerting tremendous additional expiratory force, his expiratory airflow reaches a plateau at 350 L/min and cannot be increased further. What physiological mechanism limits maximum expiratory flow beyond this point?","choices":{"A":"The alveoli become completely emptied of air, preventing further expiration","B":"The expiratory muscles have reached their maximum force-generating capacity","C":"Oxygen depletion in expired air prevents further gas exchange","D":"The diaphragm contracts reflexively to prevent complete lung collapse","E":"Pleural pressure narrows airways as much as it raises driving pressure"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Maximum expiratory flow is limited by dynamic airway compression. Once maximum flow is reached, extra expiratory effort raises pleural pressure, which raises alveolar (driving) pressure but compresses the bronchioles from outside by the same amount; because flow equals driving pressure divided by resistance, the two effects cancel and flow cannot rise further, even with greatly increased force (Guyton Ch 43). The flow plateau is effort-independent, so the respiratory muscles are not at their limit — they are generating more force with no gain in flow. The alveoli never empty completely (residual volume remains), the diaphragm relaxes rather than contracts during forced expiration, and O2 content of expired air has nothing to do with expiratory flow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In a forced expiration the same pressure that squeezes the alveoli also squeezes the bronchioles; once they nearly collapse, more effort raises alveolar pressure and airway resistance by equal amounts, so flow cannot rise further — the effort-independent maximum expiratory flow. COMLEX: Robbins' emphysema patient ('pink puffer') shows prolonged expiration, sits hunched forward and breathes through pursed lips.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — the mechanics of expiration, including the pleural pressure that compresses the airways","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"The alveoli never empty comple"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q05_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Principle of maximum expiratory flow","batch":1,"stem":"A physiologist studies maximum expiratory flow at different lung volumes. After a subject inspires maximally to total lung capacity (TLC) and then expires with maximum effort, the maximum expiratory flow rate exceeds 400 L/min. However, as lung volume decreases during the expiration, the maximum flow rate progressively declines. What explains this decrease in maximum expiratory flow as lung volume decreases?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar PO₂ decreases as lungs empty, reducing the driving force for expiration","B":"Respiratory muscles fatigue during prolonged expiratory effort","C":"Decreased oxygen content in remaining alveolar air reduces gas diffusion rates","D":"Airways collapse more easily at low volumes as elastic tethering is lost","E":"Increased airway resistance from mucus accumulation blocks smaller airways"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Maximum expiratory flow decreases as lung volume decreases primarily because bronchi and bronchioles become more susceptible to dynamic compression at lower lung volumes. When lungs are fully inflated (at TLC), the bronchi and bronchioles are held open by elastic tethering—radial traction from lung structural elements pulling outward on airway walls. As lung volume decreases during expiration, these elastic structures become relaxed, reducing the outward tethering force that normally helps keep airways open. With less structural support, the bronchi and bronchioles collapse more easily when subjected to external pressure from forced expiration, progressively reducing maximum expiratory flow. This explains why the maximum expiratory flow-volume curve shows highest flow rates at large lung volumes and progressively decreasing flow as volume decreases toward residual volume. The other options describe mechanisms that either don't occur or aren't the primary cause of flow limitation. Guyton's main reason for the fall is not muscle fatigue but that the shrinking lung relaxes the elastic pull holding the bronchi open, so they collapse more easily. Maximum expiratory flow is limited by bronchiolar collapse under chest pressure, not by alveolar oxygen content or gas diffusion. Mucus accumulation is not the cause in a normal subject; the fall in maximum flow comes from loss of elastic traction at low volumes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In the expanded lung, bronchi and bronchioles are held open partly by elastic pull of lung structural elements on their outsides; as the lung empties these relax, the airways collapse more easily under chest pressure, and maximum expiratory flow falls progressively with volume. COMLEX: FA — emphysema raises compliance and lowers elastic recoil, removing this support, so flow collapses early in expiration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — the major obstructive pulmonary diseases and their mechanics","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Guyton's main reason for the fall is not muscle fatigue but ","C":"Maximum expiratory flow is limited by bronchiolar collapse u","E":"Mucus accumulation is not the cause in a normal subject; the"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q06_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Principle of maximum expiratory flow","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old asthmatic patient performs a forced expiration test. Her physician notes that she reaches a maximum expiratory flow of only 200 L/min (normal >400 L/min). During which phase of respiration is airway obstruction typically more severe in asthma, and why?","choices":{"A":"Inspiration is more obstructed because negative pleural pressure collapses airways","B":"Both phases are equally obstructed due to bronchial smooth muscle constriction","C":"Expiration is more obstructed because positive pleural pressure further narrows already constricted airways","D":"Inspiration is more obstructed because airway dilation during expiration compensates for bronchoconstriction","E":"Neither phase is significantly obstructed because asthma primarily affects alveolar function"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In asthma and other obstructive diseases, expiration is typically much more difficult than inspiration. During forced expiration, positive pleural pressure (from chest compression) not only drives air out but also compresses airways from the outside. In asthmatic patients with already narrowed bronchioles from smooth muscle spasm, edema, and mucus, this external compression causes airways to collapse even more readily than normal, greatly increasing airway resistance during expiration. In contrast, during inspiration, negative pleural pressure actually pulls airways open (radial traction) while simultaneously expanding alveoli, making inspiration relatively easier. This explains why asthmatic patients often can inspire adequately but have great difficulty expiring—air becomes trapped in lungs, leading to increased functional residual capacity and residual volume. Inspiration being more obstructed shows the wrong direction—negative pressure opens airways. Equal obstruction of both phases is incorrect because phases are not equally affected. Inspiration from expiratory dilation and no significant obstruction are incorrect. Airways do not dilate during expiration; positive pleural pressure narrows them, making expiration worse. Asthma affects the airways, not the alveoli, and expiratory obstruction is marked.","boardPrep":"Step 1: With airway obstruction it is much harder to expire than to inspire — positive expiratory chest pressure adds to the closing tendency of narrowed airways, while negative inspiratory pleural pressure pulls them open; air enters easily and is trapped. COMLEX: FA — asthma is diagnosed by an obstructive spirometry pattern with bronchodilator response (it may be normal between attacks), +/− methacholine challenge.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — the etiology and pathogenesis of asthma and its phenotypes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Inspiration being more obstruc","B":"Equal obstruction of both phas","D":"Airways do not dilate during expiration; positive pleural pr","E":"Asthma affects the airways, not the alveoli, and expiratory "}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q08_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Obstructive vs restrictive disease on Maximum Expiratory Flow-Volume Curve","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist reviews maximum expiratory flow-volume curves from two patients with different lung diseases. Patient A has pulmonary fibrosis (restrictive disease), while Patient B has emphysema (obstructive disease). How would their curves differ from normal in terms of lung volumes?","choices":{"A":"Both patients show increased total lung capacity and increased residual volume","B":"Restrictive disease shows reduced TLC; obstructive shows normal TLC","C":"Both patients show normal lung volumes but reduced maximum expiratory flow rates","D":"Obstructive disease shows increased TLC; restrictive shows normal TLC and increased RV","E":"Restrictive disease shows reduced TLC and RV; obstructive shows increased TLC and RV"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Restrictive and obstructive diseases produce opposite patterns on flow-volume curves: (1) Restrictive diseases (pulmonary fibrosis, kyphosis, scoliosis) cause REDUCED total lung capacity (TLC) and REDUCED residual volume (RV) because the lungs cannot expand normally due to stiffness or chest cage limitation. The flow-volume curve is shifted downward and leftward, with reduced volumes at all points. (2) Obstructive diseases (emphysema, severe asthma) cause INCREASED TLC and INCREASED RV due to air trapping—airways close during expiration, trapping air in alveoli. Over time, this increases both TLC and RV. The obstructive curve shows reduced maximum expiratory flow rates and is shifted upward and rightward. A normal TLC with a raised RV does not describe restriction: Guyton (Ch 43) notes that constricted lungs have both reduced TLC and reduced RV. The other options don't correctly describe these opposite patterns. Obstructive disease such as emphysema shows increased, not normal, TLC and RV.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Constricted (restrictive) lungs have reduced TLC and RV; obstruction traps air so TLC and RV rise over months to years. FA: obstructive — ↑ RV, ↑ FRC, ↑ TLC, flow-volume loop shifted left; restrictive — ↓ volumes, loop shifted right. COMLEX: Per the pulmonary OMM lab, both patterns raise the work of breathing — from about 2–3% of energy expenditure at baseline to as much as 30–35% in significant disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 10 — the lung capacities IC, FRC, VC and TLC","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Obstructive disease such as emphysema shows increased, not n","D":"A normal TLC with a raised RV "}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q10_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Obstructive vs restrictive disease on Maximum Expiratory Flow-Volume Curve","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis undergoes pulmonary function testing. Her maximum expiratory flow-volume curve shows a total lung capacity of 3.5 L (normal 5.5 L) and residual volume of 0.8 L (normal 1.2 L). Why is her maximum expiratory flow rate also reduced despite having restrictive, not obstructive, disease?","choices":{"A":"She cannot reach the large lung volumes at which the highest flow rates occur","B":"Fibrosis directly obstructs airways similar to asthma, causing expiratory flow limitation","C":"Reduced lung compliance decreases the elastic recoil force available to drive expiration","D":"Fibrotic tissue produces excess mucus that blocks airways during forced expiration","E":"The diaphragm is weakened in restrictive disease, preventing forceful expiration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In restrictive lung disease like pulmonary fibrosis, maximum expiratory flow rate is reduced even though the primary problem is not airway obstruction. Maximum expiratory flow depends heavily on lung volume—flow rates are highest when lungs are fully inflated to total lung capacity because airways are maximally stretched open by elastic tethering from surrounding lung tissue. In this patient, her TLC is only 3.5 L (instead of normal 5.5 L) because fibrosis prevents normal lung expansion. Since she cannot reach normal lung volumes even with maximum inspiratory effort, she cannot achieve the high expiratory flow rates that would occur at those larger volumes. Her maximum expiratory flow rate is reduced because the starting point (TLC) is so much lower, not because of intrinsic airway obstruction. The curve shows reduced peak flow with a relatively normal shape, just compressed to smaller volumes. Fibrotic airway obstruction, excess mucus, and diaphragm weakness describe mechanisms that don't occur in restrictive disease. Reduced elastic recoil mentions elastic recoil but this isn't the primary reason for reduced flow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Because the constricted lung cannot expand to a normal maximum volume, even maximal effort cannot raise expiratory flow to the normal curve — flow is highest at high lung volumes. COMLEX: FA — idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis gives progressive dyspnea, dry cough, velcro crackles and clubbing, with a usual interstitial pneumonia pattern and honeycombing in advanced disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and the other major restrictive lung diseases","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Fibrotic airway obstruction, e"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q11_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Obstructive vs restrictive disease on Maximum Expiratory Flow-Volume Curve","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with severe COPD/emphysema has a maximum expiratory flow-volume curve that shows increased total lung capacity (7.0 L, normal 5.5 L) and dramatically increased residual volume (3.5 L, normal 1.2 L). What pathophysiological mechanism primarily causes these increased lung volumes?","choices":{"A":"Enhanced respiratory muscle strength from chronic training increases lung expansion capacity","B":"Loss of alveolar walls decreases lung compliance, holding the lungs stiffly expanded","C":"Mucus hypersecretion expands airways, increasing total lung air volume","D":"Airway collapse during expiration traps air in alveoli, progressively increasing volumes over time","E":"Pulmonary hypertension forces more blood into lungs, displacing air into larger volumes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In obstructive diseases like emphysema, airways tend to collapse during expiration because: (1) positive pleural pressure during expiration compresses airways; (2) loss of elastic tethering from destroyed alveolar walls means airways aren't held open normally; (3) airways may be intrinsically narrowed from inflammation and mucus. During each expiration, airways close prematurely, trapping air distal to the obstruction. This trapped air cannot be expired, leading to progressive air accumulation over time. Over months to years, this causes both TLC and RV to increase significantly—this patient's RV of 3.5 L is nearly three times normal. The increased volumes represent air that cannot be expelled due to airway closure, not increased lung capacity from other mechanisms. Stronger respiratory muscles is incorrect—muscles don't cause this. Decreased compliance is wrong because loss of alveolar walls increases, not decreases, compliance. Mucus hypersecretion and pulmonary hypertension are incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Obstruction makes expiration harder than inspiration, so air is trapped and, over months or years, both TLC and RV rise. FA: emphysema CXR shows a barrel chest, increased AP diameter, flattened diaphragm and increased lucency. COMLEX: The pulmonary OMM lab ties chronic COPD hyperinflation to a barrel chest with shortened respiratory muscles and increased resting tone — targets for rib and diaphragm OMT.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — the four main types of emphysema and their morphology","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Stronger respiratory muscles i"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q12_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — FEV1, FVC, and FEV1/FVC ratio with airway obstruction","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with suspected COPD performs forced vital capacity (FVC) maneuvers. His results show FVC of 4.5 L (normal 5.0 L) and FEV₁ of 2.1 L (normal 4.0 L). What is his FEV₁/FVC ratio, and what does this indicate?","choices":{"A":"FEV₁/FVC = 90%; indicates normal airway function","B":"FEV₁/FVC = 47%; indicates significant airway obstruction","C":"FEV₁/FVC = 53%; indicates mild restrictive disease","D":"FEV₁/FVC = 80%; indicates normal pulmonary function","E":"FEV₁/FVC = 35%; indicates severe restrictive disease"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient's FEV₁/FVC ratio is (2.1 L / 4.5 L) × 100% = 47%, which indicates significant airway obstruction. In healthy individuals, the FEV₁/FVC ratio is normally about 80%—meaning 80% of the total forced vital capacity can be expired in the first second. As airway obstruction worsens, this ratio decreases progressively because airways narrow or collapse during expiration, greatly slowing expiratory airflow. This patient can only expire 47% of his FVC in the first second, indicating substantial obstruction consistent with moderate-to-severe COPD. The relatively preserved FVC (4.5 L vs. normal 5.0 L) indicates this is primarily an obstructive process rather than restrictive disease. In severe airway obstruction such as acute asthma, the FEV₁/FVC ratio can decrease to less than 20%. 90% and 80% show normal ratios. 53% with restriction and 35% with restriction incorrectly suggest restrictive disease, which would show reduced FVC with preserved or increased FEV₁/FVC ratio.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FEV1/FVC is about 80% normally (Guyton) and falls to 47% in his obstruction example, below 20% in serious acute asthma; FA calls > 70% normal, with FEV1 reduced more than FVC in obstruction. COMLEX: A low FEV1/FVC is the spirometric definition of obstruction in COPD and asthma, so it is the first number to read on a PFT report.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — the major obstructive lung diseases and their clinical features","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"90% and 80% show normal ratios"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q13_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — FEV1, FVC, and FEV1/FVC ratio with airway obstruction","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist explains to a medical student that the FEV₁/FVC ratio is one of the most important measurements for distinguishing obstructive from restrictive lung disease. In a patient with pure restrictive disease (pulmonary fibrosis), what would be the expected pattern of FEV₁, FVC, and their ratio?","choices":{"A":"Reduced FEV₁, reduced FVC, normal or increased ratio (≥80%)","B":"Reduced FEV₁, normal FVC, reduced ratio (<70%)","C":"Normal FEV₁, normal FVC, normal ratio (~80%)","D":"Normal FEV₁, reduced FVC, increased ratio (>90%)","E":"Reduced FEV₁, reduced FVC, markedly reduced ratio (<50%)"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In pure restrictive disease such as pulmonary fibrosis the lungs cannot expand normally, so FVC is reduced, and FEV₁ falls roughly in proportion; because the stiff lungs empty quickly, the FEV₁/FVC ratio stays normal or even rises (Guyton Ch 43). A markedly reduced ratio (<50%) or a ratio below 70% with normal FVC is the obstructive pattern, in which airway narrowing slows emptying so FEV₁ falls more than FVC. All-normal values exclude disease, and a normal FEV₁ with a reduced FVC is not the expected pattern — in restriction FEV₁ falls along with FVC.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In restriction FVC and FEV1 fall roughly together, so FEV1/FVC is normal or increased; FA: restrictive disease lowers FVC and TLC with a normal or ↑ ratio. COMLEX: FA splits restriction by DLCO and A-a gradient — normal in neuromuscular or chest-wall disease, reduced DLCO with ↑ A-a gradient in interstitial disease such as pneumoconioses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 4 — the major pneumoconioses, classic interstitial causes of restriction","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"All-normal values exclude dise","D":"All-normal values exclude dise"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q14_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — FEV1, FVC, and FEV1/FVC ratio with airway obstruction","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with asthma exacerbation performs spirometry. Before bronchodilator treatment, her FEV₁ is 1.8 L and FVC is 3.6 L (FEV₁/FVC = 50%). After inhaled albuterol, her FEV₁ increases to 2.7 L while FVC increases to 4.0 L (FEV₁/FVC = 67.5%). What does this bronchodilator response indicate?","choices":{"A":"The obstruction is fixed and irreversible, indicating emphysema rather than asthma","B":"Bronchodilator therapy is ineffective and should be discontinued immediately","C":"Normal pulmonary function has been completely restored by bronchodilator therapy","D":"The patient has restrictive lung disease that paradoxically responds to bronchodilators","E":"Significant reversible airway obstruction is present, consistent with asthma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient demonstrates significant bronchodilator reversibility, which is characteristic of asthma. Her FEV₁ improved by 0.9 L (50% increase from baseline), and her FEV₁/FVC ratio improved from 50% to 67.5%. This substantial improvement indicates that much of her airway obstruction is due to reversible bronchospasm rather than fixed structural changes. In asthma, bronchodilators relieve smooth muscle constriction, improving airflow. The improvement in both FEV₁ (absolute expired volume) and FEV₁/FVC ratio (relative expiratory rate) confirms reduced airway obstruction. While she hasn't returned completely to normal (normal FEV₁/FVC ≥80%), the significant response supports asthma diagnosis and indicates bronchodilator therapy should be continued. In contrast, emphysema shows little bronchodilator response because obstruction is from destroyed alveolar walls and loss of elastic recoil, not reversible bronchospasm. The other options are incorrect. An FEV1/FVC of 50% is the low ratio Guyton describes with airway obstruction (normal 80%), not a restrictive pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Asthma is reversible obstruction — FEV1/FVC is low during an attack and FEV1 rises after a bronchodilator. FA: spirometry shows an obstructive pattern with bronchodilator response but may be normal between exacerbations; methacholine challenge can support the diagnosis. COMLEX: FA's mechanism — type I hypersensitivity, hyperresponsive bronchi and reversible bronchoconstriction with mucus plugging.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 1 — the drug classes, such as β2 agonists, used in asthma and COPD","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"While she hasn't returned comp","D":"An FEV1/FVC of 50% is the low ratio Guyton describes with ai"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q16_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Emphysema pathophysiology - hypoxia and hypercapnia","batch":1,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with 40-pack-year smoking history has chronic pulmonary emphysema. Pathological examination of his lung tissue would show which major structural changes?","choices":{"A":"Alveoli filled with inflammatory cells and fluid with intact alveolar walls","B":"Widespread bronchial dilation with hypertrophied smooth muscle and intact alveoli","C":"Thickened alveolar walls with increased collagen deposition and fibrosis throughout","D":"Destruction of 50-80% of alveolar walls with enlarged, coalesced air spaces","E":"Normal alveolar architecture with isolated areas of mucus plug obstruction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chronic pulmonary emphysema is characterized by marked destruction of alveolar walls, with loss of 50-80% of the walls in severe cases. This destruction results from chronic infection and inflammation (from smoking or pollution), which causes: (1) airway obstruction leading to air trapping and alveolar overstretching; (2) release of proteolytic enzymes from inflammatory cells that digest elastin and other structural proteins; (3) combination of mechanical stress and enzymatic degradation destroying alveolar septa. The result is enlarged air spaces where multiple alveoli have coalesced into larger, irregular spaces (sometimes called \"bullae\"). This dramatically reduces the surface area available for gas exchange and destroys pulmonary capillaries embedded in alveolar walls. Alveoli filled with inflammatory cells and fluid describes pneumonia. Bronchial dilation with hypertrophied muscle describes bronchiectasis or asthma. Thickened, fibrotic alveolar walls describes pulmonary fibrosis (restrictive disease, opposite of emphysema). Normal architecture with mucus plugs doesn't describe emphysema's widespread destruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Chronic infection, mucus and bronchiolar edema obstruct small airways, trapping and overstretching alveoli; with infection this destroys as much as 50–80% of alveolar walls, leaving large coalesced air spaces. COMLEX: FA — centriacinar emphysema (upper lobes) goes with smoking, panacinar (lower lobes) with α1-antitrypsin deficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — the four main types of emphysema and their morphology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q18_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Emphysema pathophysiology - hypoxia and hypercapnia","batch":1,"stem":"A 65-year-old woman with severe emphysema has arterial blood gases showing PO₂ of 55 mm Hg (hypoxemia) and PCO₂ of 58 mm Hg (hypercapnia). What pathophysiological mechanisms in emphysema cause BOTH hypoxia and hypercapnia to occur?","choices":{"A":"Increased metabolic rate raises O₂ consumption and CO₂ production beyond pulmonary capacity","B":"Alveolar wall destruction reduces diffusing capacity; V/Q mismatch creates shunt and dead space","C":"Pulmonary hypertension reduces blood flow through lungs, limiting gas exchange time","D":"Mucus hypersecretion blocks all airways equally, preventing both O₂ uptake and CO₂ elimination","E":"Loss of chemoreceptor sensitivity prevents appropriate ventilatory responses to hypoxia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Emphysema causes both hypoxemia and hypercapnia through multiple mechanisms: (1) Marked loss of alveolar walls (50-80% destruction) greatly decreases diffusing capacity, reducing the lung's ability to oxygenate blood and remove CO₂; (2) Extremely abnormal ventilation-perfusion ratios occur because obstruction is worse in some lung regions than others—some areas have very low V̇A/Q̇ (physiological shunt, poorly oxygenated blood) while other areas have very high V̇A/Q̇ (physiological dead space, wasted ventilation). This combination reduces both oxygenation and CO₂ elimination; (3) Bronchiolar obstruction and increased work of breathing may lead to overall hypoventilation of functional alveoli. The result is progressive, devastating hypoxia and hypercapnia that can last for years. Increased metabolic rate is incorrect—metabolism is usually normal or reduced. Pulmonary hypertension contributes but isn't primary. Uniform mucus obstruction and loss of chemoreceptor sensitivity are incorrect or incomplete.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's emphysema effects — bronchiolar obstruction raises work of breathing; loss of alveolar walls lowers diffusing capacity; very low V/Q (shunt) and very high V/Q (dead space) coexist; capillary loss raises pulmonary vascular resistance. Hypoxia and hypercapnia follow from hypoventilation of many alveoli plus wall loss. COMLEX: FA — emphysema lowers DLCO, a measurable marker of the lost membrane.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 13 — physiological shunt and dead space in COPD","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Uniform mucus obstruction and "}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q19_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Alveolar changes in emphysema vs pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist examines lung tissue from two patients: one with emphysema and one with bacterial pneumonia. What is the fundamental difference in alveolar pathology between these two conditions?","choices":{"A":"Emphysema shows alveolar wall thickening; pneumonia shows alveolar wall thinning","B":"Both conditions show identical alveolar pathology with different degrees of inflammation","C":"Emphysema shows fluid-filled alveoli; pneumonia shows air-filled but collapsed alveoli","D":"Emphysema shows intact alveoli with bronchial obstruction; pneumonia shows destroyed alveoli","E":"Emphysema destroys alveolar walls and enlarges air spaces; pneumonia fills intact alveoli"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Emphysema and pneumonia have fundamentally different alveolar pathologies: EMPHYSEMA is characterized by (1) destruction of alveolar walls with loss of 50-80% of walls in severe cases; (2) enlarged, coalesced air spaces where multiple alveoli have merged; (3) loss of pulmonary capillaries; (4) reduced surface area for gas exchange. The alveoli are overstretched and destroyed but still contain air. PNEUMONIA is characterized by (1) intact but inflamed alveolar walls; (2) alveoli filled with fluid, white blood cells, red blood cells, and bacteria; (3) progressive \"consolidation\" of lung tissue as infection spreads; (4) alveolar walls remain structurally present but are highly porous, leaking fluid. The key difference: emphysema destroys walls, pneumonia fills intact spaces. The other options incorrectly describe these pathologies. Emphysema destroys as much as 50% to 80% of alveolar walls rather than thickening them, and pneumonia fills alveoli with fluid and blood cells. The pathology differs: emphysema destroys alveolar walls, while pneumonia fills the alveoli with fluid and blood cells. The reverse is true: emphysema destroys alveolar walls, whereas pneumonia fills the alveoli with fluid and cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Emphysema destroys alveolar walls and enlarges air spaces; pneumonia fills intact alveoli with fluid and blood cells, consolidating whole lobes or a lung. COMLEX: FA — loss of alveolar walls raises compliance in emphysema, while pneumonia lowers compliance; both impair gas exchange by different routes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 5 — the four histologic stages of lobar pneumonia","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Emphysema destroys as much as 50% to 80% of alveolar walls r","B":"The pathology differs: emphysema destroys alveolar walls, wh","D":"The reverse is true: emphysema destroys alveolar walls, wher"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q20_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Alveolar changes in emphysema vs pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student studies lung histology slides from emphysema and pneumonia patients. In emphysema, what happens to the pulmonary capillaries that are normally embedded in alveolar walls?","choices":{"A":"Capillaries proliferate and increase in number to compensate for reduced gas exchange","B":"Capillaries remain intact but become dilated from pulmonary hypertension","C":"Capillaries are destroyed along with the alveolar walls, reducing pulmonary vascular beds","D":"Capillaries migrate into bronchial walls to maintain some gas exchange capacity","E":"Capillaries remain completely normal despite extensive alveolar destruction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When alveolar walls are destroyed in emphysema, the pulmonary capillaries embedded in those walls are also destroyed. Loss of large portions of alveolar walls decreases the number of pulmonary capillaries through which blood can pass. This has several important consequences: (1) reduced surface area for gas exchange; (2) increased pulmonary vascular resistance because there are fewer capillary channels for blood flow; (3) pulmonary hypertension develops from increased resistance; (4) right-sided heart strain and eventual right heart failure (cor pulmonale). The destruction of 50-80% of alveolar walls means loss of a similar proportion of pulmonary capillaries, dramatically reducing the lung's capacity for gas exchange and blood flow. The other options incorrectly describe capillary changes—they are destroyed, not proliferated, dilated, migrated, or preserved.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loss of alveolar walls also removes the pulmonary capillaries within them, so pulmonary vascular resistance rises markedly, causing pulmonary hypertension that overloads the right heart and often causes right-sided failure. COMLEX: FA — chronic hypoxic vasoconstriction adds to this, leading to pulmonary hypertension +/− cor pulmonale.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — pulmonary hypertension, its causes, mechanisms and classification","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The other options incorrectly ","B":"The other options incorrectly ","D":"The other options incorrectly ","E":"The other options incorrectly "}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q21_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Alveolar changes in emphysema vs pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A radiologist describes a chest X-ray showing bullous changes with large irregular air spaces in a patient with end-stage emphysema. What underlying structural change creates this appearance?","choices":{"A":"Mucus-filled bronchi creating air-fluid levels throughout the lungs","B":"Multiple abscesses with air-fluid interfaces scattered through lung tissue","C":"Coalescence of destroyed alveoli into large, irregular air spaces (bullae)","D":"Fluid-filled alveoli interspersed with normal air-filled alveoli","E":"Thickened interstitial septa creating a reticular pattern on imaging"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The bullous appearance of emphysematous lungs results from coalescence of multiple destroyed alveoli into large, irregular air spaces called bullae. As alveolar walls are progressively destroyed (50-80% loss in severe cases), adjacent alveoli merge into larger and larger spaces. Instead of normal tiny alveoli (about 0.2 mm in diameter, Guyton Ch 40), the lung contains enlarged, irregular air spaces (several millimeters to centimeters in diameter) with markedly reduced surface area for gas exchange. This creates the characteristic appearance on gross pathology and imaging. The destruction is irreversible—once alveolar walls are destroyed, they cannot regenerate. This explains why emphysema progressively worsens and why smoking cessation can only slow progression, not reverse existing damage. Honeycombing is the end-stage fibrotic pattern of interstitial lung disease (Robbins Ch 15), not emphysema. The other options describe different pathologies, not emphysema. Mucus-filled bronchi do not cause bullae; bullae come from coalescence of destroyed alveoli. Abscesses give air-fluid interfaces, whereas bullae are air-filled spaces formed by destroyed alveoli. Fluid-filled alveoli describe pneumonia, not the enlarged air spaces of bullous emphysema. Robbins defines emphysema by airspace enlargement with destruction of alveolar walls, not septal thickening; bullae are large subpleural air spaces.","boardPrep":"Step 1: As alveolar walls are destroyed, neighboring air spaces coalesce; Robbins notes apical blebs or bullae in advanced disease, and FA describes increased lucency with a flattened diaphragm and barrel chest. COMLEX: Robbins — subpleural blebs may rupture in emphysematous lungs, causing a fatal pneumothorax, so sudden dyspnea in emphysema demands a chest film.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 4 — other forms of emphysema, including bullous and focal emphysematous changes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Mucus-filled bronchi do not cause bullae; bullae come from c","B":"Abscesses give air-fluid interfaces, whereas bullae are air-","D":"Fluid-filled alveoli describe pneumonia, not the enlarged ai","E":"Robbins defines emphysema by airspace enlargement with destr"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q22_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Pulmonary abnormalities causing decreased O₂ saturation in pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with right lower lobe pneumonia has arterial oxygen saturation of 78% (normal 95-100%). Blood from his right pulmonary vein (draining the pneumonic lung) is 60% saturated, while blood from his left pulmonary vein (draining the healthy lung) is 97% saturated. What pulmonary abnormality primarily explains his reduced arterial oxygen saturation?","choices":{"A":"Pneumonic lung is perfused but poorly ventilated, creating low V/Q and venous admixture","B":"Increased metabolic rate from infection consumes oxygen faster than it can be delivered","C":"Reduced total lung volume from consolidation prevents adequate oxygen intake","D":"Inflammation destroys pulmonary capillaries, preventing blood from reaching functional alveoli","E":"Bacterial toxins directly poison hemoglobin, preventing normal oxygen binding"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The pneumonic lobe's alveoli are filled with exudate, so its ventilation falls while its blood flow continues: a low V/Q ratio. Blood leaving that lobe is poorly oxygenated (60% saturated here) and mixes with fully saturated blood from the healthy lung — venous admixture — lowering systemic arterial saturation (Guyton Ch 43). Pneumonia fills the alveoli but does not destroy the capillaries; if perfusion of the lobe stopped, its blood could not lower arterial saturation. Lung volume per se, a faster metabolic rate, and a toxin acting on hemoglobin do not explain a pulmonary-vein saturation of 60% on one side and 97% on the other.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In one-lung pneumonia the consolidated side keeps its blood flow but loses ventilation (low V/Q); Guyton's example — aerated lung blood 97% saturated, unaerated about 60%, so aortic saturation averages only about 78%. COMLEX: FA — V/Q mismatch and shunt raise the A-a gradient, whereas hypoventilation and low inspired O2 do not.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 12 — the ventilation-perfusion ratio and its effect on alveolar PO2 and PCO2","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Lung volume per se, a faster m"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q23_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Pulmonary abnormalities causing decreased O₂ saturation in pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with bilateral pneumonia involving both lower lobes has arterial oxygen saturation of 72%. Besides the decreased V/Q ratio, what other pulmonary abnormality contributes to her severe hypoxemia?","choices":{"A":"Increased diffusing capacity from inflammatory mediator effects on capillaries","B":"Decreased total available surface area of the respiratory membrane from consolidation","C":"Enhanced oxygen extraction by metabolically active inflammatory cells in alveoli","D":"Increased pulmonary blood flow compensating for reduced ventilation","E":"Hyperventilation of healthy lung regions completely compensating for diseased regions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Besides decreased V/Q ratio, pneumonia also reduces total available surface area of the respiratory membrane. As alveoli progressively fill with fluid, cells, and debris, those alveoli are no longer available for gas exchange—they are \"consolidated\" and non-functional. In bilateral lower lobe pneumonia, large areas of both lungs may be consolidated, dramatically reducing the total membrane surface area where oxygen can diffuse from air into blood. Pneumonia thus causes two major abnormalities: (1) reduced V/Q ratio in partially affected areas (ventilation reduced but perfusion continues); (2) reduced total respiratory membrane surface area in fully consolidated areas. Both effects contribute to hypoxemia. When significant portions of both lungs are involved (bilateral pneumonia), hypoxemia becomes severe, as in this patient with 72% saturation. The other options either don't occur or don't contribute to hypoxemia. Healthy regions cannot make up the difference, because blood through aerated lung is already about 97% saturated and cannot carry more O2, so the mixed aortic blood stays well below normal (about 78% in Guyton's example). Guyton says pneumonia reduces the total available surface area of the respiratory membrane, which lowers rather than raises diffusion. Oxygen use by inflammatory cells is not a contributor Guyton names; he cites reduced membrane surface area and a low V/Q ratio.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton names two pneumonia abnormalities — reduced total surface area of the respiratory membrane and a decreased ventilation-perfusion ratio; both cause hypoxemia and hypercapnia. COMLEX: Bilateral consolidation multiplies both effects, which is why multilobar pneumonia carries a higher risk of respiratory failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 7 — disease states that reduce gas diffusion efficiency","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Guyton says pneumonia reduces the total available surface ar","C":"Oxygen use by inflammatory cells is not a contributor Guyton"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q24_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Pulmonary abnormalities causing decreased O₂ saturation in pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with lobar pneumonia is treated with appropriate antibiotics and supportive care. As the infection resolves and inflammatory fluid is reabsorbed from the alveoli, what happens to the arterial oxygen saturation?","choices":{"A":"Saturation remains permanently reduced because pneumonia causes irreversible alveolar wall destruction","B":"Saturation decreases further as healing fibrosis reduces lung compliance","C":"Saturation fluctuates unpredictably depending on bacterial toxin levels in blood","D":"Saturation shows no change because capillary damage from inflammation is permanent","E":"Saturation gradually improves as alveolar ventilation is restored and V/Q ratio normalizes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In pneumonia the alveolar walls remain intact; the alveoli are filled with exudate rather than destroyed. As the infection resolves and the fluid is reabsorbed, ventilation returns to the affected alveoli, the low V/Q regions and venous admixture disappear, and arterial saturation gradually improves (Guyton Ch 43). Permanent wall or capillary destruction is the picture of emphysema, not lobar pneumonia, and healing pneumonia does not usually leave fibrosis that worsens saturation. Saturation follows the gas-exchange abnormality, not circulating bacterial toxin levels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pneumonia fills alveoli with fluid and cells but does not destroy their walls (Guyton Fig 43.5 contrasts it with emphysema), so as the exudate clears, ventilation, V/Q and saturation recover. COMLEX: The pulmonary OMM lab lists O2 when indicated, antibiotics and OMT (rib raising, lymphatic pump, diaphragm release) as adjuncts while the lung recovers; MOPSE found shorter hospital stay with OMT per protocol.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 5 — the four classic stages of lobar pneumonia, ending in resolution","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Permanent wall or capillary de","D":"Permanent wall or capillary de"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q25_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Pulmonary abnormalities causing decreased O₂ saturation in pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A physician explains to a patient with pneumonia why supplemental oxygen therapy may help improve oxygen saturation. Which statement best explains why oxygen therapy can be beneficial in pneumonia?","choices":{"A":"Oxygen therapy directly kills bacteria, accelerating resolution of infection","B":"High inspired oxygen increases alveolar PO₂, improving oxygen gradient even in poorly ventilated regions","C":"Oxygen therapy reverses alveolar consolidation by drying inflammatory fluid","D":"Supplemental oxygen has no benefit in pneumonia because the problem is fluid-filled alveoli, not low inspired oxygen","E":"Oxygen directly restores normal V/Q ratios by dilating airways and removing fluid"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Oxygen therapy benefits pneumonia patients by increasing the alveolar PO₂ in regions that still have some ventilation, improving the diffusion gradient for oxygen into blood. Even in poorly ventilated alveoli (those partially filled with fluid), increasing inspired oxygen concentration can raise alveolar PO₂ enough to improve oxygenation of blood perfusing those regions. By breathing higher oxygen concentrations (30-100% O₂ instead of room air's 21%), the patient can partially compensate for reduced V/Q ratios. However, oxygen therapy doesn't address the underlying problem (fluid-filled alveoli) and won't help regions with complete consolidation (zero ventilation, V/Q = 0). It's a supportive measure while antibiotics treat the infection. Antibiotics address the cause; oxygen provides symptomatic support. Bacterial killing is incorrect—oxygen doesn't kill bacteria. Drying the consolidation, no benefit, and direct restoration of V/Q are incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Supplemental O2 raises alveolar PO2 wherever some ventilation remains; in diffusion-type hypoxia it can raise alveolar PO2 from about 100 toward 600 mm Hg, increasing the diffusion gradient by more than 800% (Guyton's pulmonary-edema example shows O2 uptake 3–4 times faster). COMLEX: FA — true shunt (V/Q = 0) does not correct with 100% O2, so persistent hypoxemia on high-flow O2 signals shunt physiology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 12 — the value of oxygen therapy in different types of hypoxia","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"However, oxygen therapy doesn'"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q26_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — PO₂, PCO₂, and pH as fundamental measures of pulmonary performance","batch":1,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with COPD has arterial blood gases measured using a blood gas analyzer. The PCO₂ measurement relies on a pH electrode surrounded by sodium bicarbonate solution separated from the blood sample by a thin plastic membrane. What principle allows this system to measure PCO₂?","choices":{"A":"Oxygen molecules displace CO₂ in the bicarbonate solution, creating measurable current flow","B":"CO₂ molecules are too large to cross the membrane, creating a concentration gradient","C":"CO₂ diffuses into the bicarbonate solution, and the pH change is converted to PCO₂","D":"The plastic membrane generates voltage proportional to CO₂ concentration","E":"CO₂ binds to hemoglobin in the bicarbonate solution, changing its optical density"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The PCO₂ electrode uses a clever indirect measurement technique: CO₂ from the blood sample diffuses through a thin plastic membrane (which is permeable to CO₂) into a sodium bicarbonate solution that surrounds a standard glass pH electrode. When CO₂ enters the bicarbonate solution, it forms carbonic acid (H₂CO₃), which dissociates into H⁺ and HCO₃⁻, changing the pH of the bicarbonate solution. The pH electrode measures this pH change, and the analyzer uses the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation to mathematically convert the pH change to the corresponding PCO₂ of the blood sample. This is why it's called a \"Severinghaus electrode\" or \"bicarbonate-pH electrode system.\" Oxygen displacing CO₂ is incorrect because oxygen doesn't displace CO₂ in this measurement. CO₂ too large to cross is wrong because CO₂ must cross the membrane for measurement. Membrane-generated voltage incorrectly describes how the membrane works. Hemoglobin binding is incorrect because there's no hemoglobin in the bicarbonate solution.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The PCO2 electrode is a glass pH electrode bathed in weak sodium bicarbonate behind a CO2-permeable membrane; CO2 diffusing in changes the pH, and PCO2 is calculated from the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation, pH = 6.1 + log(HCO3−/0.03 × PCO2). COMLEX: The same equation (FA) links ABG values — knowing any two of pH, PCO2 and HCO3− defines the third.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 10 — CO2 transport through carbonic anhydrase and bicarbonate","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q27_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — PO₂, PCO₂, and pH as fundamental measures of pulmonary performance","batch":1,"stem":"A critical care physician receives blood gas results showing PO₂ of 48 mm Hg, PCO₂ of 72 mm Hg, and pH of 7.18 on a mechanically ventilated patient. The laboratory reports these values were obtained within 90 seconds from a single small blood sample. What makes rapid simultaneous measurement of these three parameters possible at the bedside?","choices":{"A":"All three electrodes use the same basic glass electrode technology with different membranes","B":"Modern analyzers use optical spectroscopy that measures all parameters instantaneously","C":"The pH, CO₂ (bicarbonate-pH) and O₂ (platinum) electrodes can be miniaturized together","D":"Capillary action separates the blood into three chambers for sequential measurement","E":"The measurements are actually performed on expired air rather than blood"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Rapid simultaneous measurement of PO₂, PCO₂, and pH is possible because the three different electrode systems can be miniaturized and positioned adjacent to each other in a single analyzer unit that requires only drops of blood. The pH electrode (glass electrode), PCO₂ electrode (bicarbonate-pH system), and PO₂ electrode (platinum polarography) are separate technologies but can be housed together, allowing simultaneous measurements within a minute or so (Guyton Ch 43) from a single small sample. This rapid turnaround time makes blood gas analysis practical for moment-by-moment monitoring at bedside, crucial for managing critically ill patients. This patient shows severe respiratory failure with marked hypoxemia, severe hypercapnia, and uncompensated respiratory acidosis, requiring immediate ventilator adjustment. Identical glass electrodes is incorrect because they use different technologies. Optical spectroscopy is wrong—these analyzers use electrodes, not optical spectroscopy. Capillary separation incorrectly describes the sampling process. Measurement on expired air is wrong—arterial blood must be measured directly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton notes the pH, CO2 and O2 electrodes can be miniaturized and combined, so blood gases are measured within minutes using no more than a few drops of blood. COMLEX: FA — in a ventilated patient with pH < 7.35 and PCO2 > 44, the respiratory acidosis points to inadequate ventilation, guiding ventilator changes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 14 — hypoxia and hypercapnia, together or independently","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q28_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — PO₂, PCO₂, and pH as fundamental measures of pulmonary performance","batch":1,"stem":"A respiratory therapist draws arterial blood for analysis on a patient with acute pulmonary embolism. The PO₂ measurement uses a platinum electrode with a negative voltage separated from blood by an oxygen-permeable membrane. What physical process allows this electrode to measure oxygen tension?","choices":{"A":"Oxygen molecules absorb light at specific wavelengths, creating measurable optical changes","B":"Oxygen is reduced at the platinum electrode, producing a current proportional to PO₂","C":"Oxygen binds to chemicals on the membrane surface, generating voltage","D":"The membrane expands when exposed to oxygen, changing electrical capacitance","E":"Oxygen molecules carry positive charge that attracts electrons from the platinum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The PO₂ electrode uses polarography (Clark electrode): a platinum cathode with negative voltage (about -0.6 volts) is covered by an oxygen-permeable plastic membrane. Oxygen from the blood diffuses through the membrane to reach the platinum electrode surface, where it undergoes reduction by accepting electrons from the negatively charged platinum (O₂ + 4e⁻ → 2O²⁻). This electron acceptance creates a current flow that is directly proportional to the PO₂—more oxygen in the blood means more O₂ molecules reach the electrode, more electrons are accepted, and greater current flows. An ammeter measures this current and converts it to mm Hg of oxygen pressure. This direct electrochemical measurement makes polarography highly accurate and responsive. Light absorption describes optical methods not used in standard blood gas analyzers. Membrane chemical binding and membrane expansion describe mechanisms that don't occur. Positively charged oxygen incorrectly describes oxygen's electrical properties—oxygen molecules are electrically neutral.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Polarography — when a platinum electrode is held more than −0.6 volt from the solution, O2 deposits on it, and the current is directly proportional to O2 concentration and therefore PO2. COMLEX: FA lists pulmonary embolism among causes of hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis, so PE often gives a low PaO2 with a low PaCO2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — the pathophysiology of pulmonary embolism","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Light absorption describes opt","C":"Membrane chemical binding and ","D":"Membrane chemical binding and "}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q29_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Principle of maximum expiratory flow","batch":1,"stem":"A healthy 25-year-old man inhales to total lung capacity and then expires with maximal effort into a flow-volume recorder. According to Guyton, approximately what maximum expiratory airflow does a healthy person reach quickly in this maneuver, a value that greater effort cannot raise?","choices":{"A":"Near 40 L/min","B":"Near 100 L/min","C":"Over 400 L/min","D":"Near 2,000 L/min","E":"Near 6 L/min"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton's maximum expiratory flow-volume curve shows a healthy person quickly reaching a maximum expiratory airflow of more than 400 L/min after a maximal inspiration; regardless of additional expiratory effort, this remains the maximum because the same pressure that compresses the alveoli also compresses and narrows the bronchioles, so resistance rises as much as driving pressure. The flow then declines as lung volume falls. A flow near 6 L/min is the resting minute ventilation, not an instantaneous expiratory flow. Flows near 40 and 100 L/min fall well below the normal peak and are values seen in obstructed or constricted lungs, not in health. A flow near 2,000 L/min is several times higher than Guyton's figure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: After a maximal inspiration a healthy person quickly reaches a maximum expiratory airflow of more than 400 L/min; regardless of additional effort, flow at each lung volume cannot exceed this maximum — the principle of effort-independent expiratory flow. COMLEX: FA — obstruction lowers FEV1 more than FVC, so the early, effort-limited part of the forced expiration is what falls first.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — the mechanics of inspiration and expiration and the pressures that drive airflow","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q30_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Principle of maximum expiratory flow","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist analyzes flow-volume curves from a healthy 30-year-old man who performed maximum forced expiration from TLC. The curve shows peak expiratory flow of 500 L/min at high lung volumes, but this decreases to 200 L/min when only 20% of vital capacity remains. The patient claims he maintained maximum effort throughout the maneuver. Why does maximum expiratory flow decrease progressively as lung volume falls?","choices":{"A":"CO₂ accumulation in remaining alveolar air reduces the diffusion gradient for expiration","B":"Respiratory muscle force decreases as the chest wall reaches its minimum volume configuration","C":"Mucus progressively accumulates in smaller airways, increasing resistance","D":"Decreased alveolar surface area at low volumes reduces gas exchange efficiency","E":"Loss of elastic radial traction on airways at low volumes makes them collapse more easily"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Maximum expiratory flow decreases as lung volume decreases primarily because of changes in elastic radial traction on airways. At high lung volumes (near TLC), the lung parenchyma is stretched, creating outward radial forces that pull on the bronchial and bronchiolar walls, helping keep them open (elastic tethering). This structural support resists dynamic compression during forced expiration. As lung volume decreases during expiration, the lung parenchyma relaxes, reducing these radial traction forces. With diminished elastic support, the airways collapse more easily when subjected to external compression from positive pleural pressure, progressively limiting maximum flow. By the time lung volume approaches residual volume, radial traction is minimal and airways are highly susceptible to collapse, explaining the marked reduction in maximum flow. This volume-dependence of flow creates the characteristic shape of the maximum expiratory flow-volume curve. The other options either don't occur or aren't the primary mechanism for progressive flow reduction. Guyton attributes the fall to bronchi collapsing more easily as the lung shrinks, not to alveolar CO2. Guyton attributes the fall at low volumes to bronchi and bronchioles collapsing more easily as their elastic pull relaxes, not to loss of muscle force. In a healthy man the fall is not from mucus; Guyton attributes it to airways collapsing more easily as the lung shrinks. Gas exchange efficiency does not determine expiratory flow, which is limited by airway collapse.","boardPrep":"Step 1: At high lung volumes the airways are held open by elastic pull of surrounding lung tissue; as volume falls this support relaxes, the airways collapse more easily, and maximum expiratory flow declines even with maximal effort. COMLEX: FA — emphysema raises compliance and lowers elastic recoil, so the same support is lost at every volume and the loop shifts left.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — the mechanics of expiration and the pressures that set airway caliber","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Guyton attributes the fall to bronchi collapsing more easily","B":"Guyton attributes the fall at low volumes to bronchi and bro","C":"In a healthy man the fall is not from mucus; Guyton attribut","D":"Gas exchange efficiency does not determine expiratory flow, "}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q31_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Principle of maximum expiratory flow","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with emphysema performs maximum forced expiration. His peak expiratory flow reaches only 180 L/min compared to predicted 450 L/min, and his flow rate decreases very rapidly as lung volume falls. His physician explains that his airways collapse more readily than normal during expiration. What pathophysiological change in emphysema increases susceptibility to dynamic airway compression?","choices":{"A":"Destruction of alveolar attachments reduces elastic radial traction supporting airways","B":"Bronchial smooth muscle hypertrophy increases baseline airway resistance","C":"Mucus hypersecretion directly obstructs the airways, reducing their cross-sectional area","D":"Inflammation of airway walls increases their thickness, making them more rigid","E":"Decreased surfactant production increases surface tension in small airways"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In emphysema, destruction of alveolar walls eliminates the normal elastic attachments between alveoli and airways. These attachments normally provide radial traction—outward pulling forces that help keep airways open, particularly during expiration. Loss of these structural connections reduces the mechanical support for airways, making them much more susceptible to dynamic compression during forced expiration. Even at relatively high lung volumes where normal individuals maintain good airway patency, emphysema patients experience premature airway collapse because of inadequate radial traction. This explains why emphysema patients have markedly reduced maximum expiratory flow rates and why their flow-volume curves show very rapid decline in flow as volume decreases. Additionally, the loss of elastic recoil means lower driving pressures for expiration while airways are simultaneously more prone to collapse—a double problem. Smooth muscle hypertrophy and mucus hypersecretion describe features of chronic bronchitis, not emphysema's primary mechanism. Wall inflammation and rigidity incorrectly suggests increased rigidity (emphysema airways are actually more collapsible). Decreased surfactant incorrectly describes surfactant's role.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Emphysema destroys the alveolar walls that tether small airways, removing their elastic pull, so bronchioles collapse readily under expiratory chest pressure. FA: elastase–antiprotease imbalance destroys elastic fibers; loss of recoil and ↑ compliance follow. COMLEX: FA — α1-antitrypsin deficiency causes panacinar emphysema of the lower lobes in younger patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — the types of emphysema and their pathogenesis","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Wall inflammation and rigidity"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q32_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Obstructive vs restrictive disease on Maximum Expiratory Flow-Volume Curve","batch":1,"stem":"Guyton describes a constricted maximum expiratory flow-volume curve, with reduced total lung capacity, reduced residual volume, and a peak flow that cannot reach normal. Some patients have this pattern from lung fibrosis, but it also arises from disease outside the lung. Which patient would most likely show this pattern?","choices":{"A":"A man with chronic asthma and air trapping","B":"A woman with panacinar emphysema from α1-AT loss","C":"A smoker with chronic bronchitis and mucus plugs","D":"A woman with severe thoracic kyphoscoliosis","E":"A man with bronchiectasis and copious sputum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guyton lists the constricted-lung diseases as fibrotic lung diseases such as tuberculosis and silicosis and diseases that constrict the chest cage, such as kyphosis, scoliosis, and fibrotic pleurisy; because the lung cannot expand to a normal maximum volume, TLC and RV are reduced and maximal expiratory flow cannot rise to normal. Severe kyphoscoliosis restricts the chest cage and produces this pattern even with normal lung tissue. Chronic asthma, panacinar emphysema, chronic bronchitis with mucus plugging, and bronchiectasis are airway-obstruction diseases, in which air enters easily but is trapped, so TLC and RV rise and maximum expiratory flow is greatly reduced.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's constricted lung diseases — fibrotic lung disease (tuberculosis, silicosis) and chest-cage constriction (kyphosis, scoliosis, fibrotic pleurisy) — reduce TLC and RV and keep peak flow below normal. COMLEX: FA classes chest-wall restriction (scoliosis, severe obesity) as extrapulmonary with normal DLCO and A-a gradient; the pulmonary OMM lab aims OMT at thoracic cage and diaphragm mechanics to lower the work of breathing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — the chest-wall structures and mechanics that expand the lungs in inspiration","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Chronic asthma, panacinar emph"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q33_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Obstructive vs restrictive disease on Maximum Expiratory Flow-Volume Curve","batch":1,"stem":"Over several years, a 50-year-old woman with poorly controlled asthma shows a progressive rise in both total lung capacity and residual volume on serial testing. Guyton explains this in terms of how airway caliber changes between the two phases of breathing. Which explanation best fits?","choices":{"A":"Inspiration pulls airways open, but expiration closes them and traps air","B":"Both phases narrow the airways equally and trap air throughout","C":"Fibrosis stiffens the lungs and holds them at a higher resting volume","D":"Alveolar walls thicken and add tissue volume to the measured TLC","E":"Weak inspiratory muscles leave more air in the lungs after each breath"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton explains that with airway obstruction it is much more difficult to expire than to inspire: the extra positive chest pressure required for expiration greatly increases the closing tendency of the airways, whereas the extra negative pleural pressure of inspiration pulls the airways open while it expands the alveoli. Air therefore enters easily but becomes trapped, and over months or years this increases both TLC and RV; in asthma the chest may become permanently enlarged (barrel chest). Equal narrowing in both phases would not explain why air enters easily but cannot leave. Fibrosis lowers TLC and RV, producing a constricted curve. Thickened alveolar walls reduce diffusion, not gas volume. Weak inspiratory muscles would limit how much air enters, lowering TLC rather than raising it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In airway obstruction expiration is much harder than inspiration — expiratory positive pressure adds to airway closure, while inspiratory negative pleural pressure pulls airways open — so air enters easily, becomes trapped, and over months or years TLC and RV rise. COMLEX: Robbins notes some patients with otherwise typical asthma develop an irreversible component, which is why long-standing asthma can come to resemble COPD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 9 — clinical differentiation of the obstructive lung diseases","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Fibrosis lowers TLC and RV, pr"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q34_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Obstructive vs restrictive disease on Maximum Expiratory Flow-Volume Curve","batch":1,"stem":"Two patients perform maximum forced expiration maneuvers. Patient A (with emphysema) shows TLC of 8.5 L, RV of 5.0 L, and VC of 3.5 L. Patient B (with pulmonary fibrosis) shows TLC of 3.8 L, RV of 0.9 L, and VC of 2.9 L. Although Patient A has 5.0 L of air remaining at RV while Patient B has only 0.9 L remaining, why can't Patient A expire this \"extra\" 4.1 L of air?","choices":{"A":"The extra air is located in non-ventilated bullae that don't communicate with airways","B":"Patient A's respiratory muscles are too weak to generate additional expiratory force","C":"The extra air represents increased blood volume in pulmonary capillaries, not actual gas","D":"Patient A's airways collapse during expiration, trapping air distally before reaching RV","E":"Patient A's alveoli are completely rigid and cannot compress further"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In obstructive disease like emphysema, the markedly elevated RV (5.0 vs 0.9 L in the other patient, 1.5 L normal) represents air trapping from premature airway closure during expiration. As Patient A expires, two pathological processes cause airways to close before the lungs can empty completely: (1) loss of elastic radial traction from destroyed alveolar attachments makes airways collapse more easily; (2) loss of elastic recoil reduces driving pressure for expiration. Airways collapse while significant air remains trapped distally in alveoli. This trapped air is ventilated and participates in gas exchange during the breathing cycle, but cannot be fully expelled even with maximum expiratory effort. This is why obstructive disease increases RV—it's not that the lungs have larger capacity, but rather that airways close prematurely, preventing complete emptying. The \"extra\" 4.1 L is trapped behind closed airways. Non-communicating bullae might describe some air in bullae but doesn't explain most of the RV increase. Weak respiratory muscles is incorrect—muscle strength isn't the limiting factor. Increased capillary blood volume incorrectly identifies the trapped air. Rigid alveoli is wrong—emphysematous alveoli are actually more compliant (less elastic), not rigid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In obstruction, airways close during expiration before the alveoli empty, trapping air so RV and TLC rise. FA: RV and any capacity that contains it (FRC, TLC) cannot be measured by spirometry. COMLEX: Guyton measures FRC indirectly by helium dilution, from which RV and TLC are then calculated — the way air trapping is quantified.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 11 — spirometry's limits and the helium dilution method for FRC","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q35_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — FEV1, FVC, and FEV1/FVC ratio with airway obstruction","batch":1,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with chronic dyspnea performs spirometry. Her results show FEV1 of 1.8 L (predicted 3.0 L), FVC of 3.0 L (predicted 3.8 L), giving an FEV1/FVC ratio of 60% (predicted 80%). What pathophysiological process best explains the reduced FEV1/FVC ratio?","choices":{"A":"Reduced lung compliance prevents full inspiration, limiting both FEV1 and FVC equally","B":"Weakness of inspiratory muscles reduces the driving force for expiration","C":"Narrowed or collapsing airways limit expiratory flow more than volume","D":"Loss of diffusing capacity reduces oxygen uptake, limiting expiratory effort","E":"Increased elastic recoil causes rapid lung emptying, reducing measurable time"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An FEV1/FVC ratio of 60% (normal ~80%, abnormal <70%) indicates obstructive lung disease. This pattern occurs because airway obstruction or premature airway collapse disproportionately affects the rate of expiration (FEV1) more than the total volume that can eventually be expired (FVC). In the first second of forced expiration, flow is limited by narrowed or collapsed airways, so only 1.8 L can be expelled (reduced FEV1). Given more time and continued effort, the patient can eventually expire a total of 3.0 L (FVC), but this takes much longer than normal. The key pathophysiology: obstructive diseases create a bottleneck that slows expiratory flow. This might result from: (1) structural airway narrowing (chronic bronchitis, asthma), (2) loss of elastic radial traction allowing airway collapse (emphysema), or (3) mucus plugging. All cause disproportionate reduction in FEV1 compared to FVC, lowering the ratio. Reduced compliance describes restrictive disease which maintains normal or increased ratio. Inspiratory muscle weakness doesn't specifically affect the ratio. Loss of diffusing capacity and increased elastic recoil don't explain the pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Obstruction slows the rate of emptying more than the total volume emptied, so FEV1 falls more than FVC and the ratio drops (Guyton: normal about 80%; FA: normal > 70%). COMLEX: FA lists COPD (chronic bronchitis, emphysema), asthma and bronchiectasis as the obstructive diseases to sort with this ratio.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 9 — clinical differentiation of obstructive lung diseases","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Reduced compliance describes r","B":"Inspiratory muscle weakness do"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q36_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — FEV1, FVC, and FEV1/FVC ratio with airway obstruction","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with chronic asthma performs spirometry before and after bronchodilator administration. Pre-treatment: FEV1 2.0 L, FVC 4.0 L, ratio 50%. Post-treatment: FEV1 2.8 L, FVC 4.2 L, ratio 67%. What explains the greater improvement in FEV1 than FVC after bronchodilator?","choices":{"A":"Bronchodilators increase inspiratory capacity more than expiratory capacity","B":"Bronchodilators lower airway resistance, most improving early expiratory flow","C":"Bronchodilators primarily affect alveolar compliance rather than airway caliber","D":"The medication corrects respiratory muscle weakness, enhancing force generation","E":"Bronchodilators increase elastic recoil, proportionally improving all lung volumes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient shows classic reversible airway obstruction (asthma). The bronchodilator causes greater improvement in FEV1 (from 2.0 to 2.8 L, +0.8 L, +40%) than in FVC (from 4.0 to 4.2 L, +0.2 L, +5%), resulting in improved FEV1/FVC ratio (from 50% to 67%). This differential response occurs because bronchodilators work by relaxing airway smooth muscle, increasing airway diameter, and reducing airway resistance. The reduction in airway resistance particularly improves the rate of air movement during the early, rapid phase of forced expiration—when flow is most limited by airway caliber. With wider airways, more air can escape in the first second (increased FEV1). The FVC also improves slightly because reduced air trapping allows more complete lung emptying, but this effect is smaller than the flow rate improvement. The pattern confirms reversible airway obstruction, distinguishing asthma from fixed obstruction like emphysema. The other options incorrectly describe bronchodilator mechanisms. Bronchodilators relax airway smooth muscle, lowering airway resistance; they do not work by raising inspiratory capacity. Bronchodilators relax airway smooth muscle; they do not act on alveolar compliance. Bronchodilators relax airway smooth muscle; they do not correct respiratory muscle weakness. Bronchodilators relax airway smooth muscle rather than raising elastic recoil.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A bronchodilator relaxes bronchiolar smooth muscle, lowering airway resistance and raising early expiratory flow (FEV1) more than total exhaled volume (FVC), so the ratio improves. FA: asthma spirometry shows an obstructive pattern with bronchodilator response. COMLEX: Robbins separates asthma from chronic bronchitis and emphysema by reversible bronchospasm, while noting overlap — some COPD has a reversible component.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — the therapeutic rationale for each pharmacologic class in asthma and COPD","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Bronchodilators relax airway smooth muscle, lowering airway ","C":"Bronchodilators relax airway smooth muscle; they do not act ","D":"Bronchodilators relax airway smooth muscle; they do not corr","E":"Bronchodilators relax airway smooth muscle rather than raisi"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q37_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — FEV1, FVC, and FEV1/FVC ratio with airway obstruction","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist evaluates spirometry from three patients. Patient A: FEV1 2.5 L, FVC 3.0 L, ratio 83%. Patient B: FEV1 2.5 L, FVC 4.5 L, ratio 56%. Patient C: FEV1 2.0 L, FVC 2.3 L, ratio 87%. All three have reduced FEV1 compared to predicted (3.5 L). Which patient(s) have obstructive disease?","choices":{"A":"Only Patient A","B":"Only Patient B","C":"Only Patient C","D":"Patients A and C","E":"Patients B and C"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The FEV1/FVC ratio is the key discriminator between obstructive and restrictive patterns. Normal ratio is approximately 80% (Guyton Ch 43), and a ratio below 0.7 generally indicates obstruction (Robbins Ch 15). Patient A has FEV1 2.5 L, FVC 3.0 L, ratio 83%—this is a restrictive pattern (both FEV1 and FVC reduced proportionally, ratio normal/high). Patient B has FEV1 2.5 L, FVC 4.5 L, ratio 56%—this is obstructive disease (FEV1 disproportionately reduced compared to FVC, ratio <70%). Patient C has FEV1 2.0 L, FVC 2.3 L, ratio 87%—this is also restrictive (both reduced proportionally, ratio elevated). The crucial concept: in obstruction, airways limit flow rate more than volume, so FEV1 falls more than FVC, decreasing the ratio. In restriction, lung expansion is limited, reducing all volumes proportionally, so the ratio remains normal or even increases (because elastic recoil may be increased, causing faster emptying). Therefore, only Patient B has obstruction. Note that reduced FEV1 alone doesn't diagnose obstruction—the ratio is essential. The other options incorrectly identify the obstructive pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The FEV1/FVC ratio, not FEV1 alone, separates the patterns — a low ratio means obstruction; a normal or high ratio with low volumes means restriction. Guyton gives about 80% as normal; FA calls > 70% normal. COMLEX: FA's table — obstruction: FEV1 ↓↓, FVC ↓, ratio ↓; restriction: FEV1 ↓, FVC ↓, ratio normal or ↑.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — the obstructive pulmonary diseases and their features","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Patient A has FEV1 2.5 L, FVC ","C":"Patient C has FEV1 2.0 L, FVC ","D":"Patient C has FEV1 2.0 L, FVC ","E":"Patient C has FEV1 2.0 L, FVC "}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q38_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Emphysema pathophysiology - hypoxia and hypercapnia","batch":1,"stem":"A 65-year-old man with severe emphysema has arterial blood gases showing PO₂ of 52 mm Hg and PCO₂ of 58 mm Hg. Chest CT demonstrates destruction of 60% of alveolar walls with formation of large emphysematous spaces. Which pathophysiological change from alveolar wall destruction most directly causes his hypoxemia?","choices":{"A":"Complete obstruction of pulmonary arterioles prevents blood flow to remaining functional alveoli","B":"Reduced alveolar surface area decreases the respiratory membrane available for O₂ diffusion","C":"Destruction of pulmonary capillaries eliminates the pathway for oxygen delivery to tissues","D":"Increased dead space ventilation prevents any oxygen from reaching alveolar spaces","E":"Loss of type II pneumocytes eliminates surfactant production, causing alveolar collapse"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Alveolar wall destruction in emphysema (typically 50-80% of walls in severe disease) directly reduces the respiratory membrane surface area available for gas exchange. Normal lungs have approximately 70 square meters of alveolar-capillary surface area; in emphysema it is often decreased as much as 5-fold (Guyton Ch 40). Even though remaining alveoli may be adequately ventilated and perfused, the total oxygen diffusion capacity is limited by reduced surface area. Additionally, the walls contain the pulmonary capillaries, so their destruction also reduces the capillary bed for gas exchange. This creates ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch: some areas have adequate ventilation but reduced perfusion (high V/Q, dead space), while other areas have adequate perfusion but reduced ventilation (low V/Q, physiological shunt). The combination of reduced membrane surface area and V/Q mismatch causes hypoxemia. Complete arteriolar obstruction is incorrect—arterioles aren't completely obstructed. Capillary destruction eliminating delivery overstates the problem. Dead space preventing any O₂ entry is wrong—oxygen does reach alveoli. Loss of type II pneumocytes incorrectly describes emphysema pathology (surfactant isn't the primary issue).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loss of alveolar walls greatly decreases the lung's diffusing capacity, reducing its ability to oxygenate blood and remove CO2. FA: emphysema lowers DLCO (Fick: area ↓). COMLEX: Robbins' emphysema-dominant 'pink puffer' has low diffusion capacity yet relatively normal resting blood gases, with severe overdistention and prolonged expiration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 9 — diffusing capacity, its definition and calculation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q39_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Emphysema pathophysiology - hypoxia and hypercapnia","batch":1,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with emphysema develops worsening hypercapnia with PCO₂ rising from 48 to 62 mm Hg over three months. Her respiratory rate is 16 breaths/min (normal 12-20), and tidal volume is normal. Despite apparently adequate minute ventilation, why is her CO₂ elimination impaired?","choices":{"A":"Her alveoli are completely collapsed and not participating in ventilation","B":"CO₂ diffuses too slowly across thickened alveolar walls to reach expired air","C":"Increased dead space ventilation and V/Q mismatch reduce alveolar ventilation effectiveness","D":"Bronchospasm prevents CO₂-rich air from exiting the lungs during expiration","E":"Her respiratory rate is too low to eliminate the increased CO₂ production from infection"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hypercapnia in emphysema results from increased physiological dead space and ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch, not simply from reduced minute ventilation. Alveolar wall destruction creates several problems for CO₂ elimination: (1) Some areas develop high V/Q ratios (ventilated but poorly perfused) functioning as dead space—ventilation to these regions doesn't eliminate CO₂ effectively because little blood delivers CO₂ there. (2) Destruction reduces capillary surface area for CO₂ exchange. (3) Some regions develop low V/Q ratios where perfusion exceeds ventilation, allowing CO₂-rich blood to pass without adequate CO₂ removal. Although her minute ventilation appears adequate, much of it is \"wasted\" ventilating dead space, so alveolar ventilation (the portion that actually exchanges with capillary blood) is inadequate. As emphysema progresses, dead space fraction increases, progressively impairing CO₂ elimination and causing hypercapnia. Collapsed alveoli is wrong—alveoli aren't collapsed, they're enlarged. Slow diffusion across thickened walls incorrectly describes the pathology (walls are destroyed, not thickened). Bronchospasm describes asthma, not emphysema's primary problem. A low respiratory rate assumes inadequate respiratory rate without evidence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Emphysema creates very high V/Q regions (physiological dead space, wasted ventilation) alongside very low V/Q regions (shunt), so normal minute ventilation can still deliver too little effective alveolar ventilation. FA: alveolar ventilation = (VT − VD) × RR. COMLEX: Rising dead space raises the work of breathing needed just to hold PaCO2 steady.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 13 — physiological shunt versus physiological dead space","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q40_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Emphysema pathophysiology - hypoxia and hypercapnia","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student asks why patients with emphysema develop both hypoxemia and hypercapnia, while some other pulmonary diseases cause only hypoxemia. What property of oxygen and carbon dioxide transport explains why CO₂ retention indicates more severe disease?","choices":{"A":"Oxygen diffuses more rapidly than CO₂ across alveolar membranes, so CO₂ is affected first","B":"CO₂ is about 20 times more diffusible than O₂, so retention needs more severe impairment","C":"Oxygen transport depends on hemoglobin while CO₂ dissolves freely in plasma","D":"Carbon dioxide has higher molecular weight, making it harder to eliminate","E":"Oxygen and CO₂ are equally affected; patients just perceive hypoxemia symptoms sooner"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Carbon dioxide is approximately 20 times more diffusible across biological membranes than oxygen due to its much higher solubility in tissue fluids and membranes. This means that even when respiratory membrane surface area is reduced or V/Q mismatching occurs, CO₂ can still diffuse adequately and be eliminated, while O₂ transfer becomes impaired. Therefore, hypoxemia typically appears before hypercapnia in progressive pulmonary disease. When hypercapnia develops, it indicates that pulmonary impairment has become so severe that even the highly diffusible CO₂ cannot be adequately eliminated. In emphysema, patients develop hypoxemia relatively early as alveolar surface area decreases and V/Q mismatch develops. Hypercapnia appears later when: (1) surface area is so reduced that even CO₂'s high diffusibility cannot compensate, (2) dead space ventilation becomes so extensive that alveolar ventilation is grossly inadequate, or (3) respiratory muscle fatigue develops. The presence of hypercapnia therefore indicates advanced disease. Faster O₂ diffusion reverses the relative diffusibility. Hemoglobin-dependent O₂ transport and higher CO₂ molecular weight don't explain the clinical pattern. Equal effects is incorrect—timing does differ.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CO2 diffuses about 20 times as rapidly as O2, so diffusion defects cause hypoxemia first; hypercapnia appears only when alveolar ventilation itself falls. FA: diffusion-limiting disease is more likely to cause early hypoxia than hypercapnia. COMLEX: New CO2 retention in a COPD patient therefore marks advanced disease or acute ventilatory failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 8 — diffusion rates of O2 versus CO2","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Equal effects is incorrect—tim"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q41_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Alveolar changes in emphysema vs pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist examines lung tissue from two deceased patients. Patient A (emphysema) shows large airspaces measuring 5-10 mm with virtually absent intervening walls. Patient B (lobar pneumonia) shows alveolar spaces filled with inflammatory cells and fluid, but walls remain intact. What accounts for this fundamental difference in alveolar wall integrity?","choices":{"A":"Emphysema involves enzymatic destruction of alveolar walls; pneumonia involves exudation without structural destruction","B":"Emphysema results from viral infection; pneumonia results from bacterial infection with different pathophysiology","C":"Emphysema causes wall thickening mistaken for absence; pneumonia causes true wall destruction","D":"Both diseases destroy alveolar walls, but pneumonia walls regenerate whereas emphysema walls cannot","E":"The difference is artifact from tissue preparation; actual pathology is identical"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The fundamental pathological distinction between emphysema and pneumonia lies in alveolar wall integrity. Emphysema involves permanent destruction of alveolar walls through enzymatic degradation (primarily by elastase when antiprotease defense is inadequate), creating enlarged airspaces from confluence of adjacent alveoli. The walls are physically absent—destroyed. This destruction is irreversible; once walls are eliminated, they cannot regenerate. In contrast, pneumonia involves filling of alveolar spaces with inflammatory exudate (neutrophils, bacteria, fluid, fibrin) but the alveolar walls themselves remain intact. The walls are structurally preserved even though the spaces are filled. This distinction has crucial implications: emphysema causes permanent structural damage and irreversible loss of gas exchange surface area, while pneumonia is potentially reversible—once infection resolves and exudate clears, the intact walls allow alveoli to return to normal function and gas exchange capacity. The other options incorrectly describe or equate these fundamentally different pathological processes. Robbins attributes emphysema to smoke injury, protease-antiprotease imbalance and oxidants; infection may exacerbate it but is not thought to start the tissue destruction. Robbins defines emphysema by destruction of alveolar walls, not thickening, while pneumonia fills alveoli with fluid and cells. The difference is real: emphysema destroys alveolar walls, whereas pneumonia fills the alveoli with fluid and blood cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Emphysema destroys alveolar walls — FA: imbalance of proteases and antiproteases (↑ elastase activity) causes loss of elastic fibers; pneumonia fills intact alveoli with exudate. COMLEX: FA — α1-antitrypsin, the antiprotease that inhibits neutrophil elastase, is deficient in panacinar emphysema.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — the etiology and morphology of the emphysema types","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Robbins attributes emphysema to smoke injury, protease-antip","C":"Robbins defines emphysema by destruction of alveolar walls, ","E":"The difference is real: emphysema destroys alveolar walls, w"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q42_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Alveolar changes in emphysema vs pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A radiologist compares chest CT scans from two patients with dyspnea. The emphysema patient shows areas of decreased attenuation (darker) representing large emphysematous spaces. The pneumonia patient shows areas of increased attenuation (whiter) representing consolidated lung. What histological difference between these diseases explains the opposite radiographic appearances?","choices":{"A":"Emphysema walls are calcified (hyperdense); pneumonia walls are destroyed (hypodense)","B":"The radiographic difference is technical artifact, not reflecting actual pathology","C":"Emphysema increases pulmonary blood flow; pneumonia decreases blood flow","D":"Emphysema thickens alveolar walls with fibrosis; pneumonia causes wall thinning","E":"Emphysema enlarges air-filled spaces; pneumonia fills them with fluid and cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The opposite radiographic appearances directly reflect the underlying pathology. In emphysema, alveolar wall destruction creates enlarged air-filled spaces (bullae, blebs)—essentially more air with less tissue per unit volume. Since air has very low radiographic density, these regions appear darker (decreased attenuation, radiolucent) on CT. The enlarged spaces can measure 5-10+ mm compared to normal alveoli of about 0.2 mm (Guyton Ch 40). In pneumonia, alveolar spaces fill with exudate (inflammatory cells, bacteria, protein-rich fluid), replacing air with higher-density material. Since cells and fluid have much higher radiographic density than air, these consolidated regions appear white (increased attenuation, opacification) on CT. The walls themselves remain intact but cannot be visualized individually; the consolidated airspace is what's visible. This pathological difference (destroyed walls creating large air spaces vs. intact walls with filled spaces) produces characteristic and opposite radiographic patterns that aid diagnosis. The other options incorrectly describe the pathological changes. Emphysematous walls are destroyed, not calcified, and pneumonia fills the alveoli with fluid and cells. Emphysema reduces, not increases, the number of pulmonary capillaries; the CT contrast reflects enlarged air spaces versus fluid-filled alveoli. Emphysema destroys alveolar walls rather than thickening them, and pneumonia fills alveoli with fluid and cells rather than thinning walls.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Emphysema means more air and less tissue per volume (FA: increased lung field lucency); pneumonia consolidates lobes with fluid and cellular debris, raising density. COMLEX: Guyton — consolidation can involve whole lobes or a whole lung, and the lost ventilation of that tissue is what drives the hypoxemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 4 — lobar pneumonia versus bronchopneumonia, gross distribution and histology","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Emphysematous walls are destroyed, not calcified, and pneumo","C":"Emphysema reduces, not increases, the number of pulmonary ca","D":"Emphysema destroys alveolar walls rather than thickening the"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q43_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Alveolar changes in emphysema vs pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A physician explains to a medical student that emphysema causes permanent disability while pneumonia, despite acute severity, is potentially reversible. The student asks what structural difference allows complete recovery from pneumonia but not emphysema. What is the correct explanation?","choices":{"A":"Pneumonia destroys bronchi which can regenerate; emphysema destroys alveoli which cannot regenerate","B":"Pneumonia leaves alveolar walls intact so they recover; emphysema destroys them permanently","C":"Pneumonia affects only one lung; emphysema affects both lungs simultaneously","D":"Pneumonia lasts only days whereas emphysema lasts weeks, allowing adaptation","E":"Pneumonia stimulates surfactant production; emphysema inhibits surfactant permanently"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The reversibility distinction is fundamental: pneumonia preserves alveolar wall architecture while filling spaces with exudate that can be cleared, whereas emphysema permanently destroys the alveolar walls themselves. In pneumonia, once the infection is controlled (antibiotics kill bacteria), the inflammatory response resolves and the exudate (cells, fluid, fibrin) is cleared by macrophages and lymphatic drainage. Since the underlying structural framework (alveolar walls with their capillary networks, elastic tissue, and epithelium) remains intact, the alveoli can return to normal architecture and function. Gas exchange surface area is restored. In emphysema, proteolytic enzymes destroy alveolar walls—the walls are physically absent, degraded. These walls contain specialized cells (type I and II pneumocytes), capillary networks, and elastic fibers that cannot regenerate once destroyed. The body cannot rebuild alveolar walls, so emphysema causes permanent loss of gas exchange surface area and irreversible functional impairment. This pathological distinction explains why pneumonia has good recovery potential while emphysema causes progressive, irreversible disability. The other options don't accurately explain the fundamental difference. Laterality is not the distinction; pneumonia can involve whole lobes or a whole lung, while emphysema's destruction of alveolar walls is irreversible. Duration is not the distinction; emphysema usually progresses over many years, and its destruction of alveolar walls is irreversible.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pneumonia's alveolar walls remain intact while the spaces fill with exudate, so clearing the exudate restores function; emphysema permanently destroys the alveolar walls themselves. COMLEX: Guyton — emphysema progresses slowly over many years toward devastating dyspnea, so smoking cessation is the key intervention.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 5 — the stages of lobar pneumonia, ending in resolution","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Laterality is not the distinction; pneumonia can involve who","D":"Duration is not the distinction; emphysema usually progresse"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q44_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Pulmonary abnormalities causing decreased O₂ saturation in pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with lobar pneumonia affecting the right lower lobe has arterial blood gases showing PO₂ of 58 mm Hg (oxygen saturation 88%) despite adequate ventilation. The affected lobe's alveoli are filled with inflammatory exudate. What mechanism most directly causes her hypoxemia?","choices":{"A":"Complete absence of ventilation to the affected lobe creates absolute anatomical shunt","B":"Low ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) ratio in the affected lobe creates physiological shunt","C":"Increased V/Q ratio in the affected lobe increases dead space ventilation","D":"Destruction of alveolar walls reduces total respiratory membrane surface area","E":"Bronchospasm prevents oxygen from reaching any alveoli in the affected lobe"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pneumonia causes hypoxemia primarily through creating low ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) ratio regions—physiological shunt. In the affected lobe, alveoli are partially or completely filled with inflammatory exudate (cells, bacteria, fluid), markedly reducing ventilation to those alveoli. However, pulmonary blood flow (perfusion) to the affected region initially continues relatively normally. This creates low V/Q regions: blood flows past poorly ventilated alveoli without adequate oxygenation, then this poorly oxygenated blood mixes with well-oxygenated blood from healthy lung regions (venous admixture), reducing overall arterial oxygen content. While some alveoli may be completely unventilated (absolute shunt), most pneumonic alveoli retain some ventilation (partial obstruction), making this physiological shunt rather than anatomical shunt. The body attempts compensation through hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (reducing blood flow to poorly ventilated regions), but this is incomplete. Absolute anatomical shunt overstates the ventilation loss. Increased V/Q reverses the V/Q change. Alveolar wall destruction describes emphysema. Bronchospasm isn't the primary mechanism in pneumonia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Exudate-filled alveoli are perfused but poorly ventilated, giving a low VA/Q that acts as physiological shunt and lowers arterial O2. FA: V/Q = 0 is shunt, does not correct with 100% O2, and raises the A-a gradient. COMLEX: The pulmonary OMM lab ties pneumonia to T2–T6 viscerosomatic findings — Still emphasized ribs 6–8 — and to rib raising among adjunctive OMT.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 12 — VA/Q and its effect on alveolar gas pressures","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Absolute anatomical shunt over","C":"Increased V/Q reverses the V/Q","E":"Bronchospasm isn't the primary"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q45_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Pulmonary abnormalities causing decreased O₂ saturation in pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student observes that a patient with right lower lobe pneumonia has hypoxemia (PO₂ 55 mm Hg) despite normal ventilation to the left lung and right upper/middle lobes. The student calculates that 75% of lung tissue is functioning normally. Why doesn't the healthy lung tissue compensate fully for the abnormal region?","choices":{"A":"The healthy lung tissue becomes inflamed and ceases gas exchange","B":"Healthy alveoli reflexly hypoventilate to match the diseased lobe's ventilation","C":"Poorly oxygenated blood from low V/Q regions mixes with well-oxygenated blood","D":"The heart preferentially shunts blood to the diseased lung lobe","E":"Normal lung tissue reduces its oxygen extraction to maintain homeostasis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hypoxemia in regional lung disease results from venous admixture—the mixing of poorly oxygenated blood (from diseased regions) with well-oxygenated blood (from healthy regions). In this patient, healthy lung regions (75%) ventilate and perfuse normally, fully oxygenating blood passing through them (PO₂ ~100 mm Hg). However, the diseased region (25%) has low V/Q ratio: blood flows past fluid-filled alveoli with inadequate oxygen uptake, emerging with a low PO₂, close to that of venous blood. When these two blood streams mix in the left atrium (venous admixture), the overall arterial PO₂ represents the weighted average of the two streams. Even though 75% of blood is well-oxygenated, the remaining 25% with low oxygen content significantly reduces the mixed arterial PO₂. The healthy lung tissue cannot compensate by \"extra\" oxygenation—blood leaving healthy alveoli is already ~98% saturated; increasing PO₂ further adds negligible oxygen due to hemoglobin saturation plateau. Thus regional disease causes systemic hypoxemia through venous admixture that healthy tissue cannot overcome. Inflamed healthy tissue, preferential shunting to the diseased lobe, and reduced O₂ extraction describe mechanisms that don't occur. Reflex hypoventilation is wrong because the healthy regions do not reflexly hypoventilate; they are fully ventilated and their blood is already nearly saturated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Well-ventilated alveoli cannot raise their blood above about 97% saturation, so they cannot offset poorly oxygenated blood from consolidated lung; the mixture lowers aortic saturation (Guyton's example averages about 78%). COMLEX: This venous admixture is the same mechanism, on a larger scale, as the normal bronchial shunt that lowers PO2 from 104 to 95 mm Hg.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — O2 transport and the shunt flow that lowers arterial PO2","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Inflamed healthy tissue, prefe"}},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q46_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Pulmonary abnormalities causing decreased O₂ saturation in pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with bacterial pneumonia affecting 30% of lung parenchyma develops progressive hypoxemia (PO₂ declining from 70 to 52 mm Hg over 24 hours) despite stable chest X-ray. His physician considers that reduced respiratory membrane surface area may be contributing. How do fluid-filled alveoli impair O₂ uptake in regions that still contain some air?","choices":{"A":"The fluid physically blocks oxygen molecules from contacting the alveolar membrane","B":"Inflammatory cells in the fluid consume oxygen before it can diffuse to blood","C":"Fluid increases the diffusion distance between inspired air and capillary blood","D":"The alveolar walls dissolve in the inflammatory fluid, eliminating the membrane","E":"Fluid prevents hemoglobin from binding oxygen even when it reaches the capillaries"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fluid-filled alveoli reduce gas exchange by increasing diffusion distance and effectively reducing functional respiratory membrane surface area. Normally, inspired air directly contacts the thin alveolar membrane (about 0.6 μm thick), with only a short diffusion distance to pulmonary capillary blood. In pneumonia, inflammatory exudate (fluid, cells, proteins, fibrin) fills alveolar spaces, creating a liquid barrier between inspired air and the alveolar membrane. Oxygen must now dissolve in and diffuse through this fluid layer before reaching the alveolar-capillary membrane, substantially increasing diffusion distance. Additionally, fluid-filled alveoli are functionally \"removed\" from gas exchange—they contribute little to oxygenation, effectively reducing available surface area from ~70 m² to ~50 m² (in 30% involvement). Some alveoli may be completely filled (no air contact at all), creating absolute shunt. Fick's law states gas diffusion is inversely proportional to distance and directly proportional to surface area, so increased distance and reduced functional area both impair oxygen uptake, causing progressive hypoxemia. Physical blocking of O₂ oversimplifies. O₂ consumption by inflammatory cells is incorrect—cells don't consume significant O₂. Dissolved alveolar walls is wrong—walls remain intact. Blocked hemoglobin binding incorrectly describes hemoglobin function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fluid in or around alveoli lengthens the diffusion path; Guyton lists edema fluid in the interstitial space and alveoli among the causes of reduced diffusion through the respiratory membrane, since rate falls as distance rises. COMLEX: FA — the Fick equation places thickness (Δx) in the denominator, the term that rises in edema and fibrosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 3 — the factors, including membrane thickness, that set the diffusion rate","isHighYield":false}],"16":[{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q01_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Atelectasis - lung collapse and effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man develops atelectasis of the right middle lobe 48 hours after abdominal surgery. A large mucous plug completely obstructs the lobar bronchus. Within 2-3 hours of the obstruction, the previously air-filled alveoli distal to the blockage begin collapsing. What mechanism explains this rapid alveolar collapse following complete airway obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Surfactant production immediately ceases when airways are obstructed, increasing surface tension","B":"Air trapped beyond the obstruction is absorbed into pulmonary capillary blood, removing alveolar gas","C":"Smooth muscle in alveolar walls contracts reflexively in response to obstruction","D":"Inflammatory cells migrate into alveoli and consume the oxygen, creating negative pressure","E":"Blood flow to the obstructed region increases dramatically, pulling air into circulation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Following complete airway obstruction, atelectasis develops rapidly because air trapped distal to the obstruction is absorbed by blood flowing through the pulmonary capillaries. The alveolar air contains oxygen (~100 mm Hg) and CO₂ (~40 mm Hg) that create concentration gradients favoring diffusion into the relatively deoxygenated mixed venous blood in pulmonary capillaries. Oxygen is absorbed much more rapidly than nitrogen because of its higher solubility and the large alveolar-capillary PO₂ gradient. Within minutes to hours, this gas absorption progressively removes air from the trapped alveoli. If the lung tissue is pliable (normal compliance), this air removal simply causes alveolar collapse—atelectasis. If the lung tissue is rigid or fibrotic and cannot collapse easily, the absorption creates very negative pressures within the alveoli (Guyton Ch 43), which then pulls fluid out of pulmonary capillaries into the alveolar spaces, filling them with edema fluid. This edema-filled pattern is typical of massive collapse of an entire lung. Immediate surfactant loss is incorrect—surfactant persists for hours to days. Alveolar smooth muscle contraction is wrong—alveoli lack smooth muscle. O₂ consumption by inflammatory cells and increased blood flow don't describe actual mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Obstructive atelectasis — air trapped beyond a blocked bronchus is absorbed within minutes to hours by blood flowing through the pulmonary capillaries, and pliable lung simply collapses; rigid fibrotic lung instead fills with edema fluid. FA: obstructive (resorptive) atelectasis from foreign body, mucous plug or tumor; prevented with incentive spirometry or PEEP. COMLEX: FA — atelectasis is dull to percussion with ↓ breath sounds and ↓ fremitus, trachea deviated toward the lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — the types of atelectasis and the causes of each","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"O₂ consumption by inflammatory"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q02_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Atelectasis - lung collapse and effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A premature infant born at 28 weeks gestation develops respiratory distress syndrome (hyaline membrane disease) due to surfactant deficiency. Chest X-ray shows diffuse bilateral atelectasis. How does lack of surfactant lead to alveolar collapse in this infant?","choices":{"A":"Surfactant normally prevents bacterial colonization; without it, infection causes collapse","B":"Surfactant normally stimulates respiratory muscle contraction; without it, breathing effort is inadequate","C":"Surfactant blocks pulmonary capillaries; its absence causes excessive blood flow that compresses alveoli","D":"Surfactant normally decreases surface tension 2-10 fold; its absence allows surface forces to collapse alveoli","E":"Surfactant normally increases airway diameter; without it, bronchial obstruction occurs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Surfactant is secreted by type II alveolar epithelial cells and coats the inner surface of alveoli, where it decreases surface tension by 2- to 10-fold compared to pure water. Surface tension creates inward-directed forces (following LaPlace's law: P = 2T/r) that tend to collapse alveoli, especially small ones. In premature infants with inadequate surfactant production, the surface tension of alveolar fluid becomes several times greater than normal, creating powerful collapsing forces. Without surfactant's surface tension-reducing effects, small alveoli collapse into larger alveoli or collapse completely. The increased surface tension also makes it extremely difficult to inflate the lungs—requiring much higher inspiratory pressures. This is why these infants experience severe respiratory distress and why exogenous surfactant administration (along with assisted ventilation and antenatal corticosteroids) has dramatically improved survival in respiratory distress syndrome. The more premature the infant, the less mature the surfactant system, and the more severe the atelectasis and respiratory distress. The other options incorrectly describe surfactant's functions—its primary role is surface tension reduction. Guyton says surfactant is secreted by alveolar epithelial cells and decreases surface tension 2- to 10-fold; preventing bacterial colonization is not its role. Guyton says surfactant is secreted by alveolar epithelial cells and decreases surface tension 2- to 10-fold; stimulating respiratory muscle contraction is not its role. Guyton says surfactant is secreted by alveolar epithelial cells and decreases surface tension 2- to 10-fold; blocking capillaries is not its role. Guyton says surfactant is secreted by alveolar epithelial cells and decreases surface tension 2- to 10-fold; changing airway diameter is not its role.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surfactant lowers alveolar surface tension 2- to 10-fold; in hyaline membrane disease of premature infants so little is secreted that surface tension rises several-fold and large areas collapse. FA: NRDS — surfactant deficiency → ↑ surface tension → alveolar collapse ('ground-glass' lungs); risk ↑ with prematurity, maternal diabetes and C-section. COMLEX: FA — an amniotic L/S ratio ≥ 2 indicates mature lungs, < 1.5 predicts NRDS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — surfactant's function, mechanism and developmental timing","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Guyton says surfactant is secreted by alveolar epithelial cells and decreases surface tension 2- to 10-fold; preventing ","B":"Guyton says surfactant is secreted by alveolar epithelial cells and decreases surface tension 2- to 10-fold; stimulating","C":"Guyton says surfactant is secreted by alveolar epithelial cells and decreases surface tension 2- to 10-fold; blocking ca","E":"Guyton says surfactant is secreted by alveolar epithelial cells and decreases surface tension 2- to 10-fold; changing ai"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q03_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Atelectasis - lung collapse and effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman develops massive atelectasis of the entire right lung following aspiration of a foreign body that completely obstructs the right main bronchus. Arterial blood gases show PO₂ of 62 mm Hg despite the left lung functioning normally. Pulmonary angiography reveals markedly increased vascular resistance in the collapsed right lung. What mechanisms cause increased pulmonary vascular resistance in the atelectatic lung?","choices":{"A":"Surfactant accumulation in blood vessels increases blood viscosity and resistance","B":"Inflammatory mediators cause systemic vasodilation, shunting blood away from the lungs","C":"Lung collapse compresses and folds vessels, and alveolar hypoxia causes vasoconstriction","D":"Increased blood flow to the collapsed region overwhelms vessel capacity","E":"Air emboli from collapsed alveoli obstruct pulmonary arterioles"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Atelectasis increases pulmonary vascular resistance through two mechanisms: (1) Mechanical compression: as lung volume decreases during collapse, pulmonary vessels are compressed and folded, increasing resistance to blood flow through the collapsed region. (2) Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction: the collapsed, unventilated alveoli become hypoxic (low PO₂), triggering local vasoconstriction of pulmonary arterioles—a protective reflex that diverts blood away from poorly ventilated regions toward better-ventilated areas to optimize V/Q matching. The combination of mechanical compression and hypoxic vasoconstriction substantially increases vascular resistance in atelectatic lung tissue. However, this compensation is incomplete, so some blood still flows through the collapsed lung without adequate oxygenation, creating physiological shunt. This shunted, poorly oxygenated blood mixes with well-oxygenated blood from the normal left lung, causing arterial hypoxemia (PO₂ 62 mm Hg). The left lung cannot fully compensate because even healthy alveoli cannot \"super-oxygenate\" blood beyond ~98% saturation due to hemoglobin saturation plateau. The other options describe mechanisms that don't occur in atelectasis. Guyton says the rise in resistance in atelectasis comes from the collapse compressing and folding the vessels plus hypoxic vasoconstriction, not from surfactant in the vessels. Guyton says the rise in resistance in atelectasis comes from the collapse compressing and folding the vessels plus hypoxic vasoconstriction, not from vasodilation. Guyton says blood flow through the atelectatic lung is greatly reduced because resistance rises, not increased. Guyton says the rise in resistance in atelectasis comes from the collapse compressing and folding the vessels plus hypoxic vasoconstriction, not from air emboli.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Collapse raises resistance in the atelectatic lung two ways — the shrinking lung compresses and folds its vessels, and alveolar hypoxia adds vasoconstriction; in Guyton's example five-sixths of the blood then passes through the aerated lung, so desaturation stays mild. COMLEX: FA — chronic hypoxic vasoconstriction raises pulmonary vascular resistance and can lead to pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — pulmonary hypertension, including hypoxic causes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Guyton says the rise in resistance in atelectasis comes from the collapse compressing and folding the vessels plus hypoxic vasoconstriction, not from surfactant","B":"Guyton says the rise in resistance in atelectasis comes from the collapse compressing and folding the vessels plus hypoxic vasoconstriction, not from vasodilati","D":"Guyton says blood flow through the atelectatic lung is great","E":"Guyton says the rise in resistance in atelectasis comes from the collapse compressing and folding the vessels plus hypoxic vasoconstriction, not from air emboli"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q04_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Asthma - immunologic process with mast cells","batch":1,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with known pollen allergy experiences an acute asthma attack during spring. She has previously developed high levels of IgE antibodies specific to tree pollen. When she inhales pollen, these IgE antibodies trigger mast cells in her lung interstitium near the bronchioles. What substances are released by activated mast cells during this allergic asthma attack?","choices":{"A":"Insulin, glucagon, and growth hormone causing metabolic changes","B":"Surfactant, mucin, and elastase that increase airway patency","C":"Acetylcholine, norepinephrine, and dopamine causing neurotransmitter effects","D":"Histamine, leukotrienes (SRS-A), eosinophil chemotactic factor, bradykinin","E":"Complement proteins, immunoglobulins, and interferons for immune defense"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When pollen antigens bind to IgE antibodies attached to mast cells in asthmatic patients, the mast cells degranulate and release multiple inflammatory mediators: (1) Histamine—causes immediate bronchoconstriction, increases vascular permeability causing edema, and stimulates mucus secretion; (2) Slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis (a mixture of leukotrienes, particularly LTC₄, LTD₄, LTE₄)—causes prolonged, powerful bronchoconstriction lasting hours (hence \"slow-reacting\"); (3) Eosinophilic chemotactic factor—attracts eosinophils to the airways, perpetuating inflammation; (4) Bradykinin—causes bronchoconstriction, vasodilation, and increased vascular permeability. The combined effects of these mediators produce: localized edema in bronchiolar walls, thick mucus secretion into bronchiolar lumens, and spasm of bronchiolar smooth muscle. These effects greatly increase airway resistance. The bronchiolar diameter becomes further reduced during expiration compared to inspiration because the increased expiratory effort compresses bronchioles externally, creating the characteristic expiratory difficulty in asthma. The other options list substances not released by mast cells during allergic reactions. Complement proteins, immunoglobulins, and interferons are not among the mast cell releases Guyton lists; IgE is the antibody bound to the mast cell, not a mediator it secretes. Guyton lists the mast cell products in asthma as histamine, SRS-A, eosinophil chemotactic factor and bradykinin, not insulin, glucagon or growth hormone. Guyton lists the mast cell products as histamine, SRS-A (leukotrienes), eosinophil chemotactic factor and bradykinin, not surfactant, mucin and elastase. Guyton lists the mast cell products in asthma as histamine, SRS-A, eosinophil chemotactic factor and bradykinin, not acetylcholine, norepinephrine or dopamine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pollen binding IgE on interstitial mast cells releases histamine, slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis (leukotrienes), eosinophilic chemotactic factor and bradykinin, producing bronchiolar wall edema, thick mucus and smooth-muscle spasm. FA: type I hypersensitivity — immediate (histamine, tryptase, leukotrienes) and late (chemokines, eosinophils) phases. COMLEX: FA — Curschmann spirals and Charcot-Leyden crystals in sputum mark the eosinophilic airway inflammation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 6 — asthma's fundamental abnormality, an exaggerated Th2 response","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Guyton lists the mast cell products in asthma as histamine, SRS-A, eosinophil chemotactic factor and bradykinin, not ins","B":"Guyton lists the mast cell products as histamine, SRS-A (leu","C":"Guyton lists the mast cell products in asthma as histamine, SRS-A, eosinophil chemotactic factor and bradykinin, not ace"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q05_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Asthma - immunologic process with mast cells","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with allergic asthma develops severe bronchospasm after exposure to ragweed pollen. Pulmonary function tests during the attack show normal inspiratory flow rates but markedly reduced expiratory flow rates (maximum expiratory flow reduced by 60%). Why does asthma cause greater difficulty with expiration than inspiration?","choices":{"A":"Positive pleural pressure in expiration compresses the already-narrowed bronchioles","B":"Inspiratory muscles are stronger than expiratory muscles, overcoming airway resistance more easily","C":"Pollen particles physically block airways only during expiration, not inspiration","D":"Mast cell mediators selectively paralyze expiratory muscles while sparing inspiratory muscles","E":"Inspiratory negative pressure dilates airways while expiratory positive pressure has no effect"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Asthma causes disproportionately severe expiratory obstruction due to dynamic airway collapse during forced expiration. In asthmatic patients, the bronchioles are already partially occluded by smooth muscle spasm, mucosal edema, and mucus secretions (from mast cell mediator effects). During expiration, especially forced expiration, the positive pleural pressure required to drive air out also compresses the outside of the bronchioles. Since asthmatic bronchioles are already narrowed, this external compression causes further severe narrowing or complete collapse, markedly increasing airway resistance during expiration. By contrast, during inspiration, the negative pleural pressure needed to expand the lungs actually \"pulls\" airways open, helping maintain airway patency despite the underlying bronchoconstriction. This explains why asthmatic patients often inspire quite adequately but have great difficulty expiring, leading to air trapping, increased functional residual capacity and residual volume, and over years, development of \"barrel chest\" from chronic hyperinflation. Clinical measurements show greatly reduced maximum expiratory rate and reduced FEV₁/FVC ratio, with relatively preserved inspiratory function. The other options don't explain the expiratory-predominant obstruction characteristic of asthma. Muscle strength is not the explanation; expiration is worse because positive pleural pressure compresses narrowed bronchioles. Pollen acts by triggering mast cell mediator release, not by blocking airways; expiration is worse because chest pressure compresses already narrowed bronchioles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In asthma the bronchiolar diameter shrinks more in expiration than inspiration, because expiratory effort compresses the outsides of already narrowed bronchioles; air enters but is trapped. COMLEX: FA — between attacks spirometry may be normal; in an exacerbation it shows an obstructive pattern that improves with a bronchodilator.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — the principle of maximum expiratory flow and dynamic compression","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Muscle strength is not the explanation; expiration is worse ","C":"Pollen acts by triggering mast cell mediator release, not by"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q06_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Asthma - immunologic process with mast cells","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman with long-standing asthma visits her physician. She reports that about 70% of her asthma attacks are triggered by exposure to specific plant pollens. What immunologic mechanism explains why only certain allergens trigger her asthma attacks while other airborne particles do not?","choices":{"A":"Her airways have developed mechanical scarring that physically traps only pollen particles","B":"She has pollen-specific IgE bound to mast cells, which degranulate on antigen contact","C":"Pollen particles are larger than other particles and directly obstruct her bronchioles","D":"Her lung macrophages selectively recognize and attack pollen while ignoring other substances","E":"Pollen produces toxins that directly damage bronchiolar smooth muscle"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Allergic asthma involves a specific type I hypersensitivity reaction mediated by IgE antibodies. This patient has been previously sensitized to certain plant pollens, causing her B cells to produce IgE antibodies specifically directed against proteins (antigens) in those pollens. These IgE antibodies attach to Fc receptors on mast cells located in the lung interstitium near bronchioles and small bronchi. The IgE antibodies remain attached to mast cells, waiting for their specific antigen. When this patient inhales the specific pollen to which she is sensitized, the pollen antigens bind to the IgE antibodies on mast cell surfaces. This antigen-antibody binding cross-links IgE molecules, triggering mast cell degranulation and release of inflammatory mediators (histamine, leukotrienes, bradykinin, eosinophilic chemotactic factor). Other airborne particles don't trigger reactions because she lacks IgE antibodies specific to those particles—there's no antigen-antibody recognition and no mast cell activation. This explains the specificity of allergic asthma: only antigens matching existing IgE antibodies cause attacks. About 70% of younger asthmatic patients have this allergic pattern; older patients more commonly develop asthma from nonallergenic irritants. The other options don't explain the immunologic specificity. Selectivity comes from IgE formed against the specific pollen and attached to mast cells, not from scarring that traps particles. Particle size does not explain it; pollen reacts with pollen-specific IgE on mast cells, triggering mediator release. Macrophage recognition does not explain it; IgE attached to mast cells does. Pollen does not act as a toxin; it reacts with mast cell-attached IgE, releasing mediators that cause bronchiolar spasm and edema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Allergic asthma (about 70% of patients younger than 30, per Guyton, mostly to plant pollens) depends on specific IgE bound to mast cells near the bronchioles; only the sensitizing antigen triggers release. In older patients the cause is almost always nonallergenic irritants such as smog. COMLEX: FA — test with skin prick or serum allergen-specific IgE.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — the etiology and phenotypes of asthma","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Selectivity comes from IgE formed against the specific polle","C":"Particle size does not explain it; pollen reacts with pollen","D":"Macrophage recognition does not explain it; IgE attached to ","E":"Pollen does not act as a toxin; it reacts with mast cell-att"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q07_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Tuberculosis - late-stage effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with untreated tuberculosis for 5 years develops progressive dyspnea. Chest CT shows multiple areas of lung tissue destruction with large abscess cavities and extensive fibrosis throughout both lungs. Pulmonary function testing reveals reduced vital capacity (2.5 L, predicted 4.8 L) and reduced breathing capacity. What pathophysiological mechanism explains his reduced lung volumes?","choices":{"A":"Tubercle bacilli directly paralyze the respiratory muscles, preventing full lung expansion","B":"Abscess cavities fill with air that cannot be expelled, paradoxically reducing measurable volumes","C":"Tuberculosis causes bronchospasm similar to asthma, trapping air and preventing full inspiration","D":"Extensive fibrosis stiffens the lungs and destroys functional tissue, lowering compliance","E":"The \"walling off\" process creates flexible tissue that hyperinflates the lungs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Late-stage tuberculosis causes progressive lung destruction with extensive fibrosis (scar tissue formation) throughout the lungs, along with formation of large abscess cavities and loss of functional lung parenchyma. Fibrotic tissue is stiff and non-compliant, requiring much greater respiratory muscle work to expand the lungs during inspiration. This increased \"work\" of breathing, combined with the reduced total amount of functional lung tissue, causes reduced vital capacity and reduced total breathing capacity. The fibrosis makes lungs less distensible (reduced compliance), limiting how much the lungs can expand even with maximal inspiratory effort. The tubercle bacilli cause this damage through an initial protective response: infected tissue is invaded by macrophages and \"walled off\" by fibrous tissue to form tubercles, limiting bacterial spread. However, when tuberculosis is untreated (occurs in ~3% of cases), this walling-off process fails, tubercle bacilli spread throughout the lungs, causing extreme destruction with abscess cavities. The combination of destroyed tissue, fibrosis, and reduced functional lung volume progressively impairs pulmonary function. The other options don't accurately describe tuberculosis pathophysiology. Guyton attributes the reduced vital capacity of late tuberculosis to widespread fibrosis and loss of functional lung tissue, not muscle paralysis. Guyton attributes the reduced vital capacity of late tuberculosis to fibrosis and less functional lung tissue, not to air trapped in cavities. Guyton describes late tuberculosis as fibrosis with loss of functional lung tissue, not asthma-like bronchospasm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Late TB leaves many areas of fibrosis and less functional lung, increasing the work of breathing and reducing vital capacity and breathing capacity; it also thins the membrane area, thickens the membrane and disturbs V/Q. COMLEX: Guyton lists tuberculosis among the fibrotic 'constricted lung' diseases with reduced TLC and RV.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 49 → Obj 4 — primary versus reactivation tuberculosis","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Guyton attributes the reduced vital capacity of late tuberculosis to widespread ","B":"Guyton attributes the reduced vital capacity of late tuberculosis to fibrosis an","C":"Guyton describes late tuberculosis as fibrosis with loss of "}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q08_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Tuberculosis - late-stage effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with advanced pulmonary tuberculosis undergoes pulmonary function testing. Her diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide (DLCO) is 45% of predicted. Arterial blood gases show PO₂ of 62 mm Hg and PCO₂ of 48 mm Hg. What mechanisms in late-stage tuberculosis cause reduced pulmonary diffusing capacity?","choices":{"A":"Tuberculosis increases surfactant production, which blocks gas diffusion","B":"Bronchospasm prevents oxygen from reaching alveoli","C":"Excessive mucus production dissolves the respiratory membrane","D":"Tubercle bacilli consume all available oxygen before it can diffuse to blood","E":"Fibrosis reduces membrane surface area and increases membrane thickness"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Late-stage tuberculosis causes reduced pulmonary diffusing capacity through two mechanisms related to the extensive tissue destruction and fibrosis: (1) Reduced total respiratory membrane surface area—the widespread lung tissue destruction with abscess cavity formation and loss of functional alveoli substantially decreases the total surface area available for gas exchange. (2) Increased thickness of the respiratory membrane—the inflammatory process and fibrosis (scar tissue deposition) increase the thickness of the alveolar-capillary membrane, increasing the diffusion distance for gases. Fick's law states that gas diffusion is directly proportional to surface area and inversely proportional to membrane thickness, so both these changes markedly reduce diffusing capacity. Additionally, tuberculosis causes abnormal ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) ratios: some areas are well-ventilated but poorly perfused (high V/Q, dead space), while other areas are poorly ventilated but still perfused (low V/Q, shunt), further reducing overall pulmonary gas exchange efficiency for both O₂ and CO₂. This explains her hypoxemia and mild hypercapnia. The other options don't describe actual tuberculosis pathophysiology. Guyton attributes reduced diffusing capacity in late tuberculosis to less membrane surface area and a thicker membrane, not to surfactant. Bronchospasm is not what reduces diffusing capacity; in late tuberculosis membrane surface area falls and membrane thickness rises. Mucus does not dissolve the membrane; in late tuberculosis the membrane loses surface area and becomes thicker. Bacilli do not use up the oxygen; diffusing capacity falls because the membrane loses surface area and becomes thicker.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In late TB, reduced total respiratory membrane surface area and increased membrane thickness progressively diminish diffusing capacity, and abnormal V/Q further reduces O2 and CO2 exchange. COMLEX: FA — fibrotic (interstitial-type) restriction lowers DLCO and raises the A-a gradient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 9 — diffusing capacity, its definition and measurement","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Guyton attributes reduced diffusing capacity in late tubercu","B":"Bronchospasm is not what reduces diffusing capacity; in late","C":"Mucus does not dissolve the membrane; in late tuberculosis t","D":"Bacilli do not use up the oxygen; diffusing capacity falls b"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q09_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Tuberculosis - late-stage effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A physician evaluates a 55-year-old man with late-stage tuberculosis who reports severe dyspnea with minimal exertion. The patient's lung tissue shows extensive fibrosis and multiple abscess cavities. Which pathophysiological change most directly explains his increased work of breathing?","choices":{"A":"Tubercle bacilli release toxins that directly stimulate the respiratory center, forcing rapid breathing","B":"Fibrotic tissue has low compliance, so the muscles must work harder to ventilate","C":"Abscess cavities create excessive dead space, paradoxically improving gas exchange","D":"Tuberculosis increases lung compliance, making lungs too elastic and difficult to control","E":"The \"walling off\" process increases airway diameter, causing turbulent flow"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The extensive pulmonary fibrosis characteristic of late-stage tuberculosis dramatically increases the work of breathing by reducing lung compliance (distensibility). Fibrotic scar tissue is stiff and non-elastic, making it much more difficult for respiratory muscles to expand the lungs during inspiration. Normal lungs require relatively modest inspiratory muscle force to create the negative pleural pressure needed for adequate tidal volumes. In fibrotic lungs, the same tidal volume requires much greater muscle force and energy expenditure because the stiff tissue resists expansion. This increased work of breathing is a major component of the dyspnea (air hunger) experienced by tuberculosis patients. Additionally, the reduced total amount of functional lung tissue means that to achieve adequate minute ventilation and gas exchange, the patient must work harder to ventilate the remaining functional alveoli. Over time, this increased work leads to respiratory muscle fatigue, further compromising ventilation. The reduced compliance also explains the reduced vital capacity seen on pulmonary function testing—even with maximal inspiratory effort, the patient cannot fully expand the stiffened lungs. The other options don't accurately describe the mechanical effects of pulmonary fibrosis. Guyton attributes the extra work of breathing in late tuberculosis to widespread fibrosis and less functional tissue, not toxins driving the respiratory center. Late tuberculosis worsens gas exchange, through lost membrane area, thicker membranes and abnormal V/Q ratios, rather than improving it. Walling off forms fibrous tubercles rather than widening airways; the extra work comes from fibrosis throughout the lungs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's first late-TB effect is increased work by the respiratory muscles, from widespread fibrosis and less functional tissue, with reduced vital and breathing capacity. FA: fibrosis lowers compliance (lung harder to fill). COMLEX: The pulmonary OMM lab — obstructive or restrictive disease raises work of breathing from about 2–3% of energy expenditure to as much as 30–35%; OMT aims to improve rib and diaphragm mechanics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — the respiratory muscles and chest-wall mechanics that do the work of breathing","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Guyton attributes the extra work of breathing in late tuberc","C":"Late tuberculosis worsens gas exchange, through lost membran","E":"Walling off forms fibrous tubercles rather than widening air"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q10_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Causes of hypoxia - oxygenation, transport, cellular utilization","batch":1,"stem":"A physician encounters five patients with hypoxia from different causes: Patient A has COPD with V/Q mismatch; Patient B has carbon monoxide poisoning; Patient C has cyanide poisoning; Patient D lives at high altitude; Patient E has severe anemia. Which patients have hypoxia due to inadequate oxygen transport to tissues rather than problems with oxygenation or cellular utilization?","choices":{"A":"Patients B and E (transport problems)","B":"Patients A and D (pulmonary causes)","C":"Patient B only (hemoglobin dysfunction)","D":"Patient C only (cellular enzyme poisoning)","E":"All five patients (hypoxia always involves transport problems)"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hypoxia can be classified into five major categories: (1) Inadequate oxygenation (atmospheric deficiency like Patient D, or hypoventilation); (2) Pulmonary disease (V/Q mismatch like Patient A, impaired diffusion, or increased shunt); (3) Venous-to-arterial shunts; (4) Inadequate O₂ transport to tissues by blood; and (5) Inadequate tissue capability to use O₂ (cellular enzyme poisoning like Patient C). Patients B and E both have inadequate O₂ transport problems: Patient B with carbon monoxide poisoning has hemoglobin that is bound to CO instead of O₂ (forming carboxyhemoglobin), drastically reducing the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity even though arterial PO₂ may be normal. Patient E with severe anemia has insufficient hemoglobin available to carry adequate oxygen to tissues, again reducing oxygen delivery despite potentially normal arterial PO₂. In contrast, Patient A has a pulmonary oxygenation problem (V/Q mismatch preventing adequate blood oxygenation), Patient C has impaired cellular utilization (cyanide blocks cytochrome oxidase, preventing cells from using delivered oxygen), and Patient D has atmospheric deficiency (low inspired PO₂ at altitude). The distinction is important because oxygen therapy helps oxygenation and some transport problems but not cellular utilization defects. The other options incorrectly categorize these hypoxia types.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's hypoxia classification — (1) inadequate oxygenation in the lungs (low atmospheric O2, hypoventilation, pulmonary disease, venous-to-arterial shunt), (2) inadequate O2 transport (anemia or abnormal hemoglobin, circulatory deficiency, edema), (3) inadequate tissue use (cyanide, enzyme deficiency). COMLEX: FA — CO lowers SaO2 and O2 content with a normal PaO2; anemia lowers content with normal SaO2 and PaO2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 11 — carbon monoxide's effect on hemoglobin and O2-carrying capacity","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"In contrast, Patient A has a p","C":"Patient E with severe anemia h","E":"In contrast, Patient A has a p"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q11_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Causes of hypoxia - oxygenation, transport, cellular utilization","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old man is exposed to cyanide gas in an industrial accident. He develops severe symptoms despite his arterial PO₂ being 95 mm Hg (normal) and his hemoglobin saturation being 98% (normal). He remains profoundly hypoxic at the cellular level. What mechanism explains his cellular hypoxia despite adequate oxygen delivery?","choices":{"A":"Cyanide causes bronchoconstriction, preventing oxygen from reaching alveoli","B":"Cyanide blocks cytochrome oxidase, so tissues cannot use the delivered oxygen","C":"Cyanide binds hemoglobin more tightly than carbon monoxide, preventing oxygen loading","D":"Cyanide causes severe anemia by destroying red blood cells","E":"Cyanide increases oxygen consumption so rapidly that delivery cannot keep up"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cyanide poisoning causes a unique form of hypoxia: inadequate tissue capability to use oxygen. Cyanide binds to and blocks cytochrome oxidase (also called cytochrome c oxidase or complex IV), the terminal enzyme in the electron transport chain located in mitochondria. This enzyme is essential for cellular respiration—it catalyzes the final step of oxidative phosphorylation where electrons are transferred to oxygen to form water, with ATP production. When cytochrome oxidase is blocked, tissues cannot utilize oxygen for aerobic metabolism even when plenty of oxygen is delivered to cells. This explains why arterial PO₂ and hemoglobin saturation remain normal (oxygen is loaded onto hemoglobin normally and delivered to tissues normally), but cellular hypoxia is severe (cells cannot extract or use the delivered oxygen). The result is that venous blood returns with abnormally high oxygen content because tissues couldn't extract it, and cells must rely on anaerobic metabolism, producing lactic acidosis. This type of hypoxia is also called \"histotoxic hypoxia\" because the toxin directly impairs cellular oxidative machinery. Oxygen therapy provides no benefit because the problem isn't oxygen availability but rather the cellular machinery to use it. The other options don't describe cyanide's mechanism. Cyanide does not cause bronchoconstriction; oxygen is delivered normally but cannot be used. Cyanide does not bind hemoglobin; it blocks cytochrome oxidase, and hemoglobin saturation is normal. Cyanide does not cause anemia; hemoglobin saturation is normal. Cyanide stops oxygen use rather than increasing it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cyanide blocks cytochrome oxidase so tissues cannot use O2 even when plenty is available — the classic inadequate-utilization hypoxia. FA: cyanide inhibits complex IV; PaO2 normal, curve normal, ↑ lactate with anion gap metabolic acidosis; bright red venous blood and bitter almond breath. COMLEX: FA — 100% O2 is ineffective; give hydroxocobalamin, nitrites or sodium thiosulfate, and suspect it in fire victims.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 6 — intracellular PO2, metabolic rate and the O2 cells actually use","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Cyanide does not cause bronchoconstriction; oxygen is delive","C":"Cyanide does not bind hemoglobin; it blocks cytochrome oxida","D":"Cyanide does not cause anemia; hemoglobin saturation is norm","E":"Cyanide stops oxygen use rather than increasing it."}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q12_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Causes of hypoxia - oxygenation, transport, cellular utilization","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student categorizes different causes of hypoxia to understand when oxygen therapy will be effective. She creates a list including: atmospheric hypoxia at altitude, hypoventilation from neuromuscular disease, V/Q mismatch from pneumonia, severe anemia, carbon monoxide poisoning, and cyanide poisoning. For which conditions would supplemental oxygen therapy be LEAST effective?","choices":{"A":"Atmospheric hypoxia and hypoventilation (can increase inspired PO₂)","B":"V/Q mismatch and hypoventilation (can improve alveolar oxygenation)","C":"Atmospheric hypoxia and V/Q mismatch (can increase alveolar PO₂)","D":"Hypoventilation and cyanide poisoning (a ventilation problem and a cellular utilization problem)","E":"Severe anemia and cyanide poisoning (transport and utilization problems with normal PO₂)"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Understanding hypoxia classifications helps predict oxygen therapy effectiveness. Oxygen therapy is LEAST effective for: (1) Severe anemia—the problem is insufficient hemoglobin to carry oxygen; increasing inspired O₂ doesn't add more hemoglobin. Arterial PO₂ may already be normal, and hemoglobin that exists is already ~98% saturated, so supplemental O₂ adds negligible oxygen content (only tiny amount dissolved in plasma). (2) Cyanide poisoning—tissues cannot use delivered oxygen due to blocked cytochrome oxidase; supplemental O₂ provides essentially no benefit. Guyton (Ch 43) groups abnormal hemoglobin transport of O₂, as in carbon monoxide poisoning, with anemia as conditions in which O₂ therapy is of much less value, although CO poisoning is still treated with 100% or hyperbaric O₂ to speed displacement of CO from hemoglobin (Guyton Ch 41; First Aid 2025). In contrast, O₂ therapy is highly effective for atmospheric hypoxia (directly corrects low inspired PO₂), hypoventilation (increases alveolar PO₂ despite low ventilation), and V/Q mismatch (increases alveolar PO₂ gradient, compensating for diffusion/ventilation problems). Understanding these distinctions is crucial for appropriate therapy selection. Atmospheric hypoxia with hypoventilation, V/Q mismatch with hypoventilation, and atmospheric hypoxia with V/Q mismatch list conditions that respond well. Hypoventilation with cyanide poisoning pairs the utilization defect with hypoventilation, in which a person breathing 100% O₂ moves five times as much O₂ into the alveoli with each breath (Guyton Ch 43).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton — O2 therapy is of much less value in anemia, abnormal hemoglobin, circulatory deficiency or physiological shunt, because alveolar O2 is already normal (though 7–30% extra O2 can travel dissolved), and gives no measurable benefit when tissues cannot use O2. COMLEX: FA — in cyanide poisoning give antidotes, since 100% O2 is ineffective.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — hemoglobin's O2-carrying capacity and O2 delivery","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Atmospheric hypoxia with hypov","D":"Hypoventilation with cyanide p"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q13_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Oxygen therapy value in different types of hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with pulmonary edema has arterial PO₂ of 52 mm Hg due to impaired alveolar-capillary diffusion. Her physician starts oxygen therapy, increasing her inspired oxygen fraction from 21% (room air) to 100%. How does supplemental oxygen improve oxygenation in impaired diffusion?","choices":{"A":"Oxygen therapy directly removes fluid from alveoli, restoring normal diffusion distance","B":"Oxygen stimulates respiratory muscles to breathe faster, improving ventilation","C":"High inspired O₂ raises alveolar PO₂ from ~100 to ~600 mm Hg, widening the gradient","D":"Supplemental oxygen decreases pulmonary capillary blood flow, allowing more time for diffusion","E":"High oxygen concentrations directly repair the damaged alveolar-capillary membrane"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Oxygen therapy is extremely beneficial in diffusion-impaired hypoxia because it dramatically increases the pressure gradient driving oxygen diffusion from alveoli to blood. Normally, breathing room air (21% O₂) produces alveolar PO₂ of ~100 mm Hg, with a diffusion gradient of ~60 mm Hg (difference between alveolar PO₂ 100 mm Hg and mixed venous PO₂ 40 mm Hg entering pulmonary capillaries). When a patient breathes 100% O₂, alveolar PO₂ increases to ~600 mm Hg, creating a diffusion gradient of ~560 mm Hg—an increase of more than 800% (9-fold). By Fick's law, the rate of gas diffusion across a membrane is directly proportional to the pressure gradient. This massively increased gradient means that even though the alveolar-capillary membrane is thickened or damaged (in pulmonary edema, interstitial lung disease, or pulmonary fibrosis), the much steeper pressure gradient can compensate, driving enough oxygen across the impaired membrane to significantly improve arterial oxygenation. Clinical data shows blood in pulmonary edema patients picks up O₂ three to four times as rapidly with 100% O₂ therapy compared to room air. The other options describe mechanisms that either don't occur or aren't the primary benefit. Oxygen does not remove alveolar fluid; it raises alveolar PO2 to as high as 600 mm Hg, widening the diffusion gradient. Guyton explains the benefit in diffusion hypoxia by raised alveolar PO2 widening the gradient, not by stimulating the respiratory muscles. Oxygen does not decrease capillary blood flow; the benefit comes from raised alveolar PO2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In diffusion hypoxia O2 therapy can raise alveolar PO2 from about 100 to as high as 600 mm Hg, lifting the alveolar-to-blood gradient from about 60 to as high as 560 mm Hg — more than 800%; in pulmonary edema blood picks up O2 three to four times as rapidly. COMLEX: Supplemental O2 buys time while the cause of edema (often cardiogenic) is treated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — the categories of pulmonary edema and their causes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Oxygen does not remove alveolar fluid; it raises alveolar PO","B":"Guyton explains the benefit in diffusion hypoxia by raised a","D":"Oxygen does not decrease capillary blood flow; the benefit c"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q14_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Oxygen therapy value in different types of hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A physician manages four hypoxic patients and must decide who will benefit most from oxygen therapy: Patient A has atmospheric hypoxia from altitude; Patient B has hypoventilation from opioid overdose; Patient C has severe anemia (hemoglobin 6 g/dL); Patient D has cyanide poisoning from smoke inhalation. Which patient(s) will show minimal or no improvement with supplemental oxygen?","choices":{"A":"Patients A and B (atmospheric and ventilation problems benefit most)","B":"Patient C only (anemia limits carrying capacity regardless of PO₂)","C":"All patients benefit equally from supplemental oxygen","D":"Patients C and D (transport and utilization defects, not low PO₂)","E":"Patient D only (cellular enzyme poisoning prevents oxygen use)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Oxygen therapy effectiveness depends on the type of hypoxia. Patients C (anemia) and D (cyanide poisoning) will show minimal improvement: Patient C with severe anemia has insufficient hemoglobin to carry oxygen. Her arterial PO₂ might be normal (~100 mm Hg), and what little hemoglobin exists is already ~98% saturated breathing room air. Supplemental O₂ cannot increase saturation meaningfully (already near maximum), and the small increase in dissolved plasma oxygen (from ~0.3 to ~2 mL O₂/dL blood) doesn't compensate for the massive deficit from low hemoglobin. She needs blood transfusion, not oxygen. Patient D with cyanide poisoning has blocked cytochrome oxidase—tissues cannot use oxygen for cellular respiration regardless of how much is delivered. His arterial PO₂ and saturation may be normal; the problem is cellular utilization. Supplemental O₂ provides no benefit. In contrast, Patients A and B benefit dramatically: Patient A's atmospheric hypoxia is completely corrected by O₂ therapy (directly increases inspired PO₂). Patient B with hypoventilation can move 5× more O₂ into alveoli with each breath when breathing 100% O₂ versus room air, markedly improving oxygenation despite reduced ventilation. Understanding these principles guides appropriate therapy. The other options don't correctly identify both non-responsive cases. Cyanide poisoning (Patient D) also gains little, because O2 therapy gives no measurable benefit when tissues cannot use the O2 delivered.","boardPrep":"Step 1: O2 therapy is 100% effective in atmospheric hypoxia and extremely beneficial in hypoventilation (about five times as much O2 per breath on 100% O2, though it does nothing for the CO2), but of much less value in anemia and none in tissue-utilization hypoxia. COMLEX: Guyton stresses O2 does not correct the hypercapnia of hypoventilation — reversing the cause (e.g., an opioid) does.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — hyperbaric O2, which raises the dissolved O2 that limited hemoglobin cannot","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Cyanide poisoning (Patient D) also gains little, because O2 ","C":"In contrast, Patients A and B "}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q15_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Oxygen therapy value in different types of hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with COPD and severe V/Q mismatch has PO₂ of 55 mm Hg on room air. His physician starts him on supplemental oxygen at 2 L/min via nasal cannula, increasing his inspired oxygen fraction from 21% to approximately 28%. His repeat PO₂ is 72 mm Hg. What mechanism explains the improvement despite continued V/Q mismatch?","choices":{"A":"Supplemental oxygen corrects the underlying V/Q mismatch by improving ventilation to low V/Q regions","B":"Oxygen therapy increases respiratory rate, correcting the V/Q mismatch","C":"Supplemental oxygen causes vasoconstriction in high V/Q regions, redistributing blood flow","D":"Higher oxygen concentrations directly dissolve the mucus plugs causing V/Q mismatch","E":"Higher inspired O₂ raises alveolar PO₂ even in poorly ventilated regions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In V/Q mismatch, some lung regions have low ventilation relative to perfusion—blood flows past poorly ventilated alveoli without adequate oxygenation. Supplemental oxygen improves oxygenation by increasing the PO₂ even in these poorly ventilated regions. Even though ventilation is reduced, the higher oxygen fraction in inspired gas means that what little gas does reach those alveoli has higher PO₂. This benefit occurs across all regions: normally ventilated areas reach even higher PO₂, and poorly ventilated areas achieve better (though still suboptimal) PO₂. The net effect is improved overall arterial oxygenation despite the V/Q mismatch persisting. This explains why oxygen therapy is moderately to highly effective in most pulmonary diseases causing V/Q abnormalities, including pneumonia, pulmonary edema, and COPD. However, care must be taken in COPD patients not to eliminate their hypoxic respiratory drive with excessive supplemental oxygen. Correcting the V/Q mismatch is incorrect—O₂ doesn't fix the ventilation problem. Increased respiratory rate, vasoconstriction in high V/Q regions, and dissolving mucus plugs don't explain the improvement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In V/Q mismatch, raising inspired O2 raises alveolar PO2 even in poorly ventilated units, so blood leaving them carries more O2; only true shunt units (no ventilation) are unaffected. FA: shunt (V/Q = 0) does not respond to 100% O2, whereas regions with some ventilation do. COMLEX: A good response to low-flow O2 favors V/Q mismatch over anatomic shunt.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 12 — VA/Q and its effects on alveolar PO2 and PCO2","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Correcting the V/Q mismatch is","B":"Increased respiratory rate, va"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q16_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Physiological consequences of systemic hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with chronic severe hypoxia (PO₂ chronically 50-55 mm Hg) from interstitial lung disease presents with progressive cognitive difficulties and memory problems. His family reports he seems confused at times and has difficulty concentrating. What physiological mechanism explains his neurological symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Low oxygen delivery causes depressed neuronal metabolic activity and impaired cerebral function","B":"Hypoxia causes brain tissue to produce excessive neurotransmitters, overstimulating neurons","C":"Chronic hypoxia increases cerebral blood flow so much that it causes intracranial hypertension","D":"Hypoxia stimulates excessive ATP production, depleting glucose stores in the brain","E":"Low oxygen levels cause neurons to switch to more efficient aerobic metabolism"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Systemic hypoxia causes depressed mental activity because brain neurons are exquisitely sensitive to oxygen deprivation. The brain has extremely high metabolic demands, consuming ~20% of total body oxygen despite being only ~2% of body weight. Neurons rely almost exclusively on aerobic metabolism (oxidative phosphorylation) to produce the ATP needed for maintaining ion gradients, neurotransmitter synthesis and release, and other energy-dependent processes. When oxygen delivery is insufficient (hypoxia), neuronal oxidative metabolism becomes impaired, reducing ATP production. This energy deficit compromises cellular functions including synaptic transmission, action potential propagation, and maintenance of membrane potentials. The clinical manifestations range from subtle (difficulty concentrating, memory problems, slowed mental processing) in mild-to-moderate hypoxia, to severe (confusion, altered consciousness) in moderate-to-severe hypoxia, to coma and ultimately brain death in severe hypoxia. The brain cannot store oxygen or significantly increase anaerobic metabolism to compensate, making it particularly vulnerable to hypoxic injury. This is why hypoxic patients often present with neurological complaints, and why restoring adequate oxygenation is critical for preserving brain function. Chronic hypoxia can trigger partial compensatory mechanisms (increased cerebral blood flow, increased hemoglobin), but these are often insufficient. The other options incorrectly describe neuronal responses to hypoxia. Guyton says systemic hypoxia mainly causes depressed mental activity, sometimes culminating in coma; it does not describe increased ATP production.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton — less severe hypoxia mainly causes depressed mental activity, sometimes culminating in coma, and reduced work capacity of muscles; severe hypoxia kills cells throughout the body. COMLEX: FA lists headache, confusion and coma among CO poisoning features — the same CNS sensitivity to low O2 delivery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — the acute effects of hypoxia on major organ systems","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Guyton says systemic hypoxia mainly causes depressed mental "}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q17_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Physiological consequences of systemic hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old man experiences acute severe hypoxia after near-drowning. Rescue breathing restores adequate oxygenation within 3 minutes. However, he initially exhibits reduced muscle work capacity and weakness. What cellular mechanism in skeletal muscle explains the reduced work capacity during hypoxia?","choices":{"A":"Inadequate oxygen impairs aerobic ATP production, forcing reliance on less efficient anaerobic glycolysis","B":"Hypoxia causes muscles to preferentially use fat metabolism, which is more efficient than glucose","C":"Low oxygen directly paralyzes the contractile proteins actin and myosin","D":"Hypoxia increases ATP production in muscle, causing muscle stiffness and reduced flexibility","E":"Hypoxia stimulates excessive muscle contraction, causing immediate fatigue"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Skeletal muscle work capacity is reduced during hypoxia because muscles must rely on anaerobic glycolysis for ATP production when oxygen is insufficient for aerobic metabolism. Normally, skeletal muscle generates ATP primarily through oxidative phosphorylation in mitochondria, which is highly efficient: complete oxidation of one glucose molecule via the citric acid cycle and electron transport chain produces ~30-32 ATP molecules. When oxygen is limited, muscle must rely on anaerobic glycolysis, which produces only 2 ATP molecules per glucose—a ~15-fold reduction in efficiency. This means muscles must consume glucose much more rapidly to maintain the same ATP production rate, quickly depleting local glucose and glycogen stores. Additionally, anaerobic glycolysis produces lactate, which accumulates in muscles and blood, contributing to metabolic acidosis and further impairing muscle contractile function. The result is rapid fatigue, reduced force generation, and decreased work capacity. Athletes at high altitude or patients with cardiopulmonary disease limiting oxygen delivery experience this as exercise intolerance and early muscle fatigue. Once adequate oxygenation is restored (as in this patient after rescue breathing), aerobic metabolism resumes, lactate is cleared, and muscle function gradually recovers. The other options don't accurately describe muscle metabolism during hypoxia. Without oxygen, ATP comes from anaerobic glycolysis at reduced levels (Marks), so hypoxia lowers rather than raises muscle ATP production.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reduced muscle work capacity is one of Guyton's two main effects of less severe hypoxia; without enough O2, muscle cannot sustain aerobic ATP production. COMLEX: The pulmonary OMM lab notes that higher respiratory-muscle workload shifts toward anaerobic metabolism, which is less efficient and can contribute to acidosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 4 — exercise and the rise in O2 demand of working muscle","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Without oxygen, ATP comes from anaerobic glycolysis at reduc"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q18_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Physiological consequences of systemic hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A physician lectures medical students about the physiological consequences of systemic hypoxia. She emphasizes two major effects that occur before death: depressed mental activity (sometimes culminating in coma) and reduced work capacity of muscles. Why are the brain and skeletal muscles particularly vulnerable to hypoxia?","choices":{"A":"Brain and muscle have minimal blood flow, making them first to experience oxygen deprivation","B":"Brain and muscle have very high metabolic demands requiring constant aerobic ATP production","C":"Brain and muscle lack mitochondria, preventing any aerobic metabolism","D":"Brain and muscle preferentially use anaerobic metabolism, which is impaired by hypoxia","E":"Brain and muscle store excess oxygen, which depletes rapidly during hypoxia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The brain and skeletal muscles are particularly vulnerable to hypoxia because both tissues have very high metabolic demands and depend heavily on aerobic metabolism for ATP production. The brain has the highest metabolic rate relative to mass of any organ, consuming ~20% of total body oxygen despite being only ~2% of body weight. Neurons require constant ATP production to maintain ion gradients (via Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase), synthesize and release neurotransmitters, and support synaptic transmission—processes that rely almost exclusively on oxidative phosphorylation. Skeletal muscles, especially during activity, also have high energy demands. During exercise, muscle oxygen consumption can increase 20-fold above resting levels. While muscle can temporarily use anaerobic glycolysis, this is inefficient (producing only 2 ATP per glucose versus ~30-32 from aerobic metabolism) and quickly leads to lactate accumulation and fatigue. Tissues with lower metabolic rates (adipose tissue, bone) or better anaerobic capacity (some glycolytic muscle fibers) tolerate hypoxia better. The sensitivity of brain and muscle to hypoxia explains why patients with systemic hypoxia present with confusion, cognitive impairment, muscle weakness, and exercise intolerance. In severe hypoxia, neurological decline progresses to coma, and muscle function is severely compromised. The other options are factually incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's two hallmark effects of systemic hypoxia are depressed mental activity (to coma) and reduced work capacity of muscles; both organs depend on continuous oxidative metabolism. COMLEX: Acute mental change in a hypoxemic patient is a red flag for respiratory failure — the OMM lab adds persistent accessory muscle use as a sign of impending failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — acute effects of hypoxia on the major organ systems","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q19_b1","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Hypoxia vs hypercapnia - coexistence and independence","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student reviews four patients: Patient A (COPD with hypoventilation) shows PO₂ 52 mm Hg, PCO₂ 68 mm Hg; Patient B (high altitude) shows PO₂ 55 mm Hg, PCO₂ 32 mm Hg; Patient C (anemia) shows PO₂ 95 mm Hg, PCO₂ 40 mm Hg; Patient D (cyanide poisoning) shows PO₂ 96 mm Hg, PCO₂ 38 mm Hg. Which patient(s) demonstrate(s) hypoxia and hypercapnia coexisting?","choices":{"A":"Patients A and B (both have low PO₂ and abnormal PCO₂)","B":"Patients B, C, and D (every type of hypoxia also causes hypercapnia)","C":"Patient A only (hypoventilation impairs O₂ intake and CO₂ removal)","D":"All four patients (hypoxia always causes hypercapnia)","E":"No patients (hypoxia and hypercapnia never coexist)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hypoxia and hypercapnia coexist primarily in hypoventilation and circulatory deficiency, but NOT in most other types of hypoxia. Patient A with COPD hypoventilation demonstrates both: hypoxia (PO₂ 52 mm Hg, normal ~80-100) because inadequate ventilation fails to bring sufficient O₂ into alveoli, and hypercapnia (PCO₂ 68 mm Hg, normal 35-45) because inadequate ventilation fails to eliminate CO₂. When alveolar ventilation is reduced, both gas exchange processes are impaired proportionally. Patient B at high altitude has hypoxia (low PO₂ from low atmospheric pressure) but hypocapnia (low PCO₂ 32 mm Hg) because the hypoxia stimulates hyperventilation through peripheral chemoreceptors, increasing CO₂ elimination. Patient C with anemia has hypoxia at the tissue level (inadequate O₂ delivery due to low hemoglobin) but normal arterial PO₂ and PCO₂—the blood gas tensions are normal even though oxygen content is reduced. Patient D with cyanide poisoning has cellular hypoxia (tissues cannot use O₂) but normal arterial PO₂ and PCO₂. This distinction is crucial: hypercapnia indicates that the problem involves ventilation or perfusion, not just oxygen delivery or utilization. CO₂ diffuses 20× faster than O₂, so impaired diffusion alone typically causes hypoxia without hypercapnia. The other options misunderstand when these conditions coexist. Guyton says hypoxia and hypercapnia coexist only when the hypoxia is caused by hypoventilation or circulatory deficiency, not in every hypoxia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton — hypercapnia accompanies hypoxia only when hypoxia is caused by hypoventilation or circulatory deficiency; low inspired O2, too little hemoglobin or poisoned enzymes do not raise CO2, and in diffusion defects any early rise in CO2 drives ventilation that corrects it. COMLEX: FA — hypoxemia at altitude drives hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis, so PaCO2 is low rather than high.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 5 — how O2, CO2 and H+ drive the control of respiration","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Patient B at high altitude has","B":"Patient B at high altitude has","D":"Guyton says hypoxia and hypercapnia coexist only when the hy","E":"Patient A with COPD hypoventil"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q20_b1","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Hypoxia vs hypercapnia - coexistence and independence","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with pulmonary fibrosis has arterial blood gases showing PO₂ of 58 mm Hg and PCO₂ of 38 mm Hg (normal 35-45). Despite significant hypoxia, she does not have hypercapnia. What property of CO₂ versus O₂ explains why impaired diffusion causes hypoxia without hypercapnia?","choices":{"A":"CO₂ is smaller than O₂, allowing it to diffuse through pores that O₂ cannot access","B":"O₂ requires active transport while CO₂ diffuses passively","C":"CO₂ is produced faster than O₂ is consumed, maintaining normal PCO₂ despite diffusion impairment","D":"O₂ binds hemoglobin more tightly than CO₂, slowing its release for diffusion","E":"CO₂ diffuses about 20 times faster than O₂ because it is far more soluble"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Carbon dioxide diffuses approximately 20 times more rapidly than oxygen across biological membranes, including the alveolar-capillary membrane, because CO₂ is much more soluble in tissue fluids and lipid membranes than O₂. This difference in diffusibility means that in conditions causing impaired diffusion (pulmonary fibrosis, interstitial lung disease, pulmonary edema), oxygen transfer becomes significantly impaired (causing hypoxia) while CO₂ transfer often remains adequate (preventing hypercapnia). Even when the respiratory membrane is thickened or surface area is reduced, the high diffusibility of CO₂ allows adequate elimination. If hypercapnia begins to develop, even slightly elevated PCO₂ immediately stimulates central chemoreceptors, increasing ventilation, which corrects the CO₂ level while often being insufficient to correct the O₂ level. This explains the common clinical pattern: patients with diffusion-limited diseases or V/Q mismatch develop hypoxemia relatively early in disease progression, while hypercapnia appears only in very advanced disease when impairment is so severe that even CO₂'s high diffusibility cannot compensate. When hypercapnia does develop, it indicates severe pulmonary impairment. Guyton (Ch 43) also notes that the blood's transport capacity for CO₂ is more than three times that for O₂. The other options don't explain the diffusibility difference. Size is not the reason; CO2 diffuses faster because of high solubility. The reason is the 20-fold greater diffusion of CO2, not its production rate. Hemoglobin binding does not explain it; CO2 is far more soluble.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In diffusion hypoxia serious hypercapnia usually does not occur because CO2 diffuses 20 times as rapidly as O2; any rise in CO2 stimulates ventilation, correcting the hypercapnia but not necessarily the hypoxia. COMLEX: FA — diffusion-limiting disease such as pulmonary fibrosis causes early hypoxia rather than hypercapnia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 8 — diffusion rates of O2 versus CO2","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Size is not the reason; CO2 diffuses faster because of high ","C":"The reason is the 20-fold greater diffusion of CO2, not its ","D":"Hemoglobin binding does not explain it; CO2 is far more solu"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q21_b1","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Hypoxia vs hypercapnia - coexistence and independence","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with severe COPD has PCO₂ of 85 mm Hg (normal 35-45 mm Hg). He is drowsy but arousable. His physician knows that further increases in PCO₂ could be life-threatening. At what PCO₂ levels do specific dangerous effects occur?","choices":{"A":"PCO₂ 60-75: air hunger; 80-100: lethargy/semicoma; 120-150: coma and death","B":"PCO₂ 50-60 mm Hg causes coma; 60-75 mm Hg causes respiratory arrest and death","C":"PCO₂ levels above 60 mm Hg cause hyperventilation that cannot be suppressed","D":"PCO₂ 45-50: lethargy; 50-60: semicoma; above 60 mm Hg: apnea and death","E":"PCO₂ levels do not affect consciousness; only PO₂ matters"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Progressive hypercapnia produces characteristic clinical effects at predictable PCO₂ levels: (1) At PCO₂ 60-75 mm Hg, patients experience severe dyspnea (air hunger) and are breathing as rapidly and deeply as possible. The elevated CO₂ maximally stimulates central and peripheral chemoreceptors, driving respiratory effort to its limit. (2) At PCO₂ 80-100 mm Hg, patients become lethargic and sometimes semicomatose. The very high CO₂ level begins to have direct depressant effects on the central nervous system, impairing consciousness. (3) At PCO₂ 120-150 mm Hg, anesthesia, coma, and death occur. At these extreme levels, CO₂'s depressant effects on the CNS, including the respiratory center, become dominant. A dangerous vicious cycle develops: high CO₂ depresses respiration → ventilation decreases → CO₂ rises further → respiration depressed more → culminating in respiratory arrest and death. This biphasic response (stimulation at moderate elevations, depression at very high levels) is critical to understand. In chronic CO₂ retention (like this COPD patient), renal compensation (bicarbonate retention) partially buffers pH changes, allowing tolerance of higher PCO₂ levels, but the progression described still occurs with acute increases or very severe chronic elevation. This patient at 85 mm Hg is in the lethargy zone and at risk of further deterioration. The other options don't accurately describe the PCO₂-effect relationship. Breathing is maximal at 60 to 75 mm Hg, but Guyton says lethargy follows at 80 to 100 and CO2 depresses respiration at 120 to 150. These ranges are too low; Guyton puts severe dyspnea at 60 to 75, lethargy or semicoma at 80 to 100, and death at 120 to 150 mm Hg.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's hypercapnia ladder — alveolar PCO2 60–75 mm Hg: breathing as hard as possible with severe dyspnea; 80–100: lethargy, sometimes semicoma; 120–150: anesthesia and death, as CO2 now depresses respiration in a vicious cycle. COMLEX: Rising PCO2 with falling alertness signals impending respiratory failure and the need for ventilatory support.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 10 — composite effects of PCO2, pH and PO2 on alveolar ventilation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Breathing is maximal at 60 to 75 mm Hg, but Guyton says leth","D":"These ranges are too low; Guyton puts severe dyspnea at 60 t"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q22_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Atelectasis - lung collapse and effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman undergoes bronchoscopy that reveals a large mucous plug completely obstructing her right lower lobe bronchus. The bronchoscopist explains that if the plug isn't removed, the lobe will collapse within hours. The patient asks why the air doesn't just stay in the blocked lobe. What physiological process causes the trapped air to disappear?","choices":{"A":"The mucous plug physically compresses the air, forcing it back into the bloodstream","B":"Bacterial enzymes in the mucus chemically break down oxygen and nitrogen molecules","C":"Trapped gases diffuse down their gradients into capillary blood and are carried away","D":"The diaphragm creates negative pressure that actively sucks air out through the lung parenchyma","E":"Surfactant absorbs the trapped air and converts it into liquid form"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When an airway is completely obstructed, gases trapped in distal alveoli diffuse into pulmonary capillary blood down their concentration gradients. Oxygen diffuses readily because alveolar PO₂ (~100 mm Hg) greatly exceeds mixed venous PO₂ (~40 mm Hg) in blood entering pulmonary capillaries. CO₂ also diffuses but adds little to total volume. Nitrogen, though less soluble, slowly diffuses as well. The net effect is progressive removal of alveolar gas into the bloodstream, which carries it away systemically. This absorption occurs within minutes to hours depending on initial gas composition and blood flow. Once sufficient gas is absorbed, the alveoli either collapse (if tissue is compliant) or fill with transudated fluid (if tissue is stiff). The rate of absorption is faster if the patient was breathing high oxygen concentrations before obstruction because oxygen is absorbed much more rapidly than nitrogen. This principle explains why preoxygenation before anesthesia induction can accelerate atelectasis if airway obstruction occurs. The other options describe mechanisms that don't occur physiologically. The diaphragm cannot pull gas out through lung tissue; Guyton describes the trapped air as absorbed by the blood flowing in the pulmonary capillaries. Guyton says trapped air is absorbed by the blood flowing in the pulmonary capillaries, not pushed into it by the plug. Trapped air is absorbed by pulmonary capillary blood, not broken down by bacterial enzymes. Trapped air is absorbed by pulmonary capillary blood; surfactant lowers surface tension and does not liquefy gas.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trapped alveolar gas is absorbed by the blood flowing through the pulmonary capillaries within minutes to hours; pliable lung then collapses, while rigid fibrotic lung develops very negative alveolar pressure that pulls edema fluid in. COMLEX: FA — incentive spirometry and PEEP reduce postoperative obstructive atelectasis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — atelectasis types and their causes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Guyton says trapped air is absorbed by the blood flowing in ","B":"Trapped air is absorbed by pulmonary capillary blood, not br","E":"Trapped air is absorbed by pulmonary capillary blood; surfac"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q23_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Atelectasis - lung collapse and effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A neonatologist treats a 26-week premature infant with respiratory distress syndrome. Despite mechanical ventilation, the infant's lungs show diffuse atelectasis on chest X-ray. Analysis of tracheal aspirate fluid reveals surface tension 5 times higher than normal. Which cellular deficiency most directly explains the elevated surface tension?","choices":{"A":"Type I pneumocytes fail to provide adequate structural support for alveolar walls","B":"Alveolar macrophages don't produce enough surfactant proteins","C":"Pulmonary capillary endothelial cells have immature tight junctions","D":"Clara cells in the bronchioles secrete excessive mucus instead of surfactant","E":"Type II pneumocytes produce insufficient surfactant due to developmental immaturity"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Type II alveolar pneumocytes are the cells responsible for synthesizing and secreting pulmonary surfactant, a complex mixture of phospholipids (primarily dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine) and proteins that reduces alveolar surface tension. In premature infants, type II pneumocytes are developmentally immature and produce inadequate amounts of surfactant; surfactant production accelerates only after about the 35th week of gestation (Robbins Ch 10). This deficiency leaves the surface tension of the alveolar lining fluid high (about 50 dynes/cm, several times the 5–30 dynes/cm with surfactant), creating powerful inward collapsing forces that overcome the structural support of alveolar walls. The resulting widespread atelectasis characterizes respiratory distress syndrome (hyaline membrane disease). Fetal lung maturity can be assessed by measuring surfactant components in amniotic fluid. Treatment includes exogenous surfactant administration (derived from animal or synthetic sources) instilled directly into airways, along with antenatal corticosteroids given to mothers to accelerate fetal lung maturation. Type I pneumocytes provide surface area for gas exchange but don't produce surfactant. Alveolar macrophages perform phagocytosis, not surfactant production. Endothelial tight junctions and Clara cells incorrectly identify the surfactant-producing cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surfactant comes from type II alveolar epithelial cells; in premature infants secretion is so depressed that alveolar surface tension becomes several times normal. FA: type II pneumocytes secrete surfactant and serve as precursors of type I cells; NRDS risk rises with prematurity, maternal diabetes and C-section. COMLEX: Antenatal corticosteroids and surfactant therapy have greatly improved survival (Guyton).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — alveolar cell types and their roles","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Alveolar macrophages perform p"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q24_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Atelectasis - lung collapse and effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with complete right lung atelectasis from bronchial carcinoma has arterial blood gases showing PO₂ of 58 mm Hg. A pulmonary angiogram demonstrates that blood flow through his collapsed right lung is only 15% of normal despite the lung receiving normal perfusion before collapse. What dual mechanism explains the markedly reduced blood flow through atelectatic lung?","choices":{"A":"Surfactant deficiency causes direct vasoconstriction independent of oxygen levels","B":"Inflammatory mediators from the tumor directly paralyze pulmonary smooth muscle","C":"Mechanical vessel compression from collapse plus hypoxic vasoconstriction reduce flow","D":"Atelectasis stimulates excessive nitric oxide production, causing paradoxical vasodilation","E":"Blood viscosity increases dramatically in regions of atelectasis, slowing flow"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Atelectatic lung experiences markedly increased pulmonary vascular resistance through two complementary mechanisms. First, mechanical compression: as lung volume decreases during collapse, the pulmonary vessels (arteries, capillaries, veins) are compressed, folded, and kinked as they are no longer held open by normal lung architecture. This physical compression increases resistance to blood flow. Second, hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (Euler-Liljestrand mechanism): collapsed alveoli are not ventilated, so alveolar PO₂ approaches mixed venous levels (~40 mm Hg). This regional hypoxia triggers vasoconstriction of small pulmonary arterioles in the affected area—a protective reflex that diverts blood away from poorly ventilated regions toward better-ventilated areas to optimize V/Q matching. In Guyton's example (Fig. 43.7), only about one-sixth of the blood still passes through the collapsed lung, close to this patient's value. However, compensation is incomplete—some blood still perfuses the collapsed lung without oxygenation, creating physiological shunt that contributes to systemic hypoxemia. This explains why his PO₂ is only 58 mm Hg despite having a functional left lung. The other options don't describe the actual mechanisms. Guyton says the resistance rise in atelectasis comes from the collapse compressing and folding the vessels plus hypoxic vasoconstriction, not from surfactant deficiency. Guyton says the resistance rise in atelectasis comes from the collapse compressing and folding the vessels plus hypoxic vasoconstriction, not from paralyzed smooth muscle. Guyton says the resistance rise in atelectasis comes from vessel compression and hypoxic vasoconstriction, so flow is greatly reduced rather than raised by vasodilation. Guyton says the resistance rise in atelectasis comes from vessel compression and hypoxic vasoconstriction, not from increased blood viscosity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Collapse compresses and folds the vessels of the atelectatic lung, and alveolar hypoxia adds vasoconstriction; most blood is diverted to the aerated lung (five-sixths in Guyton's example), so aortic desaturation is only mild despite losing a whole lung's ventilation. COMLEX: FA — hypoxic vasoconstriction shifts blood away from poorly ventilated regions, limiting the shunt.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 13 — physiological shunt and dead space","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Guyton says the resistance rise in atelectasis comes from the collapse compressing and folding the vessels plus hypoxic vasoconstriction, not from surfactant de","B":"Guyton says the resistance rise in atelectasis comes from the collapse compressing and folding the vessels plus hypoxic vasoconstriction, not from paralyzed smo","D":"Guyton says the resistance rise in atelectasis comes from vessel compression and hypoxic vasoconstriction, so flow is gr","E":"Guyton says the resistance rise in atelectasis comes from vessel compression and hypoxic vasoconstriction, not from incr"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q25_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Asthma - immunologic process with mast cells","batch":1,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with acute asthma exacerbation after exposure to cat dander. She reports her breathing difficulty began within 5 minutes of exposure and has progressively worsened over 30 minutes. Which mediator released by mast cells is primarily responsible for the prolonged bronchoconstriction lasting several hours?","choices":{"A":"Histamine causes the majority of prolonged bronchoconstriction lasting hours","B":"Leukotrienes (slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis) sustain constriction","C":"Bradykinin is the sole mediator responsible for all asthmatic bronchospasm","D":"Eosinophilic chemotactic factor directly constricts bronchiolar smooth muscle","E":"Prostaglandin E2 is the primary bronchoconstrictor in allergic asthma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The prolonged bronchoconstriction characteristic of asthma is primarily caused by slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis (SRS-A), which is a mixture of leukotrienes (LTC₄, LTD₄, LTE₄). When mast cells are activated by antigen binding to IgE, they release multiple mediators with different time courses. Histamine causes immediate bronchoconstriction within seconds to minutes but its effect is brief. In contrast, leukotrienes produce powerful, sustained bronchoconstriction lasting several hours—hence the name \"slow-reacting.\" Leukotrienes are synthesized from membrane phospholipids via the 5-lipoxygenase pathway and are released more gradually than preformed histamine. This sustained effect explains why asthma attacks can persist for hours despite removal from allergen exposure and why leukotriene receptor antagonists (montelukast) or 5-lipoxygenase inhibitors (zileuton) are effective controller medications. Leukotrienes also increase vascular permeability (causing bronchiolar wall edema) and stimulate mucus secretion, further contributing to airway obstruction. The combination of histamine's rapid effects and leukotrienes' prolonged effects creates the biphasic response in allergic asthma. The histamine option underestimates histamine's duration. Bradykinin, eosinophilic chemotactic factor, and prostaglandin E2 incorrectly identify the primary mediator of sustained bronchospasm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton singles out slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis — a mixture of leukotrienes — as the most important mast-cell product in the asthmatic bronchiole, causing edema, thick mucus and smooth-muscle spasm. FA: leukotrienes are released in the immediate type I phase. COMLEX: FA — montelukast and zafirlukast block CysLT1 receptors and are especially good for aspirin-induced and exercise-induced asthma; zileuton blocks 5-lipoxygenase.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 1 — the drug classes, including leukotriene modifiers, used in asthma","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The histamine option underesti"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q26_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Asthma - immunologic process with mast cells","batch":1,"stem":"A 19-year-old college student with allergic asthma undergoes skin prick testing to identify his specific allergens. Testing reveals positive reactions to ragweed, dust mites, and mold spores. His immunologist explains that he produces abnormally high levels of a specific antibody class. Which immunoglobulin mediates his allergic asthma responses?","choices":{"A":"IgE binds to mast cells and mediates type I hypersensitivity reactions","B":"IgG is the primary antibody responsible for allergic asthma","C":"IgM provides the initial response to allergens in asthmatic patients","D":"IgA in airway secretions triggers mast cell degranulation","E":"IgD on B cell surfaces directly causes bronchospasm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Allergic asthma is mediated by IgE antibodies in a classic type I hypersensitivity reaction. Individuals with allergic tendencies (atopy) have a genetic predisposition to produce abnormally large amounts of IgE in response to environmental antigens (allergens). When first exposed to allergens like ragweed pollen, their B cells differentiate into plasma cells that produce allergen-specific IgE antibodies. These IgE molecules bind via their Fc portions to high-affinity Fc receptors on mast cells located in lung interstitium near bronchioles. The mast cells become \"sensitized\"—coated with allergen-specific IgE, waiting for subsequent allergen exposure. Upon re-exposure, allergen binds to and cross-links the IgE molecules on mast cell surfaces, triggering degranulation and release of mediators (histamine, leukotrienes, bradykinin, eosinophilic chemotactic factor) that cause bronchospasm, edema, and mucus secretion. Serum IgE levels are typically elevated in allergic individuals, and allergen-specific IgE can be measured to identify sensitizations. Anti-IgE therapy (omalizumab) binds free IgE, preventing it from attaching to mast cells—an effective treatment for severe allergic asthma. IgG mediates type II and III hypersensitivity but not allergic asthma. IgM, IgA, and IgD don't play primary roles in allergic asthma pathophysiology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The allergic person forms abnormally large amounts of IgE, which bind mast cells in the lung interstitium near bronchioles and small bronchi. FA: type I hypersensitivity, tested by skin test or serum allergen-specific IgE; allergic asthma and anaphylaxis are its classic examples. COMLEX: FA links asthma with atopy and triggers such as allergens, viral URIs and stress.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 7 — genetic susceptibility and environmental factors in asthma","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"IgM, IgA, and IgD don't play p"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q27_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Asthma - immunologic process with mast cells","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist counsels an asthmatic patient about the difference between allergen sensitivity and actual allergen exposure. The patient asks why she doesn't have asthma symptoms constantly if her mast cells are always \"primed\" with IgE antibodies. What prevents continuous mast cell degranulation in the absence of allergen?","choices":{"A":"Mast cells automatically degranulate every 24 hours regardless of allergen presence","B":"IgE antibodies spontaneously detach from mast cells within minutes, requiring constant re-binding","C":"Cross-linking of IgE by allergen binding is required to trigger mast cell degranulation","D":"Mast cells are only present in lungs during active allergen exposure","E":"Histamine levels are too low to cause symptoms without allergen stimulation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mast cell degranulation requires cross-linking of surface-bound IgE antibodies by multivalent allergen molecules. In the sensitized state, allergen-specific IgE antibodies are already attached to mast cells via Fc receptors, but this binding alone doesn't trigger degranulation. The mast cells remain stable and quiescent. Degranulation only occurs when allergen (antigen) molecules bind to the Fab portions of multiple adjacent IgE antibodies simultaneously, physically cross-linking them together on the cell surface. This cross-linking brings together intracellular signaling components, activating phospholipase C, increasing intracellular calcium, and triggering the degranulation cascade that releases preformed mediators and initiates synthesis of leukotrienes. A single allergen molecule with multiple epitopes can bind and cross-link multiple IgE antibodies. Without allergen present, the IgE antibodies sit harmlessly on mast cells without triggering degranulation, which is why sensitized individuals only experience symptoms during allergen exposure. This requirement for cross-linking provides specificity—only the allergen that matches the bound IgE can trigger that particular mast cell. Automatic degranulation is incorrect—degranulation is stimulus-dependent. Spontaneous IgE detachment is wrong—IgE stays bound to mast-cell Fc receptors. Mast cells present only during exposure and low baseline histamine are factually incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FA — in the immediate type I reaction, antigen crosslinks preformed IgE on presensitized mast cells, causing degranulation; IgE bound alone does not trigger release. COMLEX: FA — omalizumab binds mostly unbound serum IgE and blocks its binding to FcεRI, used in allergic asthma with ↑ IgE resistant to inhaled glucocorticoids and LABAs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — the therapeutic rationale for each asthma drug class","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Mast cells present only during"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q28_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Tuberculosis - late-stage effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"In tuberculosis, macrophages invade the infected tissue and fibrous tissue walls off the lesion to form a tubercle, limiting spread of the bacilli. According to Guyton, in approximately what fraction of people with untreated tuberculosis does this walling-off process fail, allowing spread through the lungs with formation of large abscess cavities?","choices":{"A":"About 50%","B":"About 30%","C":"About 90%","D":"About 15%","E":"About 3%"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Guyton describes the tubercle as the product of macrophage invasion and fibrous walling-off, a process that protects against extension of the infection; if tuberculosis is not treated, the walling-off fails in about 3% of people in whom the disease develops, and the bacilli spread throughout the lungs, often destroying lung tissue and forming large abscess cavities. Late-stage disease then shows widespread fibrosis and reduced functional lung tissue, with increased work of breathing, reduced vital capacity, reduced diffusing capacity, and abnormal ventilation-perfusion ratios. Values of 15%, 30%, 50%, and 90% all greatly overstate the failure rate Guyton gives.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Macrophage invasion and fibrous walling-off form the tubercle; if TB is untreated this fails in about 3% of people in whom disease develops, and bacilli spread with extreme destruction and large abscess cavities. COMLEX: Late TB therefore leaves fibrosis, lost lung tissue, more work of breathing and reduced diffusing capacity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 49 → Obj 4 — primary versus reactivation TB pathophysiology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q29_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Tuberculosis - late-stage effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with healed tuberculosis but extensive residual fibrosis exercises on a treadmill during cardiopulmonary exercise testing. Her oxygen saturation drops from 96% at rest to 84% with moderate exercise. What mechanism explains exercise-induced desaturation in fibrotic lung disease?","choices":{"A":"Shorter capillary transit time in exercise leaves too little time for O₂ to cross thick membranes","B":"Exercise causes bronchospasm that physically blocks air from entering the lungs","C":"Increased cardiac output during exercise preferentially shunts blood away from functioning lung tissue","D":"Exercise suppresses respiratory drive, causing hypoventilation and reduced alveolar oxygen","E":"Skeletal muscles consume all available oxygen before it reaches the lungs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Exercise-induced desaturation in pulmonary fibrosis comes from the combination of a thickened alveolar-capillary membrane and a shortened capillary transit time. At rest blood spends about 0.8 second in the pulmonary capillaries, and normally it is almost saturated after only the first third of that time (Guyton Ch 39, Ch 41), so there is a large reserve. A fibrotic membrane slows O₂ transfer and uses up much of that reserve, but at rest the time is still enough to reach reasonable saturation (96%). In exercise the transit time can fall to as little as 0.3 second; across the thickened membrane this is no longer enough for equilibration, so blood leaves the capillaries incompletely oxygenated and saturation falls (84%). This diffusion limitation is a hallmark of interstitial lung disease, which is why these patients tolerate rest but desaturate with exertion. Supplemental oxygen helps by widening the alveolar-to-capillary PO₂ gradient. Exercise does not cause bronchospasm or suppress respiratory drive in fibrosis, rising cardiac output does not divert blood away from functioning lung, and muscles do not take up O₂ before it reaches the lungs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normally blood saturates in the first third of the capillary, leaving a threefold time reserve; a thickened membrane uses up that reserve, so the shortened transit of exercise leaves blood incompletely oxygenated. COMLEX: FA — in fibrosis O2 is diffusion limited and fails to equilibrate by the end of the capillary.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 12 — capillary transit time and gas exchange efficiency","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Exercise does not cause bronch"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q30_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Tuberculosis - late-stage effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist examines lung tissue from a patient who died from complications of tuberculosis. The tissue shows dense fibrosis, calcified granulomas, and areas of cavitation. Compared with normal lungs, these tuberculosis-affected lungs are markedly heavier. What accounts for the increased lung weight?","choices":{"A":"Increased air trapping causes lung hyperinflation, increasing total mass","B":"Fibrotic scar tissue and calcification add mass to the lung parenchyma","C":"Pulmonary edema fluid accumulation is the primary cause of weight increase","D":"Tubercle bacilli themselves contribute significantly to total lung mass","E":"Compensatory hypertrophy of remaining functional alveoli increases tissue mass"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The increased lung weight in chronic tuberculosis results from extensive deposition of fibrotic (scar) tissue throughout the lung parenchyma. Fibrosis involves replacement of normal delicate lung architecture with dense collagen and other extracellular matrix proteins. Each tubercle (granuloma) begins with macrophage infiltration and becomes surrounded by fibrous tissue attempting to wall off the infection. In untreated or extensive disease, large areas of lung become replaced by fibrotic tissue. Additionally, many tubercles undergo dystrophic calcification—deposition of calcium salts in necrotic tissue—further increasing mass. Dense fibrous tissue and calcified granulomas are much heavier than normal air-filled alveoli and thin-walled capillaries. While cavitation (abscess formation) destroys some tissue, the overall balance favors mass increase from fibrosis. This increased tissue density is visible on chest CT as areas of increased attenuation. The heavy, stiff, fibrotic lungs are difficult to inflate, explaining the restrictive physiology. Importantly, this fibrotic tissue is non-functional—it doesn't participate in gas exchange, contributing to reduced diffusing capacity despite increased total tissue mass. Air trapping is incorrect—TB causes restriction, not air trapping. Edema fluid might contribute acutely but isn't the chronic cause. The bacilli themselves and compensatory hypertrophy don't explain the weight increase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Late tuberculosis is characterized by many areas of fibrosis throughout the lungs with reduced functional lung tissue — fibrous scar replaces the delicate air-filled parenchyma. COMLEX: FA — primary TB forms a Ghon complex (Ghon focus plus hilar nodes) that heals by fibrosis and calcification, while reactivation produces fibrocaseous cavitary upper-lobe lesions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 49 → Obj 4 — primary and reactivation TB pathophysiology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q31_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Causes of hypoxia - oxygenation, transport, cellular utilization","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with long-standing alcohol use disorder and poor nutrition is diagnosed with beriberi from vitamin B₁ deficiency. His arterial PO₂ and hemoglobin are normal. Guyton cites beriberi as a special example within his classification of hypoxia. Into which category does it fall?","choices":{"A":"Inadequate tissue capability to use O₂","B":"Inadequate oxygenation from hypoventilation","C":"Inadequate O₂ transport from anemia","D":"Venous-to-arterial shunting of blood","E":"Localized circulatory deficiency"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton's third category of hypoxia is inadequate tissue capability of using O₂, with cyanide poisoning of cytochrome oxidase as the classic cause; he adds that deficiencies of cellular oxidative enzymes can do the same, and that in beriberi several important steps in tissue use of O₂ and formation of CO₂ are compromised because of vitamin B deficiency. Hypoventilation and venous-to-arterial shunting fall under inadequate oxygenation of the blood in the lungs, which would lower arterial PO₂. Anemia and localized circulatory deficiency fall under inadequate O₂ transport to the tissues; his hemoglobin is normal, and nothing points to a regional perfusion defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's inadequate-utilization hypoxia — cyanide is classic, but deficient oxidative enzymes do the same; in beriberi, vitamin B1 deficiency compromises tissue O2 use and CO2 formation. FA: thiamine (TPP) is a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase and α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase. COMLEX: Give thiamine before glucose in malnourished patients with alcohol use disorder to avoid precipitating Wernicke encephalopathy (FA).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 6 — intracellular PO2 and cellular O2 use","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q32_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Causes of hypoxia - oxygenation, transport, cellular utilization","batch":1,"stem":"A toxicology fellow asks why cyanide poisoning causes rapid death while chronic anemia causes gradual functional decline, even though both can result in similar reductions in oxygen availability to tissues. What fundamental difference in pathophysiology explains the different time courses?","choices":{"A":"Cyanide causes immediate hemoglobin destruction while anemia develops gradually","B":"Anemia allows oxygen delivery through increased cardiac output while cyanide paralyzes the heart","C":"Cyanide halts ATP production at once; anemia allows compensation through higher extraction","D":"Cyanide poisoning causes irreversible brain damage while anemia causes reversible changes","E":"Anemic patients have normal venous oxygen content while cyanide patients have low venous oxygen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The fundamental difference is that cyanide causes immediate cellular energy crisis by blocking ATP production, while anemia allows compensatory mechanisms time to partially adapt. Cyanide binds cytochrome oxidase (complex IV of the electron transport chain), immediately preventing cells from using oxygen for oxidative phosphorylation. Mitochondrial ATP production ceases within seconds, causing rapid cellular dysfunction, especially in high-energy-demand tissues (brain, heart). Cells must rely on anaerobic glycolysis alone, producing ~15-fold less ATP per glucose molecule, quickly depleting energy stores and causing rapid organ failure and death (minutes to hours). In contrast, chronic anemia develops gradually, allowing compensatory mechanisms: increased cardiac output delivers more blood (and thus more total oxygen) despite low hemoglobin concentration; rightward shift of the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve (via increased 2,3-DPG) enhances oxygen unloading at tissues; increased capillary density in some tissues improves extraction efficiency; tissues develop increased tolerance to lower oxygen tensions. While these mechanisms are incomplete, they allow survival and function at hemoglobin levels that would be poorly tolerated if they developed acutely. Additionally, in anemia, tissues can extract proportionally more oxygen from delivered blood, resulting in low venous oxygen content. In cyanide poisoning, tissues cannot extract oxygen despite adequate delivery, resulting in paradoxically high venous oxygen content. The other options contain factual errors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cyanide blocks cytochrome oxidase so cells cannot use the O2 they receive; in anemia O2 transport falls but tissues can extract a larger fraction (utilization coefficient normally about 25%). FA: cyanide raises lactate with an anion gap acidosis and needs antidotes, since 100% O2 is ineffective. COMLEX: Anemic patients do not become cyanotic — there is not enough hemoglobin for 5 g/dL to be deoxygenated (Guyton).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 4 — how O2 extraction rises to meet demand","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"In cyanide poisoning, tissues "}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q33_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Causes of hypoxia - oxygenation, transport, cellular utilization","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student creates a diagram categorizing causes of hypoxia to predict which patients benefit from supplemental oxygen. She groups: (Group 1) high-altitude exposure, hypoventilation from opioids, pneumonia with V/Q mismatch; (Group 2) severe anemia, carbon monoxide poisoning; (Group 3) cyanide poisoning, beriberi. What distinguishes these three groups?","choices":{"A":"Group 1 involves cardiac problems; Groups 2 and 3 involve primary lung problems","B":"All three groups benefit equally from supplemental oxygen therapy","C":"Group 1 has low tissue PO₂; Groups 2 and 3 have normal tissue PO₂","D":"Group 1 affects only children; Groups 2 and 3 affect only adults","E":"Group 1 lacks oxygenation, Group 2 lacks transport, Group 3 lacks utilization"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This categorization correctly separates hypoxia types by the level at which oxygen delivery fails. Group 1 (altitude, hypoventilation, pneumonia) represents inadequate oxygenation of blood—the problem is getting oxygen from atmosphere into arterial blood. Altitude reduces inspired PO₂; hypoventilation reduces alveolar PO₂; pneumonia causes V/Q mismatch and impaired diffusion. All result in low arterial PO₂. These respond excellently to supplemental O₂ which directly corrects the oxygenation problem. Group 2 (anemia, CO poisoning) represents inadequate oxygen transport—blood reaches lungs and may achieve normal or near-normal PO₂, but inadequate hemoglobin (anemia) or non-functional hemoglobin (carboxyhemoglobin from CO) prevents adequate oxygen content in blood reaching tissues. Anemia responds poorly to supplemental O₂ because the problem is carrying capacity, not oxygenation; CO poisoning is the exception — high-concentration O₂ speeds displacement of CO from hemoglobin. Group 3 (cyanide, beriberi) represents inadequate cellular utilization—oxygen is delivered to tissues adequately (normal arterial PO₂ and content), but cellular enzyme systems cannot use it (cyanide blocks cytochrome oxidase; beriberi/thiamine deficiency impairs multiple oxidative enzymes). These don't respond to supplemental O₂ at all—increasing delivered oxygen doesn't help if cells cannot use it. This classification is clinically crucial for predicting treatment response and selecting appropriate interventions. The other options incorrectly characterize these groups. Guyton classes altitude, hypoventilation and V/Q mismatch as inadequate oxygenation of blood in the lungs, not cardiac problems, and anemia as a transport defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's three tiers — inadequate oxygenation of blood in the lungs, inadequate O2 transport by the blood, inadequate tissue capability to use O2 — predict O2 therapy: great value in the first, much less in the second, none in the third. COMLEX: FA — CO lowers SaO2 and content, methemoglobin lowers both, and cyanide leaves all O2 values normal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 11 — carbon monoxide as a hemoglobin-level transport defect","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Guyton classes altitude, hypoventilation and V/Q mismatch as","B":"Anemia responds poorly to supp"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q34_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Oxygen therapy value in different types of hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with COPD and chronic hypercapnia (baseline PCO₂ 58 mm Hg) is admitted with pneumonia. His PO₂ is 48 mm Hg on room air. The intern wants to start high-flow oxygen to normalize his PO₂. Why does the attending physician recommend cautious low-flow oxygen instead?","choices":{"A":"High-flow oxygen will cause immediate oxygen toxicity and pulmonary fibrosis","B":"Removing hypoxic drive in chronic CO₂ retainers can worsen hypoventilation and PCO₂","C":"Low oxygen saturation is beneficial and should be maintained to stimulate erythropoiesis","D":"High-flow oxygen causes bronchoconstriction in COPD patients, worsening airflow obstruction","E":"Oxygen therapy has no benefit in patients with chronic lung disease"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Guyton Ch 42 explains why a chronic CO₂ retainer's breathing leans on O₂ lack: CO₂ excites the respiratory center strongly for the first few hours, but over the next 1 to 2 days that effect falls to about one-fifth, because the kidneys raise blood HCO₃⁻ and HCO₃⁻ diffuses into the cerebrospinal fluid, returning H⁺ near normal around the respiratory neurons — so a high PCO₂ has a potent acute but only a weak chronic effect on drive. Guyton makes the matching point for altitude: once the center has lost much of its CO₂/H⁺ sensitivity, low O₂ can drive ventilation far more strongly. Abolishing that hypoxic stimulus with high-flow O₂ can therefore let ventilation fall and PCO₂ rise; Guyton Ch 43 adds that O₂ therapy in hypoventilation provides no benefit for the excess CO₂, and that at a PCO₂ of 120 to 150 mm Hg the excess CO₂ begins to depress respiration in a vicious cycle. The aim is enough O₂ to relieve dangerous hypoxemia, given cautiously at low flow while PCO₂ and mental status are watched. ('Hypoxic drive' is the clinical label; neither First Aid 2025 nor the course chapters use the term.) Immediate O₂ toxicity and fibrosis overstate an acute risk; deliberately leaving saturation low to stimulate erythropoiesis is dangerous; O₂ does not cause bronchoconstriction; and O₂ therapy clearly benefits hypoxemic lung disease (Guyton Ch 43).","boardPrep":"Step 1: In hypoventilation O2 therapy moves about five times as much O2 into the alveoli per breath but provides no benefit for the excess CO2 (Guyton); at very high levels CO2 itself begins to depress respiration. COMLEX: Because O2 corrects the hypoxemia but not the CO2 retention, the blood gas, not pulse oximetry, is what tracks ventilation in a chronic CO2 retainer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 7 — how central and peripheral chemoreceptors drive respiration","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Immediate O₂ toxicity and fibr"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q35_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Oxygen therapy value in different types of hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis has arterial blood gases showing PO₂ 52 mm Hg on room air. After starting oxygen at 4 L/min via nasal cannula (FiO₂ ~36%), her repeat PO₂ is 78 mm Hg. Why does oxygen therapy effectively improve her hypoxemia despite her lung disease?","choices":{"A":"Oxygen directly dissolves the fibrotic tissue, restoring normal lung architecture","B":"Supplemental oxygen stimulates her respiratory muscles to breathe more deeply","C":"Higher oxygen concentration reduces pulmonary vascular resistance, improving perfusion","D":"Raising alveolar PO₂ widens the O₂ gradient across the thickened membranes","E":"Oxygen prevents further fibrosis progression, allowing existing tissue to heal"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Oxygen therapy is highly effective for hypoxemia caused by impaired diffusion (as in pulmonary fibrosis, interstitial lung disease, or pulmonary edema) because it dramatically increases the alveolar-to-capillary PO₂ gradient that drives oxygen diffusion. In pulmonary fibrosis, the alveolar-capillary membrane is thickened by collagen deposition, increasing diffusion distance. On room air (FiO₂ 21%), alveolar PO₂ is ~100 mm Hg, creating a gradient of ~60 mm Hg (alveolar PO₂ 100 - mixed venous PO₂ 40). This limited gradient, combined with increased diffusion distance, results in incomplete oxygenation and hypoxemia (PO₂ 52 mm Hg). When FiO₂ increases to 36%, alveolar PO₂ rises to ~200 mm Hg, creating a gradient of ~160 mm Hg—nearly tripling the driving force for diffusion. With 100% O₂, alveolar PO₂ reaches ~600 mm Hg, gradient ~560 mm Hg. By Fick's law, diffusion rate is directly proportional to the concentration gradient. This increased gradient compensates for the increased diffusion distance, allowing adequate oxygen to cross the thickened membrane and achieve acceptable arterial oxygenation (PO₂ 78 mm Hg). This principle explains why diffusion-impaired patients often require only modest supplemental oxygen to achieve significant improvement. The other options describe mechanisms that don't occur or aren't the primary benefit. Guyton explains O2 benefit in diffusion hypoxia by raising alveolar PO2 and widening the gradient, not by lowering vascular resistance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In diffusion-impaired hypoxia O2 therapy raises alveolar PO2 several-fold, multiplying the gradient that drives O2 across the thickened membrane. COMLEX: FA — IPF shows progressive dyspnea, dry cough, velcro crackles and clubbing; supplemental O2 treats the hypoxemia while the fibrosis itself progresses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and other restrictive diseases","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Guyton explains O2 benefit in diffusion hypoxia by raising a"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q36_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Oxygen therapy value in different types of hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A critical care physician manages three hypoxic ICU patients and must allocate one remaining oxygen concentrator: Patient X (massive pulmonary embolism with V/Q mismatch, PO₂ 50 mm Hg); Patient Y (severe anemia from GI bleeding, hemoglobin 5 g/dL, PO₂ 95 mm Hg); Patient Z (methemoglobinemia from dapsone, PO₂ 88 mm Hg). Which patient would benefit MOST from supplemental oxygen?","choices":{"A":"Patient Y needs oxygen most urgently because anemia causes the most severe hypoxia","B":"Patient Z needs oxygen most because methemoglobinemia is immediately fatal without treatment","C":"Patient X would benefit most because V/Q mismatch responds well to increased inspired oxygen","D":"All three patients benefit equally from supplemental oxygen","E":"None of these patients benefit from supplemental oxygen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Patient X with pulmonary embolism causing V/Q mismatch would benefit most from supplemental oxygen. Large pulmonary emboli create areas of high V/Q (ventilated but not perfused, increasing dead space) and may cause areas of low V/Q in remaining perfused regions (from atelectasis, edema, or redistribution of blood flow). The low V/Q regions have reduced alveolar PO₂, causing blood to pass without adequate oxygenation. Supplemental O₂ increases alveolar PO₂ even in these poorly ventilated regions, improving oxygen uptake and raising arterial PO₂. This should raise his arterial PO₂ and improve tissue oxygen delivery. Patient Y with severe anemia has normal arterial PO₂ (95 mm Hg) and hemoglobin saturation (~97%), meaning the limited hemoglobin present is already fully saturated. Supplemental O₂ won't significantly increase oxygen content because you can't increase saturation beyond ~100%, and dissolved O₂ contributes minimally (~0.3 to ~2 mL/dL, vs 15-20 mL/dL normally carried by hemoglobin). Patient Y needs blood transfusion. Patient Z with methemoglobinemia (oxidized hemoglobin unable to carry oxygen) has moderate PO₂ (88 mm Hg), somewhat responsive to supplemental O₂, but primary treatment is methylene blue to reduce methemoglobin back to normal hemoglobin. While all three have tissue hypoxia, only Patient X's problem is directly addressable with oxygen therapy. The other options don't reflect differential response to O₂ therapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: O2 therapy helps most where the problem is getting O2 into blood (V/Q mismatch, diffusion), and much less where O2 is already normal in the alveoli but transport fails (anemia, abnormal hemoglobin). FA: with dead space (V/Q = ∞, e.g., PE), 100% O2 improves PaO2 as long as dead space is < 100%; methemoglobin cyanosis does not improve with O2. COMLEX: Treat methemoglobinemia with methylene blue and anemia from bleeding with transfusion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — the pathophysiology of pulmonary embolism","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Supplemental O₂ won't signific","D":"While all three have tissue hy","E":"While all three have tissue hy"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q37_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Physiological consequences of systemic hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A mountain climber ascends rapidly to 14,000 feet (atmospheric PO₂ ~95 mm Hg) without acclimatization. Within hours, he develops confusion, difficulty with simple calculations, and poor judgment—symptoms of acute mountain sickness affecting the central nervous system. What property of neurons makes them exceptionally vulnerable to acute hypoxia?","choices":{"A":"Neurons can switch to anaerobic metabolism more efficiently than other cells","B":"The brain has minimal oxygen requirements compared to other organs","C":"Neurons contain large oxygen reserves that deplete slowly during hypoxia","D":"Cerebral blood flow decreases during hypoxia, reducing oxygen delivery","E":"Neurons depend almost exclusively on aerobic metabolism and cannot store oxygen"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Neurons are exceptionally vulnerable to hypoxia because they depend almost exclusively on aerobic metabolism (oxidative phosphorylation) for ATP production and cannot store oxygen. The brain accounts for ~20% of total body oxygen consumption despite being only ~2% of body weight, reflecting the enormous energy demands of neuronal function. Neurons require constant ATP production to maintain transmembrane ion gradients (via Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase), synthesize and release neurotransmitters, support action potential propagation, and maintain synaptic transmission. Unlike skeletal muscle, which can temporarily rely on anaerobic glycolysis (though inefficient at 2 ATP per glucose vs 30-32 from aerobic metabolism), neurons have very limited anaerobic capacity. The brain stores minimal glucose and essentially no oxygen, so it depends on continuous delivery. During acute hypoxia (as at altitude), inadequate oxygen delivery rapidly impairs ATP-dependent processes, causing neuronal dysfunction manifesting as cognitive impairment, confusion, poor judgment, memory problems, and in severe cases, altered consciousness or coma. Chronic hypoxia triggers partial compensation (increased cerebral blood flow, increased hemoglobin), but acute hypoxia overwhelms these mechanisms before they can develop. This explains why hypoxic patients present with neurological symptoms and why the brain is the limiting organ in severe hypoxia. The other options are factually incorrect—neurons cannot switch to anaerobic metabolism efficiently, the brain has high oxygen demands, there are no oxygen reserves, and cerebral blood flow actually increases during hypoxia (attempting compensation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypoxia first depresses mental activity (Guyton), the CNS being among the tissues most dependent on continuous O2 delivery. FA: ascent lowers PIO2 and PaO2, causing hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis; altitude sickness brings headache, nausea, fatigue, lightheadedness and sleep disturbance. COMLEX: FA — renal HCO3− excretion compensates for the alkalosis and can be augmented with acetazolamide.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 6 — high-altitude cerebral edema, its mechanisms and presentation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The other options are factuall","D":"The other options are factuall"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q38_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Physiological consequences of systemic hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A 65-year-old man with severe COPD has chronic hypoxemia (PO₂ typically 55-60 mm Hg). His family reports progressive personality changes, memory loss, and difficulty with complex tasks over recent months. Brain MRI shows diffuse cortical atrophy without focal lesions. What mechanism explains progressive brain dysfunction from chronic hypoxia?","choices":{"A":"The brain adapts to hypoxia by reducing its metabolic rate, causing reversible dysfunction","B":"Chronic hypoxia increases cerebral oxygen consumption, overwhelming available supply","C":"Sustained low O₂ delivery causes cumulative neuronal injury and cell death","D":"Hypoxia causes excessive ATP production that is toxic to neurons","E":"Chronic hypoxia has no effect on brain function; his symptoms must have another cause"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Chronic hypoxia causes progressive neuronal injury and death through sustained inadequate oxygen delivery despite compensatory mechanisms. While acute compensatory responses include increased cerebral blood flow (via vasodilation), increased hemoglobin concentration (via erythropoietin stimulation), and increased capillary density, these adaptations are often insufficient to fully normalize oxygen delivery in severe chronic hypoxia (PO₂ 55-60 mm Hg represents ~85-88% saturation). Neurons experiencing chronic energy insufficiency develop cumulative damage: impaired maintenance of ion gradients leads to chronic partial depolarization; reduced ATP availability impairs protein synthesis and cellular repair; increased oxidative stress from struggling mitochondria produces reactive oxygen species; accumulation of intracellular calcium triggers apoptotic pathways. Over months to years, this results in progressive neuronal death, particularly in high-demand regions like cerebral cortex and hippocampus, explaining personality changes, memory loss, and executive dysfunction. The cortical atrophy on MRI reflects actual loss of neuronal tissue. This progressive cognitive decline in chronic lung disease is called \"hypoxemic encephalopathy\" and may partially improve with long-term oxygen therapy (LTOT) if initiated early, but established neuronal loss is irreversible. The cognitive impairment is distinct from acute confusion (which reverses with oxygenation) because it represents structural brain damage. The other options don't explain the progressive neurodegeneration. Guyton says systemic hypoxia mainly causes depressed mental activity, so chronic hypoxia does affect brain function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sustained hypoxia depresses mental activity and work capacity (Guyton) and, if severe, causes cell death. FA: chronic hypoxemia raises erythropoietin and hematocrit, and chronic hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction leads to pulmonary hypertension and RVH. COMLEX: Robbins lists oxygen therapy with smoking cessation and bronchodilators among COPD treatments.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 5 — chronic mountain sickness, the model of chronic hypoxic injury","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Guyton says systemic hypoxia mainly causes depressed mental "}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q39_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Physiological consequences of systemic hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"An exercise physiologist studies healthy volunteers during maximal exercise. He notes that despite skeletal muscles increasing oxygen consumption 20-fold, subjects maintain adequate muscle function. However, when the same subjects exercise at high altitude, they experience rapid muscle fatigue at much lower workloads. What explains muscle fatigue during hypoxic exercise?","choices":{"A":"High altitude causes direct muscle fiber destruction, reducing contractile capacity","B":"Hypoxia increases the efficiency of aerobic metabolism, causing excessive ATP accumulation","C":"Exercise at altitude increases oxygen delivery above sea-level values","D":"Anaerobic glycolysis in hypoxic exercise yields less ATP and more lactate","E":"Muscle blood flow decreases during hypoxic exercise, reducing substrate delivery"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Muscle fatigue during hypoxic exercise results from forced reliance on anaerobic glycolysis, which is dramatically less efficient than aerobic metabolism. At sea level during exercise, even with 20-fold increased oxygen consumption, adequate oxygen delivery allows muscles to primarily use aerobic metabolism: complete oxidation of glucose via glycolysis, citric acid cycle, and electron transport chain produces ~30-32 ATP molecules per glucose. This high efficiency supports sustained muscle work. At altitude, reduced atmospheric PO₂ limits oxygen delivery despite compensatory increases in ventilation and cardiac output. When oxygen availability becomes insufficient for aerobic demand, muscles shift toward anaerobic glycolysis, which produces only 2 ATP per glucose molecule—a ~15-fold reduction in efficiency. To maintain ATP production, muscles must metabolize glucose ~15× faster, rapidly depleting local glucose and glycogen stores. Additionally, anaerobic glycolysis produces lactate as the end product. Lactate accumulation in muscles and blood causes metabolic acidosis (reducing intracellular pH), which directly impairs contractile protein function and further reduces ATP production from glycolysis (by inhibiting rate-limiting enzymes). The combination of inadequate ATP production and lactate accumulation causes rapid muscle fatigue, reducing exercise capacity at altitude. Acclimatization partially improves this through increased hemoglobin, improved oxygen delivery, and enhanced muscle oxidative capacity. The other options don't explain hypoxic muscle fatigue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reduced work capacity of muscles is a principal effect of hypoxia (Guyton); at altitude lower arterial O2 limits aerobic ATP, so fatigue comes at lower workloads. COMLEX: FA — altitude acclimatization adds ventilation, erythropoietin-driven Hct, 2,3-BPG and mitochondria, gradually restoring exercise capacity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — the acute effects of hypoxia on organ systems","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q40_b1","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Hypoxia vs hypercapnia - coexistence and independence","batch":1,"stem":"A physician evaluates three patients with different pulmonary conditions: Patient L (ARDS with severe shunt) shows PO₂ 50 mm Hg, PCO₂ 35 mm Hg; Patient M (neuromuscular weakness with hypoventilation) shows PO₂ 55 mm Hg, PCO₂ 65 mm Hg; Patient N (isolated diffusion defect from mild interstitial disease) shows PO₂ 68 mm Hg, PCO₂ 38 mm Hg. What explains the presence or absence of hypercapnia in these three patients?","choices":{"A":"All three patients have hypercapnia because hypoxia always causes CO₂ retention","B":"None have hypercapnia because CO₂ diffuses slowly and accumulates despite adequate ventilation","C":"Only Patient L has hypercapnia because shunt prevents CO₂ elimination","D":"All three have the same PCO₂ because pulmonary disease affects O₂ and CO₂ identically","E":"Only Patient M, because hypoventilation impairs CO₂ elimination; the others keep normal PCO₂"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The presence or absence of hypercapnia depends on whether the underlying problem affects ventilation. Patient M with neuromuscular weakness causes hypoventilation—reduced minute ventilation impairs both oxygen intake and CO₂ elimination proportionally. The alveolar gas equation shows that reduced ventilation decreases alveolar PO₂ (causing hypoxia) and increases alveolar PCO₂ (causing hypercapnia). This patient demonstrates the classic coexistence of hypoxia and hypercapnia. Patient L with ARDS has severe shunt (blood bypassing ventilated alveoli). While this causes severe hypoxia (PO₂ 50 mm Hg), it doesn't cause hypercapnia: shunted blood never meets alveolar gas, so CO₂ diffusibility is irrelevant here; instead, hypoxia stimulates increased ventilation (via peripheral chemoreceptors), which further enhances CO₂ elimination, potentially causing hypocapnia (low PCO₂). Patient L's PCO₂ 35 mm Hg actually represents slight hypocapnia. Patient N with diffusion defect develops mild hypoxia (PO₂ 68 mm Hg) because oxygen transfer is impaired. However, CO₂'s much higher diffusibility means it crosses the thickened membrane adequately, maintaining normal PCO₂ (38 mm Hg). Any tendency toward CO₂ retention would immediately stimulate increased ventilation, correcting PCO₂ while being insufficient to correct PO₂. Only hypoventilation and circulatory failure cause both hypoxia and hypercapnia to coexist. The other options misunderstand when hypercapnia occurs. CO2 diffuses rapidly, not slowly. Guyton says serious hypercapnia usually does not accompany a diffusion defect because CO2 diffuses 20 times as rapidly as O2, so the two gases are not affected identically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypercapnia joins hypoxia when ventilation itself falls (hypoventilation) or flow fails (circulatory deficiency); in shunt or diffusion problems any rise in CO2 stimulates ventilation, which keeps PCO2 normal or low. COMLEX: FA — shunt and diffusion limitation raise the A-a gradient, while pure hypoventilation keeps it normal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — ARDS pathogenesis, the classic shunt physiology","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"CO2 diffuses rapidly, not slowly.","C":"While this causes severe hypox","D":"Guyton says serious hypercapnia usually does not accompany a"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q41_b1","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Hypoxia vs hypercapnia - coexistence and independence","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student asks why patients with mild-to-moderate COPD typically have hypoxia without hypercapnia, while patients with severe COPD often have both. The attending physician explains that this relates to disease progression. At what point in COPD progression does hypercapnia typically develop?","choices":{"A":"Hypercapnia appears immediately with any degree of airway obstruction","B":"Hypercapnia appears late, when ventilatory capacity is overwhelmed","C":"Hypercapnia occurs first, before hypoxia, in COPD progression","D":"COPD never causes hypercapnia because CO₂ diffuses too rapidly","E":"Hypercapnia and hypoxia always appear simultaneously in COPD"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hypercapnia in COPD develops late in disease progression when ventilatory capacity is overwhelmed. In early-to-moderate COPD, patients develop hypoxia from V/Q mismatch, impaired diffusion, and increased dead space, but maintain normal or even low PCO₂ because they can compensate by increasing minute ventilation (breathing faster/deeper). Although breathing requires increased work due to airway obstruction, their respiratory muscles can still generate adequate ventilation to eliminate CO₂, which diffuses 20× more readily than O₂. As COPD progresses to severe disease, several factors eventually cause hypercapnia: (1) Respiratory muscle fatigue—chronic increased work of breathing exhausts respiratory muscles, reducing ventilatory capacity; (2) Overwhelming dead space—when most of each breath ventilates dead space, even maximum respiratory effort cannot maintain adequate alveolar ventilation; (3) Severe airway obstruction with extreme flow limitation; (4) Air trapping—dynamic hyperinflation places respiratory muscles at mechanical disadvantage, reducing efficiency. When ventilatory capacity becomes insufficient to eliminate CO₂ despite maximum effort, hypercapnia develops. The appearance of hypercapnia (PCO₂ >45 mm Hg) in COPD indicates advanced disease and worse prognosis. Some patients become chronic CO₂ retainers with compensated respiratory acidosis (elevated bicarbonate). The other options don't accurately describe COPD progression. This understanding guides prognosis and interventions like mechanical ventilation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Because any rise in CO2 drives ventilation that corrects it, hypercapnia appears only when ventilation can no longer keep up — late in COPD. Robbins: the bronchitis-dominant 'blue bloater' shows hypercapnia, hypoxemia and mild cyanosis, while the emphysema-dominant 'pink puffer' has near-normal resting gases. COMLEX: Robbins — cor pulmonale is common in the bronchitic pattern, uncommon until end stage in emphysema.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 3 — the pink puffer and blue bloater phenotypes of COPD","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"In early-to-moderate COPD, pat","C":"In early-to-moderate COPD, pat","E":"In early-to-moderate COPD, pat"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q42_b1","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Hypoxia vs hypercapnia - coexistence and independence","batch":1,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with COPD exacerbation has PCO₂ of 92 mm Hg and appears lethargic but arousable. His respiratory rate is 28 breaths/min. The resident notes this seems contradictory—high CO₂ should stimulate breathing, yet the patient seems sedated. What explains this paradox?","choices":{"A":"The patient is comfortable and relaxed despite the elevated CO₂","B":"High CO₂ always causes increased alertness and anxiety, never sedation","C":"At PCO₂ above ~80 mm Hg, CO₂ directly depresses the CNS despite chemoreceptor drive","D":"The tachypnea (rapid breathing) indicates the patient is fully alert and responding normally","E":"Sedation is impossible with elevated CO₂ because stimulation always dominates"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient demonstrates the biphasic dose-response relationship between PCO₂ and CNS effects. At moderate elevations (PCO₂ 45-75 mm Hg), CO₂ stimulates central and peripheral chemoreceptors, increasing respiratory drive and causing dyspnea, anxiety, and increased respiratory rate. The patient is hyperventilating in an attempt to \"blow off\" CO₂. However, at very high PCO₂ levels (>80 mm Hg), CO₂'s direct depressant effects on the central nervous system begin to dominate. High CO₂ causes CNS acidosis (CO₂ readily crosses the blood-brain barrier and forms carbonic acid), which depresses neuronal function, causing lethargy, confusion, and eventually coma. This patient at PCO₂ 92 mm Hg is in the transition zone where chemoreceptor stimulation still drives tachypnea (respiratory rate 28) but CNS depression causes altered consciousness (lethargy). Further CO₂ increases can trigger a dangerous vicious cycle: CNS depression → reduced respiratory drive → worsening hypoventilation → more CO₂ retention → more CNS depression → respiratory arrest → death. At PCO₂ 120-150 mm Hg, anesthesia and death typically occur. This patient requires urgent intervention (non-invasive ventilation or intubation) to support ventilation and reduce CO₂. Simply stimulating the patient to \"breathe harder\" is ineffective because respiratory muscle fatigue and CNS depression prevent adequate response. The other options don't explain the paradoxical CNS depression with high CO₂. Guyton says a PCO2 of 80 to 100 mm Hg makes a person lethargic and sometimes semicomatose, not comfortable.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton — CO2 stimulates breathing only up to a point; at very high levels the excess CO2 begins to depress the CNS and respiration rather than stimulate it, a vicious cycle of more CO2 and less breathing that ends in respiratory death. COMLEX: A drowsy COPD patient with a high PCO2 needs ventilatory support rather than reassurance from a fast respiratory rate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 10 — composite effects of PCO2, pH and PO2 on alveolar ventilation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Guyton says a PCO2 of 80 to 100 mm Hg makes a person letharg","D":"This patient at PCO₂ 92 mm Hg ","E":"However, at very high PCO₂ lev"}}],"17":[{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q01_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Etiology, Pathology, Morphology, and Clinical Features of Major Obstructive Pulmonary Diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with a 45 pack-year smoking history presents with progressive dyspnea on exertion and chronic productive cough. Spirometry shows FEV₁/FVC ratio of 0.62. Which pathophysiologic mechanism best explains the airflow limitation in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Increased elastic recoil of alveolar walls causing premature airway closure","B":"Restrictive fibrosis reducing total lung capacity","C":"Dynamic expiratory airway collapse from loss of radial traction","D":"Bronchial smooth muscle contraction causing reversible obstruction","E":"Decreased respiratory drive from chronic hypercapnia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The patient has COPD (FEV₁/FVC < 0.7), which causes airflow obstruction through multiple mechanisms. In emphysema, destruction of alveolar walls leads to loss of elastic recoil and loss of radial traction that normally keeps airways open. During expiration, when intrathoracic pressure exceeds intraluminal airway pressure, unsupported airways collapse prematurely. In chronic bronchitis, mucous gland hyperplasia, inflammation, and fibrosis directly increase airway resistance. These processes cause dynamic airway collapse and prolonged expiration. Fibrosis causes restriction, with proportionate falls in TLC and FEV1 so the FEV1/FVC ratio stays normal, not the 0.62 seen here. Reversible bronchospasm distinguishes asthma; this smoker with a productive cough and FEV1/FVC below 0.7 fits COPD.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — small airways are held open by the elastic recoil of the lung parenchyma; loss of elastic tissue in the alveolar walls around respiratory bronchioles reduces radial traction, so they collapse in expiration. COMLEX: FA — tobacco is the most important risk factor; chronic bronchitis and emphysema often coexist, and exacerbations are often triggered by respiratory infections.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — the principle of maximum expiratory flow and dynamic airway compression","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"In emphysema, destruction of a","B":"Fibrosis causes restriction, with proportionate falls in TLC","D":"Reversible bronchospasm distinguishes asthma; this smoker wi"}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q02_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Etiology, Pathology, Morphology, and Clinical Features of Major Obstructive Pulmonary Diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with chronic bronchitis undergoes bronchial biopsy. Pathologic examination reveals mucous gland hyperplasia. Which measurement would best quantify the severity of this morphologic change?","choices":{"A":"FEV₁/FVC ratio on spirometry","B":"Degree of squamous metaplasia in bronchial epithelium","C":"Number of goblet cells per high-power field","D":"Reid index (gland layer thickness / wall thickness)","E":"Extent of alveolar wall destruction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Reid index is a pathologic measurement used to quantify mucous gland hyperplasia in chronic bronchitis. It is calculated as the ratio of the thickness of the submucosal mucous glands to the thickness of the wall between the epithelium and cartilage. Normally the Reid index is approximately 0.4 and it increases in chronic bronchitis, usually in proportion to disease severity and duration (Robbins Ch 15); First Aid 2025 gives a Reid index > 50% in chronic bronchitis. This objective measurement correlates with the clinical finding of chronic productive cough. Goblet cells are epithelial cells, and their increase in small airways is a separate change from enlargement of the submucosal glands, which is what the Reid index measures. FEV1/FVC measures expiratory airflow; Robbins measures mucous gland enlargement by the Reid index. Robbins quantifies mucous gland hyperplasia in chronic bronchitis by the Reid index (gland layer to wall thickness), not by squamous metaplasia. Robbins quantifies mucous gland hyperplasia in chronic bronchitis by the Reid index; alveolar wall destruction is a feature of emphysema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reid index = thickness of the mucous gland layer ÷ thickness of the wall between epithelium and cartilage; Robbins gives about 0.4 as normal, increased in chronic bronchitis in proportion to severity; FA cutoff is > 50%. COMLEX: FA — chronic bronchitis is diagnosed clinically by productive cough for ≥ 3 months in ≥ 2 consecutive years.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — bronchial histology, including the submucosal glands","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"FEV1/FVC measures expiratory airflow; Robbins measures mucou","B":"Robbins quantifies mucous gland hyperplasia in chronic bronchitis by the Reid index (gland layer to ","C":"Goblet cells are epithelial ce","E":"Robbins quantifies mucous gland hyperplasia in chronic bronchitis by the Reid index; alveolar wall d"}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q03_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Etiology, Pathology, Morphology, and Clinical Features of Major Obstructive Pulmonary Diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist is educating medical students about distinguishing obstructive from restrictive lung diseases. Which of the following best differentiates obstructive diseases (COPD, asthma) from restrictive diseases (pulmonary fibrosis, sarcoidosis)?","choices":{"A":"Obstructive diseases show FEV₁/FVC < 0.7; restrictive show ≥ 0.7 with reduced TLC","B":"Obstructive diseases cause hypoxemia without hypercapnia, while restrictive diseases cause both","C":"Obstructive diseases result from alveolar wall thickening, while restrictive diseases result from airway narrowing","D":"Obstructive diseases primarily affect upper lobes, while restrictive diseases affect lower lobes","E":"Obstructive diseases show increased diffusing capacity, while restrictive diseases show normal diffusing capacity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The fundamental spirometric distinction between obstructive and restrictive lung diseases is the FEV₁/FVC ratio. Obstructive diseases (COPD, asthma, bronchiectasis) cause disproportionate reduction in expiratory flow, resulting in FEV₁/FVC < 0.7. In contrast, restrictive diseases (pulmonary fibrosis, sarcoidosis) cause proportional reduction in both FEV₁ and FVC, maintaining FEV₁/FVC ≥ 0.7, but with reduced total lung capacity due to stiff lungs or restricted chest expansion. Robbins separates obstructive from restrictive disease by pulmonary function tests (FEV1/FVC below 0.7 versus normal), not by hypoxemia or hypercapnia. This reverses the pathology: obstructive diseases involve airway narrowing or alveolar wall destruction, while restrictive diseases include interstitial fibrosis and chest wall disorders. Robbins separates the two categories by pulmonary function tests, not by lobar distribution. Diffusing capacity is low, not raised, in emphysema, and the categories are separated by the FEV1/FVC ratio.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — an FEV1/FVC below 0.7 generally indicates obstruction; restrictive disease lowers FVC and TLC with a normal (or ↑) ratio. Guyton gives about 80% as the normal ratio. COMLEX: FA — obstructive loops shift left with ↑ RV and TLC; restrictive loops shift right with reduced volumes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 4 — FEV1, FVC and FEV1/FVC with worsening obstruction","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Robbins separates obstructive from restrictive disease by pu","C":"This reverses the pathology: obstructive diseases involve ai","D":"Robbins separates the two categories by pulmonary function t","E":"Diffusing capacity is low, not raised, in emphysema, and the"}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q04_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Etiology, Pathology, and Morphology of Four Main Types of Emphysema","batch":1,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with COPD dies of respiratory failure. Autopsy reveals emphysematous destruction primarily affecting the respiratory bronchioles in the central portions of the acini, with relative sparing of distal alveoli. The lesions are most severe in the upper lobes. Which type of emphysema does this represent?","choices":{"A":"Centriacinar emphysema associated with heavy cigarette smoking","B":"Panacinar emphysema associated with α₁-antitrypsin deficiency","C":"Paraseptal emphysema causing spontaneous pneumothorax","D":"Irregular emphysema adjacent to scarring","E":"Bullous emphysema with large airspace cysts"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This patient has centriacinar (centrilobular) emphysema, the most common form constituting more than 95% of clinically significant cases. In centriacinar emphysema, the central or proximal parts of the acini (formed by respiratory bronchioles) are preferentially affected, while distal alveoli are initially spared. This creates a pattern where both emphysematous and normal airspaces coexist within the same acinus and lobule. Centriacinar emphysema occurs predominantly in heavy smokers and characteristically affects the upper lobes, particularly the apical segments. Panacinar emphysema involves the whole acinus uniformly and is linked to alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, not central acinar destruction with sparing of distal alveoli. Paraseptal emphysema involves the distal acinus near the pleura, not the respiratory bronchioles centrally. Irregular emphysema occurs next to scars and affects the acinus unevenly, not as central acinar destruction with sparing of the distal alveoli. Bullous emphysema is a descriptive term for large subpleural bullae that can occur in any form of emphysema, not a pattern of central acinar destruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Centriacinar emphysema — more than 95% of clinically significant cases, in heavy smokers — involves the central acinus (respiratory bronchioles) and spares distal alveoli; lesions are worse in the upper lobes, especially the apical segments. COMLEX: FA — centriacinar = upper lobes and smoking; panacinar = lower lobes and α1-antitrypsin deficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — bronchioles, including the respiratory bronchioles centriacinar disease destroys","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Panacinar emphysema involves the whole acinus uniformly and ","C":"Paraseptal emphysema involves the distal acinus near the ple","D":"Irregular emphysema occurs next to scars and affects the aci","E":"Bullous emphysema is a descriptive term for large subpleural"}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q05_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Etiology, Pathology, and Morphology of Four Main Types of Emphysema","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with α₁-antitrypsin deficiency and a 10 pack-year smoking history develops progressive dyspnea. CT scan shows uniform enlargement of airspaces throughout the acini, from the level of the respiratory bronchioles to the terminal alveoli, predominantly in the lower lung zones. Which pathophysiologic mechanism best explains the distribution pattern of this patient's emphysema?","choices":{"A":"Upper lobe preferential exposure to inhaled tobacco smoke particulates","B":"Gravity-dependent accumulation of neutrophil elastase in lower zones","C":"Genetic antiprotease deficiency throughout the lung, worsened by smoking","D":"Centriacinar pattern progressing to involve distal alveoli in severe disease","E":"Lower lobe mechanical stress from diaphragmatic contraction during breathing"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient has panacinar (panlobular) emphysema, characteristically associated with α₁-antitrypsin deficiency. α₁-Antitrypsin is a serine protease inhibitor (serpin) that protects lung tissue from neutrophil elastase and other proteases. Genetic deficiency creates a protease-antiprotease imbalance throughout the lungs, allowing unopposed proteolytic destruction of alveolar walls. This results in uniform acinar involvement. Panacinar emphysema preferentially affects lower zones and anterior lung margins, with severity typically greatest at the lung bases. Smoking exacerbates the condition by increasing oxidative stress, inflammation, and protease activity while inactivating residual α₁-antitrypsin. Upper lobe preference with smoke exposure fits centriacinar emphysema; alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency gives panacinar emphysema throughout the lung. A centriacinar pattern that spreads is not what this uniform acinar enlargement shows; it is panacinar from genetic antiprotease deficiency. Robbins ties panacinar emphysema to alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, with unchecked elastase digesting elastic tissue, not to diaphragmatic stress.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Panacinar emphysema is associated with α1-antitrypsin deficiency and exacerbated by smoking — acini uniformly enlarged from respiratory bronchiole to terminal alveoli, worst in the lower zones and bases. FA: AAT is the antiprotease that inhibits neutrophil elastase; its deficiency leaves elastase unopposed. COMLEX: FA — AAT deficiency also causes liver disease, and it is among the causes of cirrhosis that raise hepatocellular carcinoma risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — the pathophysiology of hypoxia and hypercapnia in chronic emphysema","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Upper lobe preference with smoke exposure fits centriacinar ","D":"A centriacinar pattern that spreads is not what this uniform","E":"Robbins ties panacinar emphysema to alpha-1 antitrypsin defi"}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q06_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Etiology, Pathology, and Morphology of Four Main Types of Emphysema","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old previously healthy man presents to the emergency department with sudden onset right-sided chest pain and dyspnea. Chest X-ray reveals a large right-sided pneumothorax. CT scan performed after chest tube placement shows multiple cyst-like airspaces (0.5-2.5 cm in diameter) in the peripheral lung adjacent to the pleura, particularly in the upper lobes. Which type of emphysema is most likely responsible for this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Centriacinar emphysema from undiagnosed smoking history","B":"Panacinar emphysema from α₁-antitrypsin deficiency","C":"Irregular emphysema associated with previous tuberculosis scarring","D":"Paraseptal emphysema causing spontaneous pneumothorax","E":"Compensatory emphysema from congenital lung hypoplasia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Paraseptal (distal acinar) emphysema is the likely cause of spontaneous pneumothorax in young, otherwise healthy adults. In this type, the proximal acinus is normal but the distal part is predominantly involved. The characteristic finding is multiple enlarged airspaces (0.5 cm to >2 cm) that can form cyst-like structures. The emphysema is most striking adjacent to the pleura, along lobular connective tissue septa, and at lobular margins. It typically occurs in the upper lung regions and adjacent to areas of fibrosis or atelectasis. The subpleural location makes these areas prone to rupture, causing spontaneous pneumothorax. Centriacinar emphysema comes from heavy smoking and involves the central acinus, not the subpleural distal acinus. Panacinar emphysema comes from alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency and involves the whole acinus, not the distal acinus near the pleura. Irregular emphysema accompanies scarring and is usually clinically insignificant, and no scarring is described here. Compensatory hyperinflation is alveolar dilation after loss of lung substance elsewhere, whereas paraseptal emphysema underlies spontaneous pneumothorax in young adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distal acinar (paraseptal) emphysema spares the proximal acinus and involves the distal part next to the pleura and septa, mostly in the upper half of the lungs; enlarged spaces from under 0.5 to over 2 cm can form cysts and probably underlie many spontaneous pneumothoraces in young adults. COMLEX: FA — pneumothorax is hyperresonant with ↓ breath sounds; tension pneumothorax deviates the trachea away from the lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 2 — spontaneous, secondary and tension pneumothorax","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Centriacinar emphysema comes from heavy smoking and involves","B":"Panacinar emphysema comes from alpha-1 antitrypsin deficienc","C":"Irregular emphysema accompanies scarring and is usually clin","E":"Compensatory hyperinflation is alveolar dilation after loss "}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q07_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and Contrast Clinical Features of COPD (Pink Puffers and Blue Bloaters)","batch":1,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with long-standing COPD presents to clinic. He appears thin and barrel-chested, sits leaning forward, and breathes through pursed lips with prolonged expiration. He reports minimal cough with scant sputum. Pulse oximetry shows 94% on room air. Pulmonary function tests reveal severely reduced diffusing capacity (DLCO 35% predicted) with marked lung hyperinflation. Which additional finding is most consistent with this clinical presentation?","choices":{"A":"Early development of cor pulmonale with peripheral edema and jugular venous distention","B":"Frequent respiratory infections requiring antibiotic therapy","C":"Weight loss raising concern for underlying malignancy","D":"Hypercapnia (PaCO₂ >50 mm Hg) and chronic cyanosis at rest","E":"Copious sputum production (>30 mL daily) and productive cough as the cardinal symptom"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient is a classic \"pink puffer,\" in whom emphysema dominates the clinical picture. Pink puffers characteristically present with severe dyspnea early in disease, barrel chest from hyperinflation, pursed-lip breathing, prolonged expiration, forward-leaning posture, minimal cough, low diffusing capacity from alveolar wall destruction, and relatively well-preserved oxygenation at rest (hence \"pink\"). Weight loss is common and can be severe enough to suggest occult malignancy. The weight loss results from increased work of breathing, chronic inflammation, and hypermetabolism. These patients maintain relatively normal blood gases through increased ventilatory effort until end-stage disease. Frequent infections point the other way: Robbins lists infections as common in chronic bronchitis but only occasional in emphysema. Cor pulmonale is common in chronic bronchitis but uncommon and end-stage in emphysema-dominant pink puffers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins' 'pink puffer' (emphysema-dominant) — barrel chest, prolonged expiration, hunched forward, pursed lips, slight cough, severe overdistention, low diffusion capacity and relatively normal resting gases; weight loss is common and can be severe enough to suggest occult cancer. COMLEX: Smoking history plus weight loss in COPD warrants evaluation for lung cancer, which shares the same risk factor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 2 — tobacco, industrial hazards and air pollution as lung cancer risk factors","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Cor pulmonale is common in chronic bronchitis but uncommon a","D":"These patients maintain relati","E":"Pink puffers characteristicall"}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q08_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and Contrast Clinical Features of COPD (Pink Puffers and Blue Bloaters)","batch":1,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with COPD presents with worsening dyspnea. He has a persistent productive cough with copious sputum that began 15 years ago. Physical examination reveals central cyanosis, elevated jugular venous pressure, and bilateral ankle edema. Arterial blood gas shows pH 7.34, PaCO₂ 58 mm Hg, PaO₂ 52 mm Hg. Echocardiogram demonstrates right ventricular hypertrophy and pulmonary hypertension. Which pathophysiologic mechanism best explains this patient's cor pulmonale?","choices":{"A":"Loss of elastic recoil causing dynamic airway collapse and air trapping","B":"Chronic hypoxemia stimulating pulmonary vasoconstriction and increasing right ventricular afterload","C":"Left ventricular failure causing pulmonary venous congestion and secondary pulmonary hypertension","D":"Destruction of pulmonary capillaries reducing cross-sectional area of pulmonary vascular bed","E":"Increased cardiac output from hypermetabolic state overwhelming pulmonary circulation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient is a \"blue bloater\" (chronic bronchitis dominant) with cor pulmonale. Blue bloaters develop chronic hypoxemia (PaO₂ 52 mm Hg) and hypercapnia early in the disease due to ventilation-perfusion mismatch from mucous plugging, airway inflammation, and hypoventilation. Chronic alveolar hypoxia triggers hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction, increasing pulmonary vascular resistance and right ventricular afterload. Over time, this leads to right ventricular hypertrophy, dilation, and eventual right heart failure (cor pulmonale) with systemic venous congestion (elevated JVP, peripheral edema). The cyanosis (\"blue\" bloater) reflects chronic hypoxemia and increased deoxygenated hemoglobin. Loss of capillary beds also contributes to pulmonary hypertension in COPD, but Robbins ties it to alveolar wall destruction in emphysema; this patient's copious sputum, cyanosis and hypercapnia are the bronchitic picture driven by hypoxemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins' 'blue bloater' (bronchitis-dominant) — persistent productive cough with hypercapnia, hypoxemia and mild cyanosis; cor pulmonale is common, versus uncommon until end stage in emphysema. FA: chronic hypoxic vasoconstriction → ↑ PVR → pulmonary hypertension and RVH. COMLEX: FA defines cor pulmonale as isolated right heart failure from a pulmonary cause — JVD, peripheral edema and congestive hepatomegaly follow the raised venous pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — pulmonary hypertension, causes and mechanisms","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q09_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and Contrast Clinical Features of COPD (Pink Puffers and Blue Bloaters)","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist is teaching medical students to distinguish emphysema-predominant from chronic bronchitis-predominant COPD. Which statement best characterizes the fundamental difference in elastic recoil between these two phenotypes?","choices":{"A":"Pink puffers have normal elastic recoil with increased airway resistance, while blue bloaters have low elastic recoil","B":"Pink puffers have increased elastic recoil causing restrictive physiology, while blue bloaters have normal elastic recoil","C":"Pink puffers have low elastic recoil from alveolar wall destruction, while blue bloaters have normal elastic recoil","D":"Both phenotypes have equally reduced elastic recoil from shared pathophysiology","E":"Elastic recoil is increased in both phenotypes due to chronic inflammation and fibrosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This fundamental difference in elastic recoil distinguishes the dominant pathophysiology of these two COPD phenotypes. Pink puffers (emphysema-predominant) have low elastic recoil because emphysema destroys alveolar walls and elastic fibers, eliminating the \"spring\" that normally pulls airways open and drives expiration. This loss of elastic recoil causes lung hyperinflation and loss of radial traction, contributing to dynamic airway collapse. Blue bloaters (chronic bronchitis-predominant) have normal elastic recoil because alveolar walls are preserved; their obstruction results from increased airway resistance due to mucous gland hyperplasia, mucous plugging, inflammation, and bronchiolar fibrosis affecting airways, not alveoli.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins Table 15.4 — elastic recoil is low in emphysema and normal in chronic bronchitis; airway resistance is normal or slightly increased in emphysema and increased in bronchitis. FA: emphysema raises compliance and lowers recoil. COMLEX: Recoil loss explains the pink puffer's hyperinflation, while mucus and inflammation explain the blue bloater's airway resistance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — obstructive versus restrictive patterns on the maximum expiratory flow-volume curve","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Pink puffers (emphysema-predom"}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q10_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Etiology, Pathology, Morphology, and Clinical Features of Major Obstructive Pulmonary Diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 54-year-old man with a 40 pack-year smoking history has chronic bronchitis with thick, tenacious sputum. He does not have cystic fibrosis, and genetic testing shows no CFTR mutations. Robbins cites evidence for a smoking-related change in ion transport that makes his secretions worse. Which change is it?","choices":{"A":"Acquired CFTR dysfunction that dehydrates mucus","B":"Inherited loss of dynein arms in airway cilia","C":"Deficiency of α₁-antitrypsin in airway secretions","D":"Th2-driven IgE production against inhaled smoke","E":"Overproduction of surfactant by type II cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Robbins notes substantial evidence that smoking leads to acquired cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) dysfunction, which causes secretion of abnormal, dehydrated mucus that exacerbates chronic bronchitis; this adds to mucus hypersecretion from submucosal gland enlargement and goblet cell increase, chronic inflammation, and smoke-induced impairment of ciliary action. Loss of dynein arms is the inherited defect of primary ciliary dyskinesia, not a smoking effect. α₁-Antitrypsin deficiency is a genetic antiprotease defect that causes panacinar emphysema, not altered mucus hydration. Th2-driven IgE production is the mechanism of atopic asthma. Surfactant from type II cells lowers alveolar surface tension and does not thicken bronchial mucus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — substantial evidence that smoking causes acquired CFTR dysfunction, producing abnormal, dehydrated mucus that worsens chronic bronchitis, on top of mucus hypersecretion and gland hypertrophy. COMLEX: FA — chronic bronchitis shows hypertrophy and hyperplasia of mucus-secreting glands and metaplasia toward squamous epithelium.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — CFTR structure and normal function","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q11_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Etiology, Pathology, Morphology, and Clinical Features of Major Obstructive Pulmonary Diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist examining lung tissue from a patient with COPD notes that the distance from the basement membrane of the bronchial epithelium to the cartilage plate is 3 mm, and the submucosal mucous gland layer measures 1.8 mm in thickness. What is the Reid index for this specimen?","choices":{"A":"0.3, within normal limits for healthy lung tissue","B":"0.6, elevated and consistent with chronic bronchitis","C":"1.8, indicating severe mucous gland hyperplasia","D":"2.0, suggesting end-stage chronic obstructive disease","E":"3.0, representing the absolute thickness measurement"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Reid index is the thickness of the submucosal mucous gland layer divided by the thickness of the wall between the epithelium and the cartilage: 1.8 mm ÷ 3 mm = 0.6. Robbins (Ch 15) gives a normal Reid index of about 0.4, increasing in chronic bronchitis in proportion to severity and duration; First Aid 2025 gives > 50% in chronic bronchitis. A value of 0.6 is therefore elevated by either source and fits chronic bronchitis, which correlates with chronic productive cough. A ratio cannot exceed 1, so 1.8, 2.0 and 3.0 are raw thicknesses or miscalculations, and 0.3 would be normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reid index = gland layer ÷ wall thickness from epithelium to cartilage; Robbins' normal is about 0.4 and FA's cutoff for chronic bronchitis is > 50%, so 0.6 fits bronchitis. COMLEX: Robbins — the bronchitic pattern brings early productive cough, frequent infections, early periodic respiratory insufficiency and common cor pulmonale.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 9 — clinical differentiation of the obstructive lung diseases","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"A ratio cannot exceed 1, so 1.","D":"A ratio cannot exceed 1, so 1.","E":"A ratio cannot exceed 1, so 1."}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q12_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Etiology, Pathology, Morphology, and Clinical Features of Major Obstructive Pulmonary Diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with COPD is evaluated for worsening symptoms. His physician explains that cigarette smoke causes COPD through multiple mechanisms. Which mechanism is the primary pathophysiologic driver of emphysema development in smokers?","choices":{"A":"Smooth muscle hyperplasia leading to irreversible airway narrowing and obstruction","B":"Mucous gland hypertrophy causing excessive secretions and impaired clearance","C":"Squamous metaplasia of bronchial epithelium resulting in dysplastic changes","D":"Recurrent bacterial infections triggering chronic inflammatory airway remodeling","E":"Protease-antiprotease imbalance with toxic injury causing alveolar wall destruction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Emphysema develops through three key mechanisms: (1) toxic injury and inflammation from inhaled smoke damaging respiratory epithelium and releasing inflammatory mediators (leukotriene B4, IL-8, TNF); (2) protease-antiprotease imbalance where inflammatory cells release proteases (elastase) that break down connective tissue while protective antiproteases are relatively deficient or inactivated by oxidants; and (3) oxidative stress from tobacco smoke and inflammatory cells causing tissue damage. These processes lead to irreversible alveolar wall destruction and airspace enlargement that define emphysema. Mucous gland hypertrophy is the hallmark of chronic bronchitis, not emphysema. Robbins describes squamous metaplasia as an epithelial change from smoke irritants in chronic bronchitis, not the cause of alveolar wall destruction. Infections may exacerbate but do not drive emphysema, which results from protease-antiprotease imbalance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins' emphysema mechanisms — toxic injury and inflammation (LTB4, IL-8, TNF), protease–antiprotease imbalance with relative antiprotease deficiency, and oxidative stress from smoke and inflammatory cells. FA: imbalance with ↑ elastase → loss of elastic fibers → alveolar destruction. COMLEX: Smoking cessation is the one intervention that removes all three drivers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — alveolar changes in emphysema versus pneumonia","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Mucous gland hypertrophy is the hallmark of chronic bronchit","C":"Robbins describes squamous metaplasia as an epithelial chang","D":"Infections may exacerbate but do not drive emphysema, which "}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q13_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Etiology, Pathology, and Morphology of Four Main Types of Emphysema","batch":1,"stem":"A 70-year-old retired coal miner with a 50 pack-year smoking history undergoes lung biopsy showing emphysematous changes. Microscopic examination reveals that within individual acini, the respiratory bronchioles show marked dilation and destruction, while the distal alveolar ducts and alveolar sacs appear relatively preserved. In which lung regions would these lesions most likely be concentrated?","choices":{"A":"Lower lobes and lung bases with uniform distribution throughout zones","B":"Adjacent to pleural surfaces and areas of previous scarring or fibrosis","C":"Randomly distributed small foci associated with areas of irregular scarring","D":"Anterior lung margins with symmetric bilateral involvement of all lobes","E":"Upper lobes, especially the apical segments, sparing the lower lobes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This description matches centriacinar (centrilobular) emphysema, the most common form (>95% of cases) occurring in heavy smokers. The key feature is selective involvement of the central/proximal acinus (respiratory bronchioles) with relative sparing of distal structures (alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs). This creates a pattern where emphysematous and normal airspaces coexist within the same acinus. Centriacinar emphysema characteristically affects the upper lobes, especially apical segments, more severely than lower lobes. Lesions along the pleura and next to scars are the pattern of distal acinar (paraseptal) emphysema, and small random foci tied to scarring are irregular emphysema; neither matches a lesion centered on the respiratory bronchioles. Lower lobe, uniform distribution fits panacinar emphysema, not centriacinar emphysema. Panacinar emphysema favors the anterior margins and bases; centriacinar emphysema is worst in the upper lobes, especially the apical segments.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Centriacinar emphysema involves the proximal acinus (respiratory bronchioles) while distal alveoli are spared, so emphysematous and normal spaces share the same acinus; lesions are worse in the upper lobes, especially the apical segments. COMLEX: FA — coal workers' pneumoconiosis also favors the upper lobes, with small rounded nodular opacities and risk of Caplan syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 4 — the major pneumoconioses, including coal workers' pneumoconiosis","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Lower lobe, uniform distribution fits panacinar emphysema, n","B":"Lesions along the pleura and n","D":"Panacinar emphysema favors the anterior margins and bases; c"}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q14_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Etiology, Pathology, and Morphology of Four Main Types of Emphysema","batch":1,"stem":"At autopsy of a 70-year-old man, the pathologist finds small foci of emphysema in which the acini are involved in an irregular pattern, each focus lying immediately beside an old, healed scar. Which statement best describes this type of emphysema?","choices":{"A":"It accounts for more than 95% of significant cases","B":"It is caused by α₁-antitrypsin deficiency","C":"It most often causes pneumothorax in young adults","D":"It follows scarring and is usually insignificant","E":"It is most severe at the bases and anterior margins"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins describes irregular (paracicatricial) emphysema, named because the acinus is irregularly involved, as almost invariably associated with scarring; in most instances it occurs in small foci and is clinically insignificant. Of the four types, only centriacinar and panacinar emphysema cause clinically significant airflow obstruction. More than 95% of clinically significant cases are centriacinar emphysema of heavy smokers. α₁-Antitrypsin deficiency causes panacinar emphysema, which is most severe at the bases and the anterior margins. Distal acinar (paraseptal) emphysema underlies many spontaneous pneumothoraces in young adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Irregular (paracicatricial) emphysema involves the acinus irregularly and is almost invariably associated with scarring; it usually occurs in small foci and is clinically insignificant. Of the four types, only centriacinar and panacinar cause clinically significant airflow obstruction. COMLEX: Robbins — apical blebs or bullae in advanced disease can rupture into the pleura.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 4 — other forms of emphysema with overinflation or focal emphysematous change","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q15_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Etiology, Pathology, and Morphology of Four Main Types of Emphysema","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist examines microscopic sections from the lungs of a 66-year-old woman with advanced emphysema. Besides abnormally large alveoli separated by thin septa with focal centriacinar fibrosis, which microscopic finding does Robbins describe in this disease?","choices":{"A":"Large pores of Kohn, leaving septa floating with clubbed ends","B":"Hyaline membranes lining alveolar ducts and alveolar walls","C":"Hemosiderin-laden macrophages filling the alveolar spaces","D":"Polypoid plugs of loose connective tissue in the bronchioles","E":"Asbestos bodies within dense subpleural interstitial fibrosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Robbins describes the microscopic picture of emphysema as abnormally large alveoli separated by thin septa with focal centriacinar fibrosis, loss of attachments between alveoli and the outer wall of small airways, and pores of Kohn so large that septa appear to be floating or protrude blindly into alveolar spaces with a club-shaped end; as alveolar walls are destroyed, the capillary bed area decreases. Hyaline membranes mark the acute phase of diffuse alveolar damage. Hemosiderin-laden macrophages (heart failure cells) mark chronic passive congestion. Polypoid plugs of loose connective tissue in bronchioles and alveolar ducts characterize organizing pneumonia. Asbestos bodies with interstitial fibrosis indicate asbestosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins' emphysema histology — abnormally large alveoli separated by thin septa with focal centriacinar fibrosis, loss of attachments between alveoli and small-airway walls, and pores of Kohn so large that septa seem to float or end in club shapes. COMLEX: Loss of these alveolar attachments removes radial traction, the structural basis of expiratory airway collapse.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — alveolar wall structure, including the pores of Kohn","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q16_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and Contrast Clinical Features of COPD (Pink Puffers and Blue Bloaters)","batch":1,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with long-standing COPD presents for routine follow-up. She reports severe dyspnea that began in her early 60s, limiting her daily activities. Physical examination shows a barrel-shaped chest with hyperresonance to percussion. She sits leaning forward on her hands and breathes through pursed lips. Cough is minimal with scant sputum. Arterial blood gas shows pH 7.42, PaCO₂ 38 mm Hg, PaO₂ 88 mm Hg on room air. Which pulmonary function test abnormality would most likely be present?","choices":{"A":"Normal FEV₁/FVC ratio with isolated reduction in total lung capacity","B":"Normal elastic recoil with isolated increase in airway wall thickness","C":"Increased airway resistance with elevated total lung capacity from restriction","D":"Reduced residual volume indicating inability to fully exhale trapped air","E":"Markedly reduced diffusing capacity (DLCO) due to alveolar wall destruction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient is a classic \"pink puffer\" with emphysema-predominant COPD. The key features include severe early dyspnea, barrel chest (hyperinflation), pursed-lip breathing, forward-leaning posture, minimal cough, and relatively preserved blood gases at rest. Pink puffers characteristically have markedly reduced diffusing capacity because emphysema destroys alveolar walls and pulmonary capillaries, reducing the surface area available for gas exchange. Despite this impairment, they maintain relatively normal blood gases through increased ventilatory effort (hence \"pink\" from adequate oxygenation and \"puffer\" from labored breathing). Normal FEV1/FVC with reduced TLC describes restriction, not emphysema. Emphysema has lost elastic recoil, not normal recoil. Restriction lowers, not raises, TLC; the high TLC of emphysema comes from air trapping. Residual volume is increased, not reduced, in emphysema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Emphysema destroys alveolar walls and capillary membrane, so DLCO falls (FA); Robbins' pink puffer has low diffusion capacity yet relatively normal resting blood gases. COMLEX: The pulmonary OMM lab notes hyperinflation in COPD shortens respiratory muscles and raises resting tone, and accessory muscle use (scalenes) signals primary muscle strain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 11 — the CO method for measuring diffusing capacity","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Normal FEV1/FVC with reduced TLC describes restriction, not ","B":"Emphysema has lost elastic recoil, not normal recoil.","C":"Restriction lowers, not raises, TLC; the high TLC of emphyse","D":"Residual volume is increased, not reduced, in emphysema."}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q17_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and Contrast Clinical Features of COPD (Pink Puffers and Blue Bloaters)","batch":1,"stem":"A 46-year-old man presents with a 4-year history of productive cough with thick sputum, worse in the mornings. He has had three episodes of acute bronchitis requiring antibiotics in the past year. Physical examination reveals central cyanosis and scattered wheezes. Spirometry shows FEV₁/FVC 0.58 with minimal bronchodilator response. Arterial blood gas shows pH 7.32, PaCO₂ 62 mm Hg, PaO₂ 56 mm Hg. What is the most likely age of symptom onset and expected timing of respiratory insufficiency in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Symptom onset age 50-75 years with end-stage respiratory insufficiency only","B":"Symptom onset age 30-40 years with respiratory insufficiency never developing","C":"Symptom onset age 40-45 years with early periodic respiratory insufficiency","D":"Symptom onset age 60-70 years with respiratory insufficiency unpredictable","E":"Symptom onset variable, with respiratory insufficiency based solely on smoking burden"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient is a \"blue bloater\" with chronic bronchitis-predominant COPD. The hallmarks include chronic productive cough (the cardinal symptom, often present for years before dyspnea), frequent respiratory infections, cyanosis, hypercapnia (PaCO₂ 62 mm Hg), and hypoxemia (PaO₂ 56 mm Hg). Blue bloaters typically develop symptoms in their 40s (younger than pink puffers who present at 50-75 years) and experience early, periodic episodes of respiratory insufficiency triggered by infections or other stressors. The hypercapnia and hypoxemia occur relatively early because mucous plugging and V/Q mismatch impair gas exchange before severe ventilatory capacity limitation develops. Robbins gives respiratory insufficiency in chronic bronchitis as early and periodic, with typical onset at 40 to 45, so it does develop. Robbins gives typical onset of chronic bronchitis as 40 to 45 years, earlier than 60 to 70. The course is not defined solely by smoking burden; onset is about 40 to 45 with early periodic insufficiency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins Table 15.4 — bronchitis-dominant disease: age 40–45, mild late dyspnea, early cough with copious sputum, common infections, early periodic respiratory insufficiency, common cor pulmonale; emphysema: age 50–75, severe early dyspnea, end-stage insufficiency. COMLEX: Robbins — death in COPD often comes from pulmonary hypertension and heart failure or acute respiratory failure with exacerbation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 14 — when hypoxia and hypercapnia coexist","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Blue bloaters typically develo","B":"Robbins gives respiratory insufficiency in chronic bronchiti","D":"Robbins gives typical onset of chronic bronchitis as 40 to 4","E":"The course is not defined solely by smoking burden; onset is"}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q18_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and Contrast Clinical Features of COPD (Pink Puffers and Blue Bloaters)","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student reviews chest radiographs from two patients with severe COPD, one with emphysema-predominant disease and one with chronic bronchitis-predominant disease. Which radiographic finding would be expected in the emphysema patient (pink puffer) but not in the chronic bronchitis patient (blue bloater)?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral pleural effusions indicating right heart failure and volume overload","B":"Prominent pulmonary vessels and cardiomegaly suggesting pulmonary hypertension","C":"Ground-glass opacities and interstitial markings suggesting active inflammation","D":"Flattened diaphragms and a larger retrosternal airspace from hyperinflation","E":"Kerley B lines and cephalization indicating pulmonary venous congestion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pink puffers (emphysema-predominant) show characteristic chest X-ray findings of hyperinflation: flattened diaphragms, increased anteroposterior diameter (barrel chest), increased retrosternal airspace on lateral view, and attenuated pulmonary vascular markings. The heart size appears normal or even small. These findings reflect air trapping and overdistention from loss of elastic recoil. In contrast, blue bloaters show prominent pulmonary vessels, increased bronchovascular markings from chronic inflammation, and cardiomegaly (right heart enlargement from cor pulmonale).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins Table 15.4 — the emphysema chest radiograph shows hyperinflation with a normal heart size; chronic bronchitis shows prominent vessels and a large heart. FA: emphysema CXR — barrel chest, ↑ AP diameter (best on lateral), flattened diaphragm, ↑ lucency. COMLEX: A flattened diaphragm has poor excursion; the pulmonary OMM lab targets diaphragm release and rib motion in COPD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — the diaphragm and the mechanics of inspiration and expiration","isHighYield":false}]};
